<!-- cell: note id="b0332032-48d9-5051-b945-cb926198d5c3" -->

## Concept arcs of How the war begun

1.1 The space agency
  Communication and understanding: —
1.2 Excavation team is sent, robots are lowered down and begin excavating
  Communication and understanding: —
1.3 Excavation uncovers the object made out of rare materials, resembling a stone sculpture.
  Communication and understanding: The humans read the object in their own terms, as a stone sculpture and an artifact, with no sign to tell them it is a living being.
1.4 Samples are sent to orbit, to a laboratory ship. Scientists begin studying the object.
  Communication and understanding: The scientists try to understand the object by analysing its matter, as one studies a mineral specimen, and so look for no sign that it might read them back.
2.1 The object is an organism embedded in the rock. It was monitoring the probe's movement and its scan, and the arrival of the excavation team.
  Communication and understanding: The organism has watched every human action, the probe, the scan and the team's arrival, but no sign passes between them, so it can read these acts only in its own terms.
2.2 The excavation begins, and the organism grows concern. But it's not until the excavators pierce its exoskeleton, hurting it, that is sends a distress call.
  Communication and understanding: Unable to tell scientific digging from aggression, the organism reads the excavation as a growing threat, and when the drills pierce its exoskeleton it takes the wound as an attack.
2.3 The communication is FTL, he is asked what is the nautre of emergency. The organism says he was discovered and attacked. He is told to stay put and that help is on the way.
  Communication and understanding: The organism and its own kind share signs and understand each other at once, so its misreading of the humans passes intact to the others as fact: "discovered and attacked."
3.1 Alien ships emerge on a far orbit around Mars. Earth ships spot them immediately.
  Communication and understanding: The Earth ships see the alien fleet at once, but seeing gives them no sign of why it has come.
3.2 Earth sends orders to protect the discovery. The mission consists of the lab ship and one weakly armed escort.
  Communication and understanding: With no way to learn the fleet's intent, Earth reads its arrival in its own terms, as a threat to its find, and orders the discovery protected.
3.3 Ships are approaching, they are completely foreign and no one on the earth ships knows how to even interpret if they are powering up weapons or not. hails are sent, but to no effect.
  Communication and understanding: The two sides share no signs at all: the crews cannot tell whether the alien ships are powering weapons, and their hails go unread.
3.4 Scientists on the lab ship are scrambling in attempt to understand anything about the alien ships. They suspect they are here for the artifact, and they are trying to correlate what they found with the sensor readings. The mineral scans agree, the origin seems the same, but that doesn't say anything about communication.
  Communication and understanding: The scientists link the fleet to the artifact through matching mineral scans, but they see that a shared origin gives them no shared language to speak with.
3.5 All the while the excavation efforts are not stoping, the scientists wanting to find out more about the fleet that approaches by studying the object buried on Mars.
  Communication and understanding: The humans try to understand the fleet by digging deeper into the object, and in doing so they keep up the very act the organism and the fleet read as an attack.
4.1 The object communicates with the fleet that he is in need of dire assistance.
  Communication and understanding: The organism tells the fleet, in signs they both read, that it needs dire help, and this hardens its own misreading into the fleet's picture of what is happening.
4.2 The fleet observes the efforts and identifies the ships in orbit, classifying them as belionging to the same tribe as the entities attacking the object.
  Communication and understanding: The fleet reads the human ships in its own terms and classes them with the attackers, since nothing tells it these are scientists who meant no harm.
4.3 They fire on the ships in orbit, decimating them.
  Communication and understanding: The error grows into harm: acting on its misreading, the fleet destroys the ships that had tried to hail it.
4.4 They automated excavators stop.
  Communication and understanding: The digging that the organism read as an attack ends, not because either side has understood the other, but because no one is left to command the machines.
4.5 Earth notices the disapeparance of the ships, and assumes they have just been attacked in an act of war.
  Communication and understanding: Earth now reads the fleet's act in its own terms, as an unprovoked act of war, never knowing it answered what the organism took as an attack, and the mutual misreading turns into war.

<!-- cell: markdown id="1395a7f0-9154-4766-8333-681a0e2fe965" -->

# Captain Varn and her daughter

## High-level plot

1. Captain Varn leaves her daughter on Earth to command the corvette that escorts the mission to Mars.

2. At Mars, Varn and her daughter keep talking, and Varn tells her of her doubts about the excavation.

3. When the alien fleet arrives, Varn fears it will go on to Earth.

4. Varn believes her daughter will have no chance unless someone stands in the fleet's way. She says goodbye to her daughter, puts her corvette in the fleet's path, and dies.

## Low-level plot

Captain Varn watches the news with her daughter: a probe has found something under the surface of Mars.

Varn is given command of the corvette that will escort the mission to Mars, and tells her daughter she has to leave.

Varn says goodbye to her daughter and promises to talk to her every day.

Varn's corvette arrives at Mars with the laboratory ship, as the first samples come up from the excavation.

Varn calls her daughter. Her daughter says she misses her, and Varn says she misses her too.

Varn watches the excavators dig deeper, and tells her daughter that no one knows what the object is, yet the digging goes on.

Varn's daughter asks whether the object is dangerous, and Varn tells her not to worry.

Varn sees alien ships appear on her corvette's screens, far out around Mars.

Varn is ordered to protect the discovery, and knows her one weakly armed corvette cannot stop a fleet.

Varn hails the alien ships again and again, and gets no answer.

Varn asks the scientists on the laboratory ship what the aliens want, and they cannot tell her.

Varn tells her daughter about the ships, and that she is afraid they will go on to Earth.

Varn believes that unless someone stands in the fleet's way, her daughter will have no chance on Earth. She sends her daughter a last message to say goodbye.

Varn moves her corvette into the fleet's path to buy her daughter time.

The fleet fires, and Varn's corvette is destroyed.

Varn's daughter receives her mother's last message.

<!-- cell: note id="dca7728d-3313-43e2-9dc1-2878b99cc3c0" -->

## Captain Varn and her daughter

How Captain Varn gave her life to buy her daughter time.

1. Captain Varn leaves her daughter on Earth to command the corvette that escorts the mission to Mars.
2. At Mars, Varn and her daughter keep talking, and Varn tells her of her doubts about the excavation.
3. When the alien fleet arrives, Varn fears it will go on to Earth.
4. Varn believes her daughter will have no chance unless someone stands in the fleet's way. She says goodbye to her daughter, puts her corvette in the fleet's path, and dies.

