<!-- cell: note id="0cfa6b29-624b-5add-9390-7cd869c942f5" -->

## Concept arcs of Captain Varn and her daughter

1.1 Captain Varn watches the news with her daughter: a probe has found something under the surface of Mars.
  Motherhood: Varn's care shows only as everyday closeness: she sits beside her daughter while they watch the news together.
1.2 Varn is given command of the corvette that will escort the mission to Mars, and tells her daughter she has to leave.
  Motherhood: Varn tells her daughter herself that she has to leave, preparing her for the time they will spend apart.
1.3 Varn says goodbye to her daughter and promises to talk to her every day.
  Motherhood: Varn's promise to talk every day is how she means to keep caring for her daughter across the distance, so the girl is not left alone.
1.4 Varn's corvette arrives at Mars with the laboratory ship, as the first samples come up from the excavation.
  Motherhood: —
2.1 Varn calls her daughter. Her daughter says she misses her, and Varn says she misses her too.
  Motherhood: Varn keeps her promise and calls, and she answers her daughter's longing with her own, so the girl knows her mother's love has not gone away.
2.2 Varn watches the excavators dig deeper, and tells her daughter that no one knows what the object is, yet the digging goes on.
  Motherhood: Varn keeps her daughter part of her days, telling her plainly what she sees at Mars.
2.3 Varn's daughter asks whether the object is dangerous, and Varn tells her not to worry.
  Motherhood: Varn shields her daughter from fear and tells her not to worry, protecting her from a threat that is still only a question.
3.1 Varn sees alien ships appear on her corvette's screens, far out around Mars.
  Motherhood: —
3.2 Varn is ordered to protect the discovery, and knows her one weakly armed corvette cannot stop a fleet.
  Motherhood: —
3.3 Varn hails the alien ships again and again, and gets no answer.
  Motherhood: —
3.4 Varn asks the scientists on the laboratory ship what the aliens want, and they cannot tell her.
  Motherhood: —
3.5 Varn tells her daughter about the ships, and that she is afraid they will go on to Earth.
  Motherhood: Varn no longer tells her daughter not to worry. She warns her that the ships may reach Earth, preparing her for a danger her mother may not be there to keep from her.
4.1 Varn believes that unless someone stands in the fleet's way, her daughter will have no chance on Earth. She sends her daughter a last message to say goodbye.
  Motherhood: Varn decides to put her daughter's chance of living above her own life, and her last act of care is a goodbye message for the girl she will leave behind.
4.2 Varn moves her corvette into the fleet's path to buy her daughter time.
  Motherhood: Varn places her corvette, and herself, between the fleet and her daughter. Here her protection reaches its limit: her life given for her child's.
4.3 The fleet fires, and Varn's corvette is destroyed.
  Motherhood: Varn pays the full price of her choice: she dies so that her daughter may have time.
4.5 Varn's daughter receives her mother's last message.
  Motherhood: Varn's love reaches her daughter after her death: her last message is the care she leaves behind.

<!-- cell: note id="9797dc34-5962-43ff-aab0-4a23cfea7922" -->

## The politician's deal

How a corrupt politician used Captain Varn to win Mars for his backers, fell for her, and lost everything.

1. The politician sets out to win a mafia-tied mining company exclusive rights to Mars, and befriends Captain Varn, who commands the mission's escort, for what she can tell him and the weight she gives him.
2. He passes on what Varn tells him, and uses it to push the committee into faster and deeper digging.
3. When the alien fleet arrives, the company makes him keep the digging going, and his love for Varn turns against his deal.
4. The excavation is lost to the war, and the evidence from the custody case exposes him: he loses the deal, his seat and Varn.

<!-- cell: plot id="3e1100db-02f4-434a-af42-9e9c3cf08843" title="The politician's deal" edges="90af46c5-8935-484b-9e2d-abb68c62c8ab:589f3663-64dc-4aec-ad1e-493a8927f191>8422be60-7d37-42d3-a1f1-6f0f82555ba4, 7b5149a2-d430-406d-87a6-a47ca5ccd7dc:8422be60-7d37-42d3-a1f1-6f0f82555ba4>ca180136-4258-4d25-a292-3af0d6137af2, bab104ec-a35e-47a0-94e4-fa1b450bf3c6:ca180136-4258-4d25-a292-3af0d6137af2>6e2e062f-d5fe-46ef-a477-b3a02db36617, 012949a1-cc62-48b6-befe-e4e1c4aa2c5f:6e2e062f-d5fe-46ef-a477-b3a02db36617>faf4f244-09a4-4ba8-8562-ba1fff92e507, 477ea5f0-3a6b-46da-a336-c15a53fe2e82:faf4f244-09a4-4ba8-8562-ba1fff92e507>67d53dfb-4625-4a74-9585-00b066fe60ce, 8f48dd3e-34f8-4e51-a872-627347c11a3e:67d53dfb-4625-4a74-9585-00b066fe60ce>198e68d9-b03d-4513-bb1e-240d50fe333b, fd70ff4f-22b1-4d6f-90e6-104971076b73:198e68d9-b03d-4513-bb1e-240d50fe333b>9e4e9f1f-7654-40d9-a654-458acdb305c8, fc67428f-78ef-437b-acb5-3b162b0ed2b2:9e4e9f1f-7654-40d9-a654-458acdb305c8>2fa3fe8a-cab8-4827-bb25-43ab957c1af0, 2c20b181-7179-4df5-b3b5-0bd1ebdf21ea:2fa3fe8a-cab8-4827-bb25-43ab957c1af0>6ea45030-a6f2-4529-9465-0ae2a297ddaf, 08354c6b-b2c5-48b8-a882-c709e76511c4:6ea45030-a6f2-4529-9465-0ae2a297ddaf>fad9aa0b-ff15-4826-9e82-1bfde9b2dcc5, c94d3dda-36ed-4d1e-bc58-423e87893859:fad9aa0b-ff15-4826-9e82-1bfde9b2dcc5>20042bf3-b404-4347-a71d-3753222c0e04, 5b9efebb-935a-4d69-9f0d-1779894a06b6:20042bf3-b404-4347-a71d-3753222c0e04>a5a49d44-dad4-4333-8212-212220328d0c, 2be0883d-fcc7-4be9-8a73-526869905e9b:a5a49d44-dad4-4333-8212-212220328d0c>4411bd09-df3e-4c5a-a5e3-3dd672641722, afe0500e-e187-43ea-a53a-edbc76998d46:4411bd09-df3e-4c5a-a5e3-3dd672641722>1f1c7e43-2b23-4f69-a91b-80a28775b123, a7a17ba3-6402-4138-9f24-61c23e789fc0:1f1c7e43-2b23-4f69-a91b-80a28775b123>5d242200-e8fe-439b-8513-b62f37a3ecda" -->

<ol title="A politician in the pay of a mafia-tied mining company befriends Captain Varn to learn what the government knows about Mars, keeps the digging going for his backers even when the alien fleet arrives, and is exposed and ruined as the war begins.\nIllustrates: Corruption">
<li id="589f3663-64dc-4aec-ad1e-493a8927f191" timestamp="0.2" location="earth, a restaurant" words="2018-3657">The politician, who sits on the committee that oversees the space agency, uses his meetings with Captain Varn to learn what the government is doing on Mars, and is not telling the committee.</li>
<li id="8422be60-7d37-42d3-a1f1-6f0f82555ba4" timestamp="1.1" location="earth, the company's offices" words="5986-6522">The owners of a mining company with ties to the mafia, who paid for the politician's career, tell him to win them exclusive rights to mine Mars, now that something has been found there.</li>
<li id="ca180136-4258-4d25-a292-3af0d6137af2" timestamp="1.3" location="earth, parliament" words="8534-9111">He puts the company's bid for an exclusive licence to the committee, but the other members will not commit until they know what was found.</li>
<li id="6e2e062f-d5fe-46ef-a477-b3a02db36617" timestamp="1.4" location="earth, the company's offices" words="9887-11236">He tells the owners that Varn will be his eyes on Mars, and that through her he will know more than anyone on the committee.</li>
<li id="faf4f244-09a4-4ba8-8562-ba1fff92e507" timestamp="2.1" location="orbit" words="11720-13433">On their calls, Varn tells him that the object is unlike any rock, and that the agency is keeping what it finds quiet. He passes it on to the company.</li>
<li id="67d53dfb-4625-4a74-9585-00b066fe60ce" timestamp="2.2.1" location="earth, parliament" words="17291-17866">With what Varn told him, he persuades the committee to order faster and deeper digging, with machines the company supplies.</li>
<li id="198e68d9-b03d-4513-bb1e-240d50fe333b" timestamp="2.3.1" location="earth, parliament" words="21441-21957">Members who cannot get the agency's findings come to him for them, and the company's licence gains a majority.</li>
<li id="9e4e9f1f-7654-40d9-a654-458acdb305c8" timestamp="3.1.1" location="earth, the company's offices" words="24866-26080">The owners order him to push Varn to investigate the object more closely, so that whatever is in the ground is theirs before anyone can stop it.</li>
<li id="2fa3fe8a-cab8-4827-bb25-43ab957c1af0" timestamp="3.2" location="earth, parliament" words="26810-27383">Against the members who want the excavation halted, he talks the committee into ordering the mission to protect the discovery and dig on, which keeps Varn's corvette in the fleet's path.</li>
<li id="6ea45030-a6f2-4529-9465-0ae2a297ddaf" timestamp="3.3" location="orbit" words="28802-29897">Varn tells him that her corvette cannot stop a fleet. He understands that the order he fought for may cost her her life.</li>
<li id="fad9aa0b-ff15-4826-9e82-1bfde9b2dcc5" timestamp="3.4" location="earth, the company's offices" words="31422-31963">His concerns show the owners that he has grown attached to Varn, and they begin to doubt him.</li>
<li id="20042bf3-b404-4347-a71d-3753222c0e04" timestamp="3.5" location="earth, parliament" words="34060-34628">Word reaches the committee that his name, and his ties to the company, have come up in a court hearing over the custody of Varn's daughter.</li>
<li id="a5a49d44-dad4-4333-8212-212220328d0c" timestamp="4.1.1" location="earth, parliament" words="38140-38621">The committee suspends him while his ties to the company are investigated.</li>
<li id="4411bd09-df3e-4c5a-a5e3-3dd672641722" timestamp="4.3" location="earth, the company's offices" words="41186-41749">The owners fire him: he has grown too attached to Varn, and become a liability.</li>
<li id="1f1c7e43-2b23-4f69-a91b-80a28775b123" timestamp="4.4" location="earth, the company's offices" words="42244-42855">The company loses contact with its excavators on Mars, and with them the licence it was promised.</li>
<li id="5d242200-e8fe-439b-8513-b62f37a3ecda" timestamp="4.5" location="earth, the court" words="43884-45099">He learns that Earth's ships at Mars, Varn's corvette among them, are gone, and that Earth calls it an act of war.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: note id="8fe6a4ab-71fe-5a78-92a2-ae0b34f1d94e" -->

## Concept arcs of The politician's deal

0.2 The politician, who sits on the committee that oversees the space agency, uses his meetings with Captain Varn to learn what the government is doing on Mars, and is not telling the committee.
  Corruption: The politician uses his seat on the oversight committee to gather knowledge of the Mars mission for himself, keeping from the committee what his office gives him.
1.1 The owners of a mining company with ties to the mafia, who paid for the politician's career, tell him to win them exclusive rights to mine Mars, now that something has been found there.
  Corruption: The mob-linked owners who paid for his career call in their purchase, telling him to turn his influence over the committee into an exclusive licence for them.
1.3 He puts the company's bid for an exclusive licence to the committee, but the other members will not commit until they know what was found.
  Corruption: The politician brings his paymasters' bid before the committee as though it were public business, serving the company from inside the body meant to oversee it.
1.4 He tells the owners that Varn will be his eyes on Mars, and that through her he will know more than anyone on the committee.
  Corruption: The politician offers the owners his private channel to Varn as part of what they have bought, promising to outdo the committee with access his office gave him.
2.1 On their calls, Varn tells him that the object is unlike any rock, and that the agency is keeping what it finds quiet. He passes it on to the company.
  Corruption: The politician hands what Varn confides about the object and the agency's secrecy straight to the company, turning official confidences into his payers' advantage.
2.2.1 With what Varn told him, he persuades the committee to order faster and deeper digging, with machines the company supplies.
  Corruption: The politician uses Varn's information to steer the committee into an order that speeds the dig and puts the company's machines on Mars, bending a public decision to private gain.
2.3.1 Members who cannot get the agency's findings come to him for them, and the company's licence gains a majority.
  Corruption: The politician trades the findings he alone holds for his colleagues' support, and the company's licence gains a majority built on his hoarded access.
3.1.1 The owners order him to push Varn to investigate the object more closely, so that whatever is in the ground is theirs before anyone can stop it.
  Corruption: The owners give the politician orders like an employee, telling him to press Varn so that whatever the mission finds becomes their property before anyone can intervene.
3.2 Against the members who want the excavation halted, he talks the committee into ordering the mission to protect the discovery and dig on, which keeps Varn's corvette in the fleet's path.
  Corruption: The politician overrides the members calling for caution and wins an order that serves the company's claim, keeping Varn's crew in danger for his payers' sake.
3.3 Varn tells him that her corvette cannot stop a fleet. He understands that the order he fought for may cost her her life.
  Corruption: The politician sees the price of the order he won for the company, which may now be paid with Varn's life.
3.4 His concerns show the owners that he has grown attached to Varn, and they begin to doubt him.
  Corruption: The owners grow suspicious the moment the politician cares about anything but their interest, showing that what they bought was his undivided loyalty.
3.5 Word reaches the committee that his name, and his ties to the company, have come up in a court hearing over the custody of Varn's daughter.
  Corruption: The politician's hidden ties to the company surface in court and reach the committee, and the deal he kept secret begins to come out.
4.1.1 The committee suspends him while his ties to the company are investigated.
  Corruption: The committee suspends the politician and investigates whether he sold his office to the company, taking back the public power he misused.
4.3 The owners fire him: he has grown too attached to Varn, and become a liability.
  Corruption: The owners discard the politician once his office is lost and his loyalty is in doubt, showing that he was only ever a purchased tool.
4.4 The company loses contact with its excavators on Mars, and with them the licence it was promised.
  Corruption: The company loses both its machines on Mars and the licence the politician was paid to win, so the corrupt bargain comes to nothing.
4.5 He learns that Earth's ships at Mars, Varn's corvette among them, are gone, and that Earth calls it an act of war.
  Corruption: The politician learns the public cost of the dig he forced through for the company: Earth's ships, Varn's among them, are lost, and Earth now faces war.

<!-- cell: note id="397a460f-3636-47fb-9687-f747fed675bc" -->

## The politician and Captain Varn

How a politician pretended to like Captain Varn, and came to love her.

1. The politician meets Captain Varn and pretends to like her, to get into her good graces. She tells him about her daughter, and that she is not looking for romance; he keeps playing coy.
2. When Varn is sent to Mars, he arranges remote dates. They start talking about the future, and she asks him to look in on her daughter.
3. A day with her daughter reminds him of his own childhood, and he is strangely moved. When his overseers want Varn pushed closer to the object, he voices concerns.
4. As the alien fleet closes in, in a last desperate call, he realizes he loves her, and promises to look after her daughter.

<!-- cell: plot id="54d2a399-e38c-4f8e-8ba7-22e965ec879a" title="The politician and Captain Varn" edges="1d6ffa76-53b4-4551-bbbd-d4eef96091fe:b27fcca3-cf80-4241-b658-9a505feb075e>48e7772f-91a8-4a29-aeed-e43711d3e858, ea4fcbbf-0257-43f0-9c3f-2d2767eebfd7:48e7772f-91a8-4a29-aeed-e43711d3e858>374c0da6-5fff-4063-bbf5-630fdbbacdb9, 3edfc3a6-7c5e-4032-99da-b82b12abfed6:374c0da6-5fff-4063-bbf5-630fdbbacdb9>d95e8bae-814f-440c-8313-2c1fe2eec0d7, f25de376-eaec-469f-a60b-61e859174605:d95e8bae-814f-440c-8313-2c1fe2eec0d7>2a276b38-e6d9-4db1-aef5-15c942b2ae6f, 75031a43-cfbb-4833-ab12-1301068ca59c:2a276b38-e6d9-4db1-aef5-15c942b2ae6f>604ce2a6-1ab6-4bdc-ba72-7b7526d6562e, 9cc74a17-ad4d-4ab9-859e-485620632ebc:604ce2a6-1ab6-4bdc-ba72-7b7526d6562e>1d2cbbcf-2c53-48ff-a775-9fa5493261dd, 495e4e72-9a07-4e10-8a94-24aef9132427:1d2cbbcf-2c53-48ff-a775-9fa5493261dd>b84d945c-ea9e-4266-874c-62fea7bec038, 25d9cba0-e7c2-4d5e-80c0-bbc58719ee79:b84d945c-ea9e-4266-874c-62fea7bec038>5413d17f-ab61-45b0-8bf5-1a628fd98fc3, c2bbc7c4-a301-4caa-bcd8-0f0785a8abd3:5413d17f-ab61-45b0-8bf5-1a628fd98fc3>c3e8b8f1-dad3-460d-b9e8-a529481ff041" -->

<ol title="A politician courts Captain Varn for what she can tell him, keeps up remote dates when she is sent to Mars, is moved by a day with her daughter, and, as the alien fleet closes in, realizes he loves her and promises to look after the girl.\nIllustrates: The need for partnership and closeness, Good beats evil, Classical romance">
<li id="b27fcca3-cf80-4241-b658-9a505feb075e" timestamp="0.1" location="earth, command center" words="775-1563">The politician meets Captain Varn, and starts pretending to like her, to get into her good graces.</li>
<li id="48e7772f-91a8-4a29-aeed-e43711d3e858" timestamp="0.2" location="earth, a restaurant" words="2018-3657">He invites her to dinner. She tells him about her daughter, and that she is not looking for romance.</li>
<li id="374c0da6-5fff-4063-bbf5-630fdbbacdb9" timestamp="0.3" location="earth, a restaurant" words="3658-5102">They keep meeting, and he keeps playing coy.</li>
<li id="d95e8bae-814f-440c-8313-2c1fe2eec0d7" timestamp="1.4" location="earth, the company's offices" words="9887-11236">He learns that Varn is being sent with the mission to Mars: a relief, and at first an opportunity to learn more about the mysterious object.</li>
<li id="2a276b38-e6d9-4db1-aef5-15c942b2ae6f" timestamp="2.1" location="orbit" words="11720-13433">With Varn in space, he arranges remote dates: he calls her, and they start talking about the future.</li>
<li id="604ce2a6-1ab6-4bdc-ba72-7b7526d6562e" timestamp="2.2" location="orbit" words="14575-15951">On one of their calls, Varn asks him to check on her daughter, and he agrees.</li>
<li id="1d2cbbcf-2c53-48ff-a775-9fa5493261dd" timestamp="2.3" location="earth, Varn's house" words="18473-20852">He spends a day with her daughter, taking care of her. Among the photos of Varn and the stories the girl tells about her, something reminds him of his own childhood.</li>
<li id="b84d945c-ea9e-4266-874c-62fea7bec038" timestamp="3.1" location="orbit" words="21958-23486">The next time he calls Varn, he is strangely moved.</li>
<li id="5413d17f-ab61-45b0-8bf5-1a628fd98fc3" timestamp="3.1.1" location="earth, the company's offices" words="24866-26080">When his overseers order him to push Varn to investigate the object more closely, he voices concerns.</li>
<li id="c3e8b8f1-dad3-460d-b9e8-a529481ff041" timestamp="4.1" location="orbit" words="36151-37660">As the alien fleet closes in, in a last desperate call with Varn, he realizes that he loves her, and promises to look after her daughter.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: note id="59358625-b348-599f-bd87-ea640e6f93ed" -->

## Concept arcs of The politician and Captain Varn

0.1 The politician meets Captain Varn, and starts pretending to like her, to get into her good graces.
  The need for partnership and closeness: —
  Good beats evil: The politician starts on the side of scheming: his warmth toward Varn is a calculated act to win her favour for his own ends.
  Classical romance: The politician and Varn meet, the first step of a classical love story, but his feeling is false from the start, and this deceit is what stands between them.
0.2 He invites her to dinner. She tells him about her daughter, and that she is not looking for romance.
  The need for partnership and closeness: Varn shows that her closeness is already given to her daughter, and she says she wants no other bond.
  Good beats evil: The dinner is another calculated move by the politician, set against Varn's plain honesty about her daughter and what she wants.
  Classical romance: Varn's refusal of romance puts a second obstacle between them, beside his deceit.
0.3 They keep meeting, and he keeps playing coy.
  The need for partnership and closeness: The meetings give Varn and the politician the steady contact a close bond is built on, though on his side it is still hollow.
  Good beats evil: The politician's coyness is part of his scheme, a deliberate way to draw Varn closer while he gives nothing real.
  Classical romance: The courtship goes on through its classical motions, with each meeting drawing Varn in, but on a false footing.
1.4 He learns that Varn is being sent with the mission to Mars: a relief, and at first an opportunity to learn more about the mysterious object.
  The need for partnership and closeness: —
  Good beats evil: The politician sees Varn's mission only as a chance to learn about the object for his own side, and here his scheming seems to be winning.
  Classical romance: Separation comes to the pair, the classical trial of a growing love, but the politician welcomes it with relief.
2.1 With Varn in space, he arranges remote dates: he calls her, and they start talking about the future.
  The need for partnership and closeness: Through the calls, the politician keeps up warm, constant contact with Varn across the distance, and their talk of the future first suggests a bond that could last.
  Good beats evil: The politician arranges the remote dates in part to keep his access to the mission, so his scheming continues.
  Classical romance: Varn and the politician grow closer despite the separation, and their talk of the future draws them toward each other.
2.2 On one of their calls, Varn asks him to check on her daughter, and he agrees.
  The need for partnership and closeness: Varn trusts the politician with her daughter, a sign that to her the bond has become mutual and dependable.
  Good beats evil: Varn's request is an act of care and trust, and when the politician agrees, the good side gains its first hold on him.
  Classical romance: Varn's trust in the politician deepens their closeness across the separation and lowers the wall she had put up.
2.3 He spends a day with her daughter, taking care of her. Among the photos of Varn and the stories the girl tells about her, something reminds him of his own childhood.
  The need for partnership and closeness: Caring for the girl and seeing her bond with her mother stirs the politician's memory of his own childhood, and with it a need for closeness he had not admitted.
  Good beats evil: The care the politician gives Varn's daughter, even if he began it as part of his scheme, starts to change him.
  Classical romance: Through the girl's photos and stories, the politician comes to see Varn as she really is, and he is drawn to her for the first time.
3.1 The next time he calls Varn, he is strangely moved.
  The need for partnership and closeness: The politician feels the warmth of contact with Varn as something he truly needs, not something he performs.
  Good beats evil: The politician's feigned feeling turns real, the first sign that he is coming over from scheming to care.
  Classical romance: On this call the politician's attraction to Varn is genuine, and his deceit begins to fall away.
3.1.1 When his overseers order him to push Varn to investigate the object more closely, he voices concerns.
  The need for partnership and closeness: The politician now cares for Varn's safety, so the bond has become mutual on his side as well.
  Good beats evil: The politician pushes back against his overseers, as his loyalty to Varn begins to outweigh his scheming for them.
  Classical romance: The politician's masters and his role in their scheme now stand between him and Varn, and he begins to choose her over them.
4.1 As the alien fleet closes in, in a last desperate call with Varn, he realizes that he loves her, and promises to look after her daughter.
  The need for partnership and closeness: In their last call, the politician's bond with Varn is fully mutual, and his promise to raise her daughter makes it a care that will last beyond her.
  Good beats evil: As the alien fleet closes in and everything seems lost, the politician comes over completely to the good, choosing love and loyalty to Varn and her daughter.
  Classical romance: Across the final separation, the politician realizes that he loves Varn, and their story reaches the culmination of a classical romance.

<!-- cell: note id="46a0a8ec-de09-415a-bf2b-498fb9518907" -->

## The detective hired against Captain Varn

How a detective hired to find dirt on Captain Varn came to take her side, and helped her daughter to the man she trusted.

1. Captain Varn's ex-husband hires a detective to find dirt on her. He finds nothing on her, but sees a politician courting her, and follows him to a company with ties to the mafia.
2. His client's glee at anything that will hurt Varn troubles him, and he starts looking into the client instead. Watching the politician look after Varn's daughter, he sees a bond forming, and asks Varn to meet him.
3. He finds that Varn is the better parent, and that the father only wants to hurt her. As his own photographs are used against her in court, he cancels the contract and sides with Varn.
4. When Varn's ship is destroyed, he helps the politician win custody of her daughter.

<!-- cell: plot id="d747aff2-bac6-4448-a95e-2b00d83c5f0d" title="The detective hired against Captain Varn" edges="831ce51a-19e7-4614-8a50-c4de9de9fb67:ecfd224a-7ab6-4397-8f49-6fe043e233c1>213dd038-fb32-48ad-90b4-63f3f78e35ac, cb54ba22-e80c-4aef-a96c-ff1294cb90ba:213dd038-fb32-48ad-90b4-63f3f78e35ac>508750bb-0570-4732-a097-ec2d251e25f5, b111f900-09ca-4f24-b8ed-6d338ee254bd:508750bb-0570-4732-a097-ec2d251e25f5>41317c82-ab7b-4df6-8c17-e33939448a3e, 103a36ba-110e-4a00-b6c7-88104477181c:41317c82-ab7b-4df6-8c17-e33939448a3e>b615a6e6-19d5-453f-a3d7-f2e42276d7c3, 155f4eae-86a0-433b-bff2-f7b0bc21869d:b615a6e6-19d5-453f-a3d7-f2e42276d7c3>85c4d4ab-e749-42dd-9451-94eb366ce7bb, b883d1f3-9a7a-493f-b640-8912f8c26cd0:85c4d4ab-e749-42dd-9451-94eb366ce7bb>7ff121b2-0d29-4e9b-b12c-f6e8fc012f89, fceb5632-5ffe-4499-929b-638eba75e5f8:7ff121b2-0d29-4e9b-b12c-f6e8fc012f89>f6d510af-2eb5-46e9-a1aa-2385f1c38b7f, 7e0626ca-cb2b-4048-9e01-85d07c58095a:f6d510af-2eb5-46e9-a1aa-2385f1c38b7f>b1c6a60a-d757-4ad9-a788-c60803b617ad, 4ffc1f56-a07a-4682-8f07-d0ce7e721c86:b1c6a60a-d757-4ad9-a788-c60803b617ad>7e266d4a-1d4d-450e-bcaa-be35b6f5d402, c685f3e7-908e-4928-a445-631665745967:7e266d4a-1d4d-450e-bcaa-be35b6f5d402>d8092fc5-4dea-43a6-a8e1-c68dde5a9d80, 74a974b1-cdd7-4ec9-aa18-9331e1da67bb:d8092fc5-4dea-43a6-a8e1-c68dde5a9d80>edb80954-ecf5-4ec2-8d2a-37bdc28790ff, e55329cf-10bf-4251-bdc3-fa0f41d7ec64:edb80954-ecf5-4ec2-8d2a-37bdc28790ff>1705e003-c8a9-41a9-9ff6-a25d3f36902b, 389e2686-960d-4936-b850-3f643b4fb05b:1705e003-c8a9-41a9-9ff6-a25d3f36902b>b234cb66-d2dc-4a51-978d-fcbbfdb3b796" -->

<ol title="A detective hired by Captain Varn's ex-husband finds nothing on her, is troubled by his client's wish to hurt her, finds that she is the better parent and the father only wants revenge, cancels his contract, and, when her ship is destroyed, helps the politician win custody of her daughter.\nIllustrates: Telling right from wrong, Diligence at work">
<li id="ecfd224a-7ab6-4397-8f49-6fe043e233c1" timestamp="0.1" location="earth, the detective's office" words="1564-2017">Captain Varn's ex-husband, who is not over her and wants custody of their daughter, hires the detective to find dirt on her.</li>
<li id="213dd038-fb32-48ad-90b4-63f3f78e35ac" timestamp="0.2" location="earth, a restaurant" words="2018-3657">Following Varn, the detective watches her dine with a man he recognizes: a politician on the committee that oversees the space agency.</li>
<li id="508750bb-0570-4732-a097-ec2d251e25f5" timestamp="0.3" location="earth, a restaurant" words="3658-5102">He photographs their meetings, but finds nothing on Varn: she drinks little, talks about her daughter, and goes home alone.</li>
<li id="41317c82-ab7b-4df6-8c17-e33939448a3e" timestamp="1.4" location="earth, the company's offices" words="9887-11236">Told by his client to dig into the man, he tails the politician to the offices of a mining company known for its ties to the mafia.</li>
<li id="b615a6e6-19d5-453f-a3d7-f2e42276d7c3" timestamp="2.1" location="earth, the detective's office" words="13434-14017">He hands his client the photographs and the company's name. The man is glad of anything that will hurt Varn, and never once asks about his daughter. For the first time, the detective is uneasy.</li>
<li id="85c4d4ab-e749-42dd-9451-94eb366ce7bb" timestamp="2.2" location="earth, the detective's office" words="15952-16745">Instead of looking for more on Varn, he starts looking into his client.</li>
<li id="7ff121b2-0d29-4e9b-b12c-f6e8fc012f89" timestamp="2.3" location="earth, Varn's house" words="18473-20852">Watching Varn's house, he sees the politician spend the day looking after her daughter, and a bond forming between them.</li>
<li id="f6d510af-2eb5-46e9-a1aa-2385f1c38b7f" timestamp="3.1" location="earth, the detective's office" words="23487-24203">He sends Varn a message on her ship, asking to meet her when she can.</li>
<li id="b1c6a60a-d757-4ad9-a788-c60803b617ad" timestamp="3.2" location="earth, the daughter's school" words="27384-28198">From the girl's teachers and Varn's neighbours, he learns that Varn has raised her daughter alone, and raised her well.</li>
<li id="7e266d4a-1d4d-450e-bcaa-be35b6f5d402" timestamp="3.3" location="earth, the detective's office" words="29898-30704">He finds that the father has hardly seen his daughter in years, and has told his friends that the custody case is about making Varn pay.</li>
<li id="d8092fc5-4dea-43a6-a8e1-c68dde5a9d80" timestamp="3.4" location="earth, the court" words="31964-33021">He sits in on the custody hearing, where the photographs he took are used to paint Varn as the companion of a man tied to the mafia.</li>
<li id="edb80954-ecf5-4ec2-8d2a-37bdc28790ff" timestamp="3.5" location="earth, the detective's office" words="34629-35115">He cancels his contract with Varn's ex-husband, and sides with Varn.</li>
<li id="1705e003-c8a9-41a9-9ff6-a25d3f36902b" timestamp="4.2" location="earth, the politician's office" words="39618-40411">He brings the politician what he has found: that Varn is the better parent, and that the father only wants to hurt her.</li>
<li id="b234cb66-d2dc-4a51-978d-fcbbfdb3b796" timestamp="4.5" location="earth, the court" words="43884-45099">When Varn's ship is destroyed, he puts his evidence before the court for the politician, so that her daughter goes to the man Varn trusted with her, not to her father.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: note id="edfb2fb4-da22-56cd-9354-739195887a99" -->

## Concept arcs of The detective hired against Captain Varn

0.1 Captain Varn's ex-husband, who is not over her and wants custody of their daughter, hires the detective to find dirt on her.
  Telling right from wrong: —
  Diligence at work: —
0.2 Following Varn, the detective watches her dine with a man he recognizes: a politician on the committee that oversees the space agency.
  Telling right from wrong: —
  Diligence at work: The detective tails Varn closely enough to notice and place the man she dines with, a detail a careless watcher would miss.
0.3 He photographs their meetings, but finds nothing on Varn: she drinks little, talks about her daughter, and goes home alone.
  Telling right from wrong: —
  Diligence at work: He records every meeting and watches Varn to the end of each evening, and reports the nothing he finds rather than stretching it into dirt.
1.4 Told by his client to dig into the man, he tails the politician to the offices of a mining company known for its ties to the mafia.
  Telling right from wrong: —
  Diligence at work: He follows the new lead on the politician patiently until it turns up the mining company and its mafia ties.
2.1 He hands his client the photographs and the company's name. The man is glad of anything that will hurt Varn, and never once asks about his daughter. For the first time, the detective is uneasy.
  Telling right from wrong: The detective first judges his client, uneasy that the man cares only for hurting Varn and never asks about his daughter.
  Diligence at work: —
2.2 Instead of looking for more on Varn, he starts looking into his client.
  Telling right from wrong: The detective acts on his unease and turns away from the job he is paid for to question the man paying him.
  Diligence at work: The detective will not settle for his client's story, and turns his investigation on the client himself.
2.3 Watching Varn's house, he sees the politician spend the day looking after her daughter, and a bond forming between them.
  Telling right from wrong: The detective sees the politician caring for the girl, and begins to judge the man differently from what his photographs implied.
  Diligence at work: The detective keeps watch on Varn's house all day and sees what his photographs of the dinners never showed.
3.1 He sends Varn a message on her ship, asking to meet her when she can.
  Telling right from wrong: The detective reaches out to the woman he was hired against, ready to hear her side.
  Diligence at work: The detective seeks Varn's own account as one more source to check.
3.2 From the girl's teachers and Varn's neighbours, he learns that Varn has raised her daughter alone, and raised her well.
  Telling right from wrong: The detective judges from what he hears that Varn is a good mother, the opposite of what his client wants shown.
  Diligence at work: The detective questions the girl's teachers and Varn's neighbours, checking Varn's parenting with those who have seen it.
3.3 He finds that the father has hardly seen his daughter in years, and has told his friends that the custody case is about making Varn pay.
  Telling right from wrong: The detective judges that the custody case is revenge, not care for the child.
  Diligence at work: The detective digs into the father's history and his friends until he finds how seldom the man has seen his daughter and what he wants from the case.
3.4 He sits in on the custody hearing, where the photographs he took are used to paint Varn as the companion of a man tied to the mafia.
  Telling right from wrong: The detective sees his own photographs used to paint Varn falsely, and sees the wrong his work has served.
  Diligence at work: The detective follows his work into the courtroom to see how it is used.
3.5 He cancels his contract with Varn's ex-husband, and sides with Varn.
  Telling right from wrong: The detective gives up his paying client and sides with Varn, choosing what is right over what pays.
  Diligence at work: —
4.2 He brings the politician what he has found: that Varn is the better parent, and that the father only wants to hurt her.
  Telling right from wrong: The detective puts what he has learned in the hands of the man who truly cares for the girl.
  Diligence at work: The detective hands the politician the full case his careful work has built: Varn's good parenting and the father's spite.
4.5 When Varn's ship is destroyed, he puts his evidence before the court for the politician, so that her daughter goes to the man Varn trusted with her, not to her father.
  Telling right from wrong: The detective acts on his judgement in court so that the girl goes to the man Varn trusted, not to the father who wanted her only to hurt Varn.
  Diligence at work: The detective lays the evidence he checked so thoroughly before the court, and it carries the case.

<!-- cell: note id="68dd0411-78f1-4cd8-9f16-b123a091cfdc" -->

## The custody of Captain Varn's daughter

How a lawyer fought to take Captain Varn's daughter from her, and lost the girl to the man Varn trusted.

1. Armed with the detective's evidence, Captain Varn's ex-husband hires a lawyer and sues for custody of their daughter while Varn is away on her mission to Mars.
2. At the hearings, Varn has to answer from her ship over a delayed link. The lawyer argues her dangerous assignment and her ties to a politician linked to the mafia, and the court leans toward the father.
3. Varn's ship is lost before the ruling. The politician asks for custody as Varn wished, the detective's evidence shows that the father only wanted to hurt her, and the court gives the girl to the politician.

<!-- cell: plot id="8f4f71f1-37ca-4d57-a0cf-45471d855f9a" title="The custody of Captain Varn's daughter" edges="baea2afe-a380-488b-8a6c-fe480a595662:3de9da00-a114-44c2-b928-5a9a4607b20e>125e3aff-9837-4abf-b289-527ba3e949f5, 6707810e-3776-4402-b17f-f2d07b04a8c3:125e3aff-9837-4abf-b289-527ba3e949f5>cd62e9ff-c532-4813-89af-554fcbcab636, 750b8823-44dd-4838-901d-a782ca0568bc:cd62e9ff-c532-4813-89af-554fcbcab636>74048a74-1b5e-4b90-a21f-54044574db30, dfbe00c4-7b1b-4dcb-b8a0-d787076ab381:74048a74-1b5e-4b90-a21f-54044574db30>c58e4b7f-b365-4c69-9b62-1001dc2d6fc9, fac08b49-6ac7-4efa-aa6e-7fea5e450c82:c58e4b7f-b365-4c69-9b62-1001dc2d6fc9>e6e5cb18-ddf3-4bbb-a06f-af763b38d43b, 546955cd-360f-42f8-b501-205d64cb7154:e6e5cb18-ddf3-4bbb-a06f-af763b38d43b>dfe42004-939c-483e-9583-148504e78d3f, 1259a8c7-0369-4a37-9163-23d3ddcda356:dfe42004-939c-483e-9583-148504e78d3f>4a8327f1-a68b-4923-9884-de47f1b33e74, 93f1d7a7-bd76-466a-9d1b-c096ed1f94e3:4a8327f1-a68b-4923-9884-de47f1b33e74>145b23ba-cb74-4c19-bd9e-d9b934bc245f" -->

<ol title="Hired by Captain Varn's ex-husband, a lawyer sues for custody of their daughter while Varn is at Mars, turns the court against her with the detective's photographs, and, when Varn's ship is lost, sees the girl given to the politician once the father is shown to have only wanted revenge.\nIllustrates: Parenthood">
<li id="3de9da00-a114-44c2-b928-5a9a4607b20e" timestamp="2.2" location="earth, the lawyer's office" words="16746-17290">Captain Varn's ex-husband brings a lawyer the detective's photographs, and hires her to win him custody of his daughter.</li>
<li id="125e3aff-9837-4abf-b289-527ba3e949f5" timestamp="2.3" location="earth, the court" words="20853-21440">The lawyer sues for custody: the mother is away on a dangerous mission, and keeps company with a man tied to the mafia.</li>
<li id="cd62e9ff-c532-4813-89af-554fcbcab636" timestamp="3.1" location="earth, the court" words="24204-24865">At the first hearing, Varn has to answer from her ship over a delayed link. Her answers arrive minutes late, and the court hears them as evasions.</li>
<li id="74048a74-1b5e-4b90-a21f-54044574db30" timestamp="3.2" location="earth, the court" words="28199-28801">The lawyer argues that Varn's assignment puts her life at risk, and leaves the girl with no parent at home.</li>
<li id="c58e4b7f-b365-4c69-9b62-1001dc2d6fc9" timestamp="3.4" location="earth, the court" words="31964-33021">She puts the detective's photographs before the court: Varn with the politician, and the politician at the doors of the company.</li>
<li id="e6e5cb18-ddf3-4bbb-a06f-af763b38d43b" timestamp="3.5" location="earth, the court" words="35116-35636">When news of the alien ships around Mars reaches the court, the judge leans toward the father: the girl needs a parent who will come home.</li>
<li id="dfe42004-939c-483e-9583-148504e78d3f" timestamp="4.1" location="earth, the court" words="37661-38139">The judge announces that she will rule for the father.</li>
<li id="4a8327f1-a68b-4923-9884-de47f1b33e74" timestamp="4.5" location="earth, the court" words="43884-45099">On the day of the ruling, word comes that Varn's ship is lost. The politician asks the court for custody of her daughter, as Varn asked him to.</li>
<li id="145b23ba-cb74-4c19-bd9e-d9b934bc245f" timestamp="5.1" location="earth, the court" words="45100-45486">The detective's evidence shows that the father only wanted to hurt Varn. The court gives the girl to the politician, and the lawyer is left knowing whom she fought for.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: note id="d65d7729-a7a3-55b2-8eb1-bcf86aec8310" -->

## Concept arcs of The custody of Captain Varn's daughter

2.2 Captain Varn's ex-husband brings a lawyer the detective's photographs, and hires her to win him custody of his daughter.
  Parenthood: The father asks to become the one who raises his daughter, but what he brings is photographs of her mother, not any sign of his own care for the girl.
2.3 The lawyer sues for custody: the mother is away on a dangerous mission, and keeps company with a man tied to the mafia.
  Parenthood: The lawyer judges Varn as a parent by her absence and the company she keeps, not by how she has raised her daughter.
3.1 At the first hearing, Varn has to answer from her ship over a delayed link. Her answers arrive minutes late, and the court hears them as evasions.
  Parenthood: Varn has to defend her raising of her daughter from far away, and the distance that keeps her from the girl also makes her answers sound evasive.
3.2 The lawyer argues that Varn's assignment puts her life at risk, and leaves the girl with no parent at home.
  Parenthood: The lawyer argues that raising a child needs a parent who is at home and alive, and that Varn's work leaves her daughter without one.
3.4 She puts the detective's photographs before the court: Varn with the politician, and the politician at the doors of the company.
  Parenthood: —
3.5 When news of the alien ships around Mars reaches the court, the judge leans toward the father: the girl needs a parent who will come home.
  Parenthood: The judge comes to believe that the girl needs a parent who will be there every day, and she sees that parent in the father because he is safely at home.
4.1 The judge announces that she will rule for the father.
  Parenthood: The court prepares to give the raising of the girl to her father because of blood and presence, without asking what care he has given her.
4.5 On the day of the ruling, word comes that Varn's ship is lost. The politician asks the court for custody of her daughter, as Varn asked him to.
  Parenthood: Varn had already chosen who would raise her daughter if she did not come back, and she chose by trust rather than blood; the politician comes forward to raise a child who is not his own.
5.1 The detective's evidence shows that the father only wanted to hurt Varn. The court gives the girl to the politician, and the lawyer is left knowing whom she fought for.
  Parenthood: The court sets aside the father, who used his daughter only as a weapon against her mother, and gives her to the man who will care for her, because a parent is whoever does the raising.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="b9c0b1c4-d7a0-43a0-9851-43b2f20d2377" title="Communication and understanding" words="e858ceeb-bc58-41f1-8a4f-4d5d581edfc5:7597-7901, 9419ba30-5f4f-4fec-ae20-aa4582b5286d:9377-9687, 6319f876-9707-4b02-8043-7ac43f7652b6:11307-11579, 14ae7774-7500-40a4-8e0a-923e7ff3db3b:14135-14463, 8871e09f-88e2-4325-ab9a-a12835a06da0:18045-18312, ce3391ff-5aed-4f42-992d-37c5a286a178:23012-23215, 8da15c7e-9bbe-4e3d-a712-238a1ab7950e:26193-26468, 5adfe47a-32fc-4121-b93d-307976b3381c:28802-29093, 7598ee9c-ea9c-4d6f-93d5-872904a4c80e:30874-31156, e8083115-46c8-4c71-974a-d62aea028e24:33115-33396, 807afcd7-07c8-494e-83b9-a3ae7433e917:35762-36033, 8c933009-80b8-436e-baaa-451edefc095e:38666-38987, 37621e28-7a39-4b52-b1b1-6ca62db0dba7:39502-39553, e957ea49-0a1d-4167-a9c0-1542c08764c3:40449-41094, 42ff196c-b027-4ff9-92da-502f75db29de:41855-42102, 685f2044-6061-4db9-98de-247f02b3d09d:42957-43279" -->

How beings exchange their understanding of the world through signs both sides can read. Without shared signs, each side reads the other's actions in its own terms, and the errors this produces grow into fear and harm.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="9b3935d3-7478-41e4-b6f1-b798fb29a439" title="Motherhood" words="f6f55fc4-b6de-4d3b-a2d0-7a3718a8e690:5607-5862, f1b0a4b4-3c07-4577-9ea3-641d6b963534:7079-7365, 0ebdac08-47c4-49f7-b887-3907acdc0d30:8128-8401, 40b8c27e-3e63-4a9b-b4c6-86ae07b7a92c:11720-11986, c225c2c7-86c4-4b5a-9f92-ac5688d06218:14575-14849, 1abcb896-419a-41b5-8858-04d297d48d1e:20421-20730, 67168517-3743-4dc7-8b17-e4b83289f5dd:33606-33953, 9c44e595-ed1e-4684-b6d9-b66919fb7c1e:36151-36467, 0553b229-143d-4dac-99e5-3988340d91f2:39036-39216, 2c540c4c-6d9d-4fee-b10d-530db32f2ffa:39217-39501, cd7dc74c-a5d3-4065-a119-73fddf6fec8d:39518-39531, 6d08ccf2-7b73-4e61-b7e0-62e8ba2cb60c:40603-40762, 0386a293-aa5a-4b6b-9385-b844f346dfe3:40813-41006, 6ccbe0f9-8266-4645-b8cc-9da44675edd8:43451-43807" -->

A mother's unconditional love for her child, shown as care. She protects the child from whatever threatens it now, and prepares it ahead of time by teaching it what it will need to live on its own and do well. At the limit, she puts the child's life above her own.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="2ff08bb2-8566-426e-b0c9-fba88100c806" title="Corruption" words="91f89af4-4cfe-4a02-9ffb-447c833e8889:2220-2373, 7b4f79b7-cdb4-47f7-a5fa-dde695905bf0:3365-3489, 8dcc1ad9-2970-4e49-a1f9-b7839c436bbd:6089-6364, e5b9feef-0de6-40e3-8428-aa9de3f3da23:8655-8989, ab53b9c9-7ebe-4d01-b5ae-979f903483bb:10364-10693, fb8c6a75-d30b-4ad4-9778-9b4fae03f7b0:12852-13264, f6b9974a-10b7-470f-8d3c-1ddc2318985e:17408-17756, 582637fd-ced0-4314-8f3b-24a49a579d0b:21533-21867, 522e216f-73c3-4f8d-873d-c1a531c9438e:24934-25212, 53e81572-b6cc-46f4-b85f-7c724c9782cc:26882-27255, 361b8e79-fa40-4f7b-bdb1-0f72347987db:29355-29580, 8ec07727-041d-42dd-a506-d956a6f93f0a:29581-29715, cc6a022f-eaad-490d-8244-03e3088c1639:31507-31851, 2e550145-c13f-4925-88f5-5b31ba422cc7:34158-34467, 62b3c80f-970e-4735-856e-f07373aaa774:38206-38508, 875ef78c-171c-4cbd-a29a-c820c068f92b:41290-41590, 3b4f3e84-e3b5-4e45-b9bb-67395790da73:42361-42692, 9682dc5c-8461-4d72-a016-44e704c9ef36:43952-44176" -->

The use of public power for illegitimate private gain: an official who sells his influence over decisions to those who pay him, and serves them instead of the public.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="d2d12cb8-843f-4d96-af62-7071d89238b1" title="The need for partnership and closeness" words="89f79177-852f-4ae3-9fbf-1cb0ee068e90:2374-2437, 2f0ce1f0-8efe-496f-b3d3-639b7eea027f:2495-2586, a34b23fb-6c28-4ffd-b88b-9df1b6ae3020:2728-2761, efefacbd-0b59-45b4-844d-caf023728b97:2791-2819, 720f451c-0b15-4b4c-9562-fbf87129ee2f:3658-3940, bcb88fac-d2de-4693-8130-caabc93a3d38:4174-4487, c044424f-7772-48ac-8cfd-5f94f3a533e2:12031-12699, 414ea471-8080-4d39-b394-84b0fcb5a0cf:14963-15771, 2bc4378f-90b0-41ac-8afc-df7e84222b76:19632-20314, e45b7460-ac64-4364-934d-06543482e12f:22563-22855, e4fd541f-e49b-4606-9663-2efbbcd32e60:23297-23366, d537bc07-ffe6-46bc-bb05-a20ca184b08b:25299-25445, 522ffb84-3866-4b48-8b6c-6bac77bcabe2:25460-25516, 05c5f738-5693-4940-9721-caee84ff28af:25570-25639, 84294f27-41f9-4886-b918-a9c2dea51cba:36535-37570" -->

The need to be close to someone: warm, constant contact with another person, and knowing that the bond is stable, that the care is mutual, and that it will last. Without it, a person is lonely even among others.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5a08cf6c-2808-4c65-9290-af60f1588e0b" title="Good beats evil" words="5242b0b8-2ad6-4059-8747-35781c06ba4f:867-1515, c056d058-1b49-423e-8ccc-c848c6ef11d1:2041-2114, 3bd7da16-6e72-477f-bda9-030ab54f0ce5:2160-2219, f40ac85f-d8fb-4d95-82f1-113c389456f0:2438-2494, 097755a1-3ded-4149-a0e0-e18c5085e797:2650-2673, ca2dd683-74ee-4117-ba74-0ddc53a6c449:2687-2727, b2829142-2bdc-4fe0-a076-9b4bbdea9863:2762-2790, 31acae01-ab07-49f1-9291-19a3a37d70d9:2820-2854, 792861d4-647e-46a2-b143-853690dc9175:3941-4173, 2eb8a954-aee5-4463-993c-86e03ddf21f0:4252-4522, 07a22306-eadd-4abb-a75f-51248ad29e91:10009-10738, 1f347dd1-95b5-43da-8dc8-ef21fa78fc19:12760-13293, f763ba53-44a9-407e-a9e7-5bd8fb48037a:15065-15847, 83fccd27-30f9-44fb-a9a9-cc66ec474001:19475-20373, 914e854e-2b4e-45af-8307-1760e4a023a2:22287-22465, 3f678dea-1f4c-4ac5-9832-2beeea0cd390:22620-22738, 4bf99ea7-2abf-4a4a-940e-0551919d8fd8:22830-22841, 6010f959-1f34-40d0-be3c-321ca2f2e09f:25677-25971, 6a597c84-26cd-45cb-9efa-ddf18fab0ec0:36567-37545" -->

The belief that, in the end, good prevails over evil: honesty, care and loyalty outlast scheming, greed and cruelty, even when evil seems to be winning, and someone who served evil can come over to the good.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="be3a0598-653a-4769-a8de-d05a677c86ef" title="Classical romance" words="aa3cc880-d434-4ba3-a735-706f3ef9b67c:936-968, e95f0a71-be43-4e73-bcc1-d47721e2ef33:974-1020, 854b960e-6abd-4ba1-9ce7-7f12e56daba0:1232-1328, 7dd28b3f-19d6-41bc-90a9-c00f2d27e921:1366-1480, e1469c92-0163-4c40-b2dd-ee9868ecbc54:2115-2159, 18c61771-3691-47df-86b1-b3d525fc8475:2587-2649, 9bcbc6e3-c6a5-4618-b44c-d2b7cc2770b0:2687-2727, 6da57041-5248-4a59-8cbb-f3fe30a78b7e:2855-2891, 0b5c95a2-8c99-4857-8b75-efacedec6ae9:3670-3940, a971d5d6-6b15-4e8f-aa98-21a5bb763c8f:4057-4146, 16e9e643-c604-47b0-a84c-33cf7bb72002:4348-4459, 6184fe83-9307-4448-84f3-030ccd3470f6:10009-10305, d5df251b-8347-421e-b45a-f75e223efaf1:12134-12759, 08da1964-7b47-416c-8231-a00a4eeab0d3:14904-15820, f6a6e393-876c-46d3-8382-58b860f0b14d:19742-20345, 5050fa66-562a-44c1-99aa-318bb30f6997:21958-22277, 59110b0c-0fc2-41cf-811c-7957f73e8d92:22278-22562, 883c72bd-40f2-4def-a562-adda2e8e8bcb:23216-23337, 78fd6718-dd31-4c89-a242-729bd7bace20:25213-25298, 4cf715bb-0a51-495d-b861-257f6a7145f1:25314-25339, df003908-ff91-4fae-a257-2049ba275ac1:25446-25569, 59aa8785-61d0-4e52-908b-d863aeed7c09:25640-25676, 653ddb4f-414e-4fd5-a250-e5cef2f57081:25795-25843, 76938a16-5b3a-404f-b398-f5e6c886b0a1:25961-25971, f6da449c-1d7a-4af0-9f2d-0f668814da49:36699-36744, 09b730c6-9552-4309-b856-eccaa88abd73:36904-37545" -->

A love story told the classical way: two people meet, are drawn to each other despite what stands between them, grow close through obstacles and separation, and come to love each other.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="82b6924e-4633-4b1f-b63c-30349a4d1e69" title="Telling right from wrong" words="996325ad-a784-4a4d-b44b-cb4d59e1fbb6:13534-13882, a7c69d39-2e1b-48b0-96dc-c6c54b46d3c2:15978-16249, 92dffd7b-0b07-4e16-bae7-7e9aa8d3b76b:18779-19457, de64fff5-104b-4a17-94b0-a8d5a4f59372:23744-23805, bc03dac5-03da-4850-8a23-1d1308f18bf9:23833-24004, 5fa44036-a428-42b1-ad03-0d868d80001c:24076-24095, fb10cc48-d675-41fb-9c4e-22ec0d38df9b:27433-28120, 1862c58a-6c91-4ce7-a3b6-9c2c7e041279:30335-30611, 66d4c64f-4713-4eaa-aded-e886cebee4af:32268-32783, 8915b7d5-7d64-4920-a6cb-ade2aa374c5a:34724-35022, a257a622-73f8-4d0b-aa5a-43eeda1cb1c9:39700-40292, 91aec9e7-a2c6-485f-a152-b13dcdb1f1f5:44468-44954" -->

Judging actions and character as right or wrong, and acting on that judgement, even when what is right goes against what pays.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="914405c4-e4f3-450a-ba98-1697758bfa6a" title="Diligence at work" words="058fed84-a5c9-41e6-934a-e1ffbddc130d:2899-3364, a5177468-6183-4fd2-8629-da5865091049:3490-3533, f836845d-8c9c-463b-853c-9f8ae4e8e318:4523-4761, bd0bfb2a-66e5-4573-b4ee-b44950f92d06:4762-4991, 5fcddf95-4f86-442c-861e-f4cdc848df92:10739-11124, f2b94d9f-ab9d-44f3-bae8-b310554d5c16:16250-16346, a197fd83-98d5-4487-80b0-efe1dcc31234:16347-16677, 4111e4d7-0ea3-4d45-b5f2-bea5fea313ea:18582-19474, 0ba164e0-2a13-4479-93ed-1b06ce552958:23540-23743, 9a057434-53ea-447f-9fbe-0d237fdd057b:23806-23832, e0299836-ae7e-4de8-a3ca-edf790b3188e:24005-24075, 14c7984b-ed12-4f3c-89a8-930c0631826d:24096-24117, 4f226af6-d8f6-44cb-81fe-fdeb4356713a:27433-27978, b6cc67bf-7031-4e5e-a2e2-f778a185e693:29965-30334, 5278d4b4-5477-4f88-b0b1-e32f37e06334:32140-32806, a5912353-5538-4343-bc4e-69819c6a5683:39754-39947, e14bedd5-32e0-49a8-814d-d0e02d396bcf:39956-40178, d1782b5b-ce57-485b-a80a-028f43175c5c:44535-45031" -->

Doing one's work carefully and thoroughly: following every lead, checking every fact, and not settling for the easy answer, even where no one would notice the shortcut.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="d09fe0ce-24f1-4f3d-a308-27eecce17fa2" title="Parenthood" words="e89408e3-470f-479e-8991-bacc28427734:16853-17139, fa0f8398-e0ad-44eb-aa04-41cb2e1ff36e:20949-21268, 1f593069-f126-4275-a624-860f561ec89f:24366-24741, cf98f97f-783b-4443-9115-54b0a4761e20:28322-28648, c31c8cc2-f7e8-468b-b0b3-7731e3f086c6:35202-35482, b1b0f87f-b772-4d93-9ef1-225f0ec2c189:37755-37982, 08545808-d7f6-417e-bd71-c039972d1a26:44213-44927, 2df9729b-6705-4060-be96-8bce45096c40:45185-45452" -->

Raising a child: supporting their physical, emotional and social growth until they can stand on their own. It is made by care, not by blood: a parent is whoever does the raising.

<!-- cell: markdown id="9e492f45-cd01-4e49-846e-396b7a09b1ed" -->

The command center ran on the low hum of its machines and the murmur of controllers talking into headsets. Rows of consoles stepped down toward a wall of screens where orbital tracks curved in thin green lines. In one corner Mars hung as a rust-colored disc, turning slowly under the eye of a distant probe. Officers in agency blue moved between the stations with coffee and tablets. A few visitors stood among them, marked out by the pale badges clipped to their lapels, and one of them had come on committee business.

The politician had chosen his smile before he crossed the command center floor, the way another man might choose a tie. It was a good smile. He had practised it for years on voters and donors and the other members of the committee, and on his way in, before he had ever seen her face, he had decided that Captain Varn was the woman he would use it on. He saw her across the room before anyone introduced them: a woman in a flight jacket who stood very straight by the long bank of screens and did not look toward the visitors. An aide brought him over. She turned when he said her name, and for half a breath the line he had prepared slipped away from him. She looked tired, and amused, and nothing like the stiff photograph in her file. He caught the line again before she could notice it had gone. He took her hand and kept it a moment longer than courtesy required, and he smiled the way a man smiles when something in a room has caught him off guard. He praised her service record, which he had read that morning for exactly this purpose, and asked about her work with an eagerness he had rehearsed. Each compliment was placed where it would do the most good. He was not there to admire her. He was there because a captain who trusted him would tell him things the committee did not know, and things he could sell.

Varn answered him plainly and briefly, as she answered everyone. She did not flatter him back or angle for anything. When she made some dry remark about the coffee, he laughed a little too warmly and caught her eye as if they now shared a private joke. He asked about her work, then about herself, and he let his questions drift from the official toward the personal so gradually that she hardly saw the change. When he asked whether the agency kept her busy, she said it did. "I only mind the hours for my daughter's sake," she said. "She keeps a calendar of my shifts on the fridge and crosses them off."

Her voice softened when she spoke of the girl, and he found himself listening longer than the game required, to a story about a drawing of a rocket taped above a bed. It unsettled him for a moment, the way a wrong note unsettles a man who thinks he knows the tune, and he put it down to the late hour and the bad coffee. She, for her part, found him easier company than she had expected of a committee man. She said a little more than she had meant to and smiled once without deciding to. He leaned in as though nothing else in the building mattered, and he took her straightness for a door left unlocked. He judged her an easy mark: honest people, in his experience, were the simplest to use. To anyone watching from the next console it would have looked like the start of something: a man and a woman drawn to each other across a crowded room, finding reasons to stand a little closer. Varn half believed it, and stayed polite and guarded all the same. The politician did not believe it at all. He left her with a warm word and a promise to see her again soon. When she turned back to the screens he let his gaze follow her, as a smitten man's would. Behind it he was already working out when he could next arrange to cross her path, and what he would pretend to feel when he did. As he walked away the smile fell from his face as cleanly as it had gone on. She had no idea that anything had been set in motion. For now the game seemed entirely his.

A few days later, the politician found a pretext to telephone Captain Varn, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="1d6ffa76-53b4-4551-bbbd-d4eef96091fe">and, as the line rang, he rehearsed the easy, offhand tone in which he meant to suggest they talk it over somewhere quieter than the command center, perhaps over dinner.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="ea8feefc-fd46-4f05-aab5-683bb7837556" -->

The detective's office was on the third floor of a building that had been grand once, above a pawnshop and a shuttered travel agency. The window looked out on a brick wall close enough to touch, and the light that came through it was always the same gray, whatever the hour. There was a desk, two chairs, a filing cabinet with one drawer that stuck, and a radio on the sill turned low to the news. The man came in a little after noon, in a good coat, and sat down without taking it off.

"I need someone followed," he said. "My ex-wife. Captain Varn. She's an officer with the space agency." They had been divorced for years, he went on, and they had a daughter. He wanted custody of her, and to get it he needed something on her mother, anything a court would take seriously. "Drinking. Men. Money. Carelessness with the girl. Whatever there is." He took a photograph from his inside pocket and laid it on the desk: Varn in uniform, unsmiling, her hair pinned back. He left his finger on it a moment longer than he needed to.

The detective asked the usual questions: where she lived, where she worked, what her days looked like. The man answered all of them quickly and in detail, the street and the house number, the hours she kept, the café where she bought her coffee, as if he had kept track of her all along. He still spoke of her as though the divorce were recent, sometimes bitter, sometimes not, and once he said "my wife" before correcting himself. "She always thought she was better than everyone," he said, and then, more quietly, "She was good at most things. That was the trouble." The detective wrote it all down and said nothing.

He named his rate, and the man agreed to it without arguing, counting the advance out in cash, one bill at a time, onto the blotter. They shook hands across the desk. When the man had gone, his footsteps fading down the stairwell, the detective sat for a while with the photograph in front of him. Then he wrote Varn's address on a card, clipped the two together, and put them in his jacket. On the sill the radio murmured on about weather, and the gray light did not change.

For the next several evenings the detective kept a careful distance behind Varn as she left the agency, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="831ce51a-19e7-4614-8a50-c4de9de9fb67">until one evening she turned away from her usual route home and stepped into a small restaurant, and he settled at a table across the street from which he could watch the room through its lit front window.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="5c807762-97ea-4d76-bdb5-f25ae7b560e5" -->

The rain had started early, and the windows had fogged at the edges, so that the street outside showed only as moving lights. He had chosen the restaurant the way he chose committee votes: quiet enough to talk, expensive enough to flatter, a corner table by the window where she would sit with her back to the room and look only at him. He had rehearsed the warm surprise he would show when she arrived and the questions that would make her feel listened to, and he had ordered a bottle he judged she would not refuse. When Captain Varn came in out of the rain, he stood, as a man courting her might, and pulled out her chair with a smile he had practised. Every courtesy he paid her that evening was meant to win her trust and not her heart. She had come in her uniform jacket and kept it on. When she sat down she thanked him for the invitation, poured herself half a glass, and set the bottle on his side of the table. Every charming line he had prepared seemed to land on her and slide off, like rain on a coat she had worn for years. No one from the committee ate there, which was the other reason he had chosen it. He had first met Varn on official business at the command center, where his seat on the committee that oversaw the space agency opened every door to him. Now he used that seat over dinner. When she mentioned the probe's survey of Mars, he spoke in the easy tone of a man whose office already entitled him to know. When she hesitated over what the agency had not yet made public, he reminded her, lightly, that he sat on the body that funded her work, and that nothing she told him would leave the oversight it was meant for. Then he asked the next question, and the one after it. He let her talk about orbits, survey grids and budgets for robotic diggers. Each detail went into his memory as if into a ledger he kept for himself.

Under the low lamps, she kept her phone face up beside her plate. When it lit with a photo of a girl in a paper crown, she turned it toward him before she had touched her wine. Her daughter had sent it from the kitchen at home, she said, where she had been allowed to stay up until her mother called to say goodnight. She talked about her daughter because he asked, and she kept talking because it was true and she had nothing to hide. She told him how the girl did her homework at the kitchen table, how she stayed up for her mother after long shifts, how there was no one else at home to do the raising. She talked about the girl the way people talk about the house they live in: the drawings taped to the fridge, the book they had read a chapter of every night for a month, the way the girl still padded into her room at dawn and fell asleep again with her cold feet against her mother's back. When the phone buzzed again halfway through the main course, she answered it at the table and bent her head close to the screen. She said goodnight twice, because the girl asked for it twice. Yet as she talked he found himself watching the candle catch in her eyes and forgetting, for a breath at a time, the questions he had come to ask. She laughed once at something he said, a short, surprised laugh, and leaned toward him across the cloth. For a moment the two of them looked like any couple at the start of something. Then the moment passed, and he nodded where he had planned to nod and filed it all away, a man counting the ways in.

Over coffee he began to ask whether she had dinner like this often. She set down her cup, folded her napkin and looked straight at him. She liked his company, she said, plainly and kindly, holding his gaze, and she would gladly have dinner with him again, but she was not looking for romance. She already had someone, she told him, someone who waited at the window when she came home late and would still be waiting in ten years, and that was enough to fill a life. Her daughter came first, and every hour she had off duty belonged to the girl. She would rather he heard it from her now than guessed at it later. Then she glanced at the dark phone, as if it might light again, and smiled at him across the candle like a woman who had already been called home. He smiled as if it cost him nothing, said he understood, and lifted his glass to her honesty. Across the table, her plain answer sat between them, steadier than anything he had brought with him. Behind the gesture, the lie he had brought to the table now had her refusal beside it. Two walls stood between them where there had been one, and still neither of them made a move to leave. He steered the talk back to Mars.

Across the room, at a small table by a pillar, a man in a grey coat ate slowly and did not look at the corner table more than he had to. He had chosen that table because from it he could see both the door and the mirror behind the bar. He had followed Varn from the command center through two changes of train, waited ten minutes outside in the rain to be sure she was not simply meeting a colleague on the doorstep, and walked past the restaurant twice to learn where its exits were before he went in. He had ordered something he would not have to look down at while he ate. A cruder man would have watched only her, written down the time and the name of the place, and called the evening done, since his client was paying for Varn and not for whoever she ate with. But the detective studied the man across from her as carefully as he studied her. He noted the cut of the suit, the way the man ordered without a glance at the menu, and the small badge pin on the lapel that caught the light whenever he leaned forward. The face nagged at him. He had seen it somewhere, not in person, flattened on a screen. He did not trust the feeling, and he did not let it go. Between courses he went back over it in his head: the evening news, a row of seated men behind a long desk, a hearing about budgets and launch schedules that had played in the background of some other job. He took out his phone, kept it below the edge of the table, and searched for the committee that oversaw the space agency until he found the members' portraits. He compared them one by one with the man in the window, the heavy brow, the careful smile, the receding hair at the temples, the thin scar through one eyebrow, until only one fit. Even then he waited until the man turned toward the light to be sure. Before he wrote the name down, he checked it against the committee's member list and then against a news photograph from the man's last election. It was the same man, a politician with a seat on that committee, sharing bread with a captain of the agency he was supposed to oversee. Next to the name the detective noted the time Varn had arrived and the table the man had picked for her, with her back to the room. He photographed them twice, once as the waiter topped up the wine and once as the man leaned in to ask a question, and wrote down what each had ordered, though no one would have faulted him for leaving it out.

None of what Varn said that night would go to the committee. At its sessions the politician sat among the other members and listened while they complained that the agency told them too little about Mars, and he said nothing of what he had heard over wine. He kept to himself what his office had brought him, and its worth grew because the others did not have it. He was not there to oversee the agency for the public that had elected him. He was there to know first, and the people who had paid for his career would want to know what he knew. When the plates were cleared he thanked Varn warmly, paid the bill, and made a note to arrange another dinner. At the table by the pillar, the man in the grey coat noted that the politician paid with a personal card and not the committee's. He waited until the door had closed behind them both, counted out exact change for his meal, and followed.

Over the weeks that followed, as the rain gave way to a dry, cold season, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="90af46c5-8935-484b-9e2d-abb68c62c8ab">the politician went on dining with Varn and playing coy, until the morning the news from Mars broke and he was summoned, without explanation, to the offices of the mining company that had paid for every step of his career.</li>
<li edge="ea4fcbbf-0257-43f0-9c3f-2d2767eebfd7">the politician's driver came to know the way to the little restaurant by heart, and on certain evenings the same corner table was held for him, with a second chair waiting across from his own.</li>
<li edge="cb54ba22-e80c-4aef-a96c-ff1294cb90ba">the detective kept his table by the restaurant's window, his camera in his lap, waiting each time Captain Varn and the politician sat down to dinner together.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="7c8ac1ad-9569-4ce5-a89d-95d4c1fce695" -->

The dinners became a habit before either of them called them one. They went on meeting at the same restaurant, at the same corner table, and each evening ran a little longer than the one before. Every week or two Varn found him already there, her glass of water poured and the bread untouched until she came. She began to save things up for these evenings: what her daughter had said about the moon, a drawing brought home from school, the small worries of a woman who had raised a child alone and had no one at home to tell them to. He listened, and remembered, and the next time he asked after the drawing by name. She noticed that he remembered. She noticed, too, that he never quite said what these dinners were. When she joked about the waiter treating them like a couple, he smiled and let it pass. When she mentioned the next week, he said "we'll see," and then was always there. He never pressed her. He held her chair and then sat back, let her choose the wine, and asked about her day, about her daughter, about the ships, and never about her heart. She had told him on the first night that she was not looking for romance, and he had taken her at her word so gracefully that she found herself looking forward to him more for it. It was the shyness that undid her. A man who wanted nothing from her was a man she could relax beside, and by the third dinner she had stopped watching the door, and by the fifth she had stopped counting the minutes until she had to go home.

What she took for reticence was craft. He let Varn talk. He asked about her daughter and listened with his chin on his hand, and when she turned the question round he smiled and looked down at his glass. "And you?" she asked one night. "You never tell me anything." "There's not much worth telling," he said. It was a careful smile, measured to seem shy, and it worked as he meant it to: it made her lean in, made her ask again, made her feel she was being let past a guard. He gave her small things that cost him nothing, a half-story about a summer by the sea, a regret he did not name. He knew the steps by heart and danced them well. When their hands met over the bread basket, he drew his back first, slowly, as though it cost him something. When she mentioned an old film she loved, he turned up two weeks later having seen it, and spoke of it haltingly, as if embarrassed to admit he had gone looking for it. When the talk drifted near anything tender, he laughed a beat after she did, looked down as if he had been caught, and changed the subject himself. He always changed it to the agency, to her postings and the rumours at command, and he filed away what she said as he refilled her water.

Around them the restaurant filled and emptied: couples leaning together over shared plates, families, old friends laughing too loudly near the bar. Across the table, the two of them looked like any pair who had settled into each other's company. Varn's shoulders loosened by the second course now, and she laughed more easily than she had the first night. He laughed at the right moments and touched her hand once, lightly, when she spoke of her daughter's fever. He watched her warm to him the way a man watches a lock turn. Varn paid for her own share each time and told him plainly that she was glad of the company and wanted nothing more from it. She meant every word. She spoke of her daughter's school play, of the long hours, of how little she drank because someone had to drive home sober, and she never once shaded a thing to look better than she was. He watched that honesty from across the table as if it were a weakness he had found. At the door each night he hesitated with his hand half-raised, and she saw him hesitate; then he let it fall, the gesture of a man too modest to presume, wished her a good night, and stepped back into the rain to let her leave. She walked to her car smiling and could not have said why, a little less guarded than the time before, and drove home alone. She never noticed that every hesitation came at exactly the right moment, or that his eyes, when she looked away, were not soft at all but watching, measuring how far she had come toward him and how far she still had to go. He stood in the rain until her tail-lights had gone, unmoved by anything that had passed between them, as alone as he had been when he sat down. In his car he wrote down the names of the officers she had mentioned. For now the evening was his: everything he had hidden was still hidden, and everything she had offered had been taken.

Two tables from the window, a man in a grey coat ate his soup slowly and read the same page of a newspaper for an hour. He came every time they did, and took the same table each night. He had learned the restaurant's rhythm: which waiter worked the corner, when the lights over the bar were dimmed, which chair gave a clear line to Varn's face without catching his own reflection in the glass. The camera sat in his lap under a folded napkin, and a small notebook lay beside his plate, half-hidden by the bread basket. In it he logged every meeting: the date, the hour she arrived and the hour she left, the frame number of each photograph. He counted her glasses and wrote down one, sometimes half of a second, left unfinished when the bill came. He watched her hands when she talked, saw her take out her phone to show the man a picture, and caught enough over the clink of cutlery to note that it was her daughter, again, and a school play, and a cough that would not clear. When the man leaned in, a man the detective had known on sight from the evening news, a member of the committee that oversaw the space agency, he raised the camera and took the shot, and then waited to see what followed. Nothing did. She sat back, and he wrote that down too.

He did not leave when the plates were cleared. Each night he paid before they did, so that he could be out on the pavement when she came through the door, and while the other man stood in the rain watching her go, he followed her car across the city to her street rather than assume where it went. He sat in his own car until her porch light came on and the upstairs window went dark, never later than eleven, and he stayed a while after that, in case anyone came; no one ever did. He checked the bar bill with a waiter who liked a tip, and it matched his count. It would have been easy to write that she seemed to be drinking, that the man saw her home, that the evenings ran late. No one would have checked. At the end of the week he laid the prints out in order on his desk and typed the report the same way he had kept the notebook, line by line: a little wine, talk of the child, home alone before eleven. He read it through twice, struck out the one sentence where he had written that the two of them seemed close, because he had seen nothing that proved it, and sent his client a report with nothing in it, because nothing was what he had found.

Some weeks later, on a grey morning after the news from Mars, the politician stepped out of a car at the doors of a mining company's offices, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="3edfc3a6-7c5e-4032-99da-b82b12abfed6">with nothing to offer the owners but the committee's refusal, and a name, Captain Varn, that he had not yet worked out how to use.</li>
<li edge="b111f900-09ca-4f24-b8ed-6d338ee254bd">and did not notice the plain car that had followed his through the city, nor the detective at its wheel, who had been told by his client to find out everything about this man, and who now slowed to a stop across the street and looked up at the name above the doors.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="e2249669-3aa8-4a1a-b925-cc52579e2781" -->

The space agency's building stood at the edge of the city, low and wide, its roof crowded with dishes turned toward the sky. Inside, the control floor ran in long rows of desks beneath a wall of screens, and most of the screens showed Mars: the probe's position above the red surface, the path it had flown, and the scan it had sent back that morning. Engineers and analysts sat at their stations with headsets on, reading numbers off their monitors and calling them across the room. The scan showed something under the ground. It sat in the rock below the dust, too regular to be a fold in the stone, and the readings put it in materials the agency had never recorded on Mars.

People came and went between the floor and the offices behind it, carrying tablets, printing the images and pinning them to the boards until the cork was layered three sheets deep. Groups gathered at the main screen and argued in low voices about depth, density, and what the probe could and could not see from orbit. "It's a mineral deposit," one analyst said. Another tapped the outline on the screen and asked her to name a deposit with edges like that. No one could. The probe was ordered to pass over the site again and repeat the scan. The floor went quiet while the second set of data came down, line by line, and when it was complete it matched the first. Someone let out a breath, and the talking started again, louder than before.

In a glass-walled room off the control floor, the agency's directors sat around a table with the images spread before them, coffee going cold at their elbows. They asked what equipment could reach the find, how long it would take to get there, and what it would cost, and they wrote the answers on a whiteboard that soon had no space left. Through the glass they could see the analysts still bent over their screens. On the wall outside the room, the news channels were already showing the probe's pictures, the same grey shape beneath the red dust, with a caption that asked what it was.

Within weeks of the second scan, the whiteboard in the glass-walled room had been wiped clean, and _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8c1e507b-7ce0-4319-a470-d7db8470c2ab">in its place a single word had been written in capitals, EXCAVATE, beneath a photograph of the reddish plain where, far away on Mars, a team and its robots would soon be lowered towards whatever lay under the surface.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="bf374336-8678-4dca-94b2-a414f491d0d4" -->

The house stood at the end of a quiet street, and by evening the only light in it came from the living room, where the television threw its shifting colours across the low ceiling and over a shelf of framed photographs. Rain had begun after dinner and ran down the window in thin, uneven lines. Two plates still sat in the kitchen sink, and a schoolbag lay open by the front door where it had been dropped.

Captain Varn sat on the couch beside her daughter, close enough that their shoulders touched, while the evening news played across the room. The girl had folded her legs under her and leaned into her mother's side as though it were the most natural place to be, and Varn let her, one arm resting along the back of the couch behind her. When the anchor said that a probe had found something under the surface of Mars, the girl shifted to see the screen better, and Varn shifted with her without thinking, so that the space between them stayed as small as it had been.

Grainy images from the probe filled the screen: a pale scar in the red ground, a shape too blurred to name. The girl tipped her head against her mother's shoulder to watch.

"What is it?" she asked.

"Nobody knows yet," Varn said. "That's why people will want to go and look. When you don't know what something is, you look carefully before you decide."

The girl considered this and said nothing more. Varn watched the images too, but every so often her eyes went down to the top of her daughter's head, to the hair the girl had not brushed since school, and she stayed where she was. She did not reach for the remote or for her phone. When the report ended and the news moved on to other things, neither of them got up, and Varn kept still so as not to disturb the warm weight leaning against her.

On the screen the anchor turned to the weather, and a map of cloud drifted slowly across the coast. Outside, the rain kept on against the glass, and the photographs on the shelf caught the light and lost it again as the pictures changed.

A few days later, Varn stood in a bright room at the command center, her cap under her arm, while _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="be9f3907-80db-4266-aeab-13658c114004">on the wall screens behind her the probe's grainy images from beneath the surface of Mars glowed beside a list of ships readying for the voyage, and on the desk before her lay a sealed folder bearing her name, her phone heavy in her pocket with the call she had promised to make home.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="2ca68b71-7edb-4112-9c72-d008c7f66a7d" -->

The company's offices filled the top three floors of a glass tower above the river, and that afternoon the rain ran down the windows in sheets, blurring the cranes along the docks into grey shapes. In the lobby, a model of an open-pit mine sat under a glass case, its tiny trucks frozen on roads that spiralled down into painted rock. A receptionist took the politician's coat without a word and led him to a private elevator that rose so smoothly he felt it only in his ears. Upstairs, the carpet swallowed their footsteps, and the corridor smelled of polish and cold coffee.

The owners did not rise when the politician was shown into the top-floor office. A screen on the wall replayed the agency's announcement of the find beneath the Martian surface, and the elder of the two let it run while he slid a folder across the desk: receipts, transfers, the donations that had paid for every campaign the politician had ever won, the last of them dated the week he took his seat on the committee that oversaw the space agency. "We bought that chair," the younger one said, "and today it starts earning." They wanted the rights to mine Mars, all of them and for no one else, before the agency had finished deciding what it had found. They wanted the licence to come from the committee, with his vote and his voice behind it, so that it would bear the government's seal and not theirs.

The politician did not ask whether the find belonged to the public, or whether the committee ought to wait until the scientists knew what it was. "How soon?" he asked. "And what should the bid look like?" The elder owner told him the company's lawyers had already drafted it, worded so that it would sound like the nation's interest. The politician would bring it to the committee as his own, and he would find out which members could be persuaded and which could be paid. He took the draft, put it in his briefcase beside his committee papers, and promised that by the next session the bid would be on the table. The younger owner closed the folder of receipts and kept it on his side of the desk.

On the screen, the announcement ended and began again: the same grainy image of red dust, the same spokeswoman at the same lectern. No one turned it off. The receptionist was waiting in the corridor when the door opened, and she rode down with him in silence, watching the floor numbers fall. Outside, the rain had not let up. The politician stood under the awning with the briefcase held against his coat while his driver brought the car around, and behind him, through the lobby glass, the little trucks went on not moving down their painted roads.

At the next session of the committee, the politician waited until the routine business had been read into the record, and then he _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="7b5149a2-d430-406d-87a6-a47ca5ccd7dc">rose from his seat with a folder under his hand, the mining company's bid tucked inside it, and looked down the long table at the members who would have to vote on it.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="7fcd590c-6b29-4929-84b2-401fbec67213" -->

The plain lay flat and rust-coloured under a thin, butterscotch sky, broken only by low ridges of wind-carved rock and the long shadows they threw in the morning light. Nothing moved there but the dust. Then a point of light grew overhead, and the excavation team came down to Mars in a lander that settled on its spread legs above the site the probe had marked. The team was machines: a cradle of digging robots, folded tight against their frames for the descent, and the cranes and tethers that would set them in the ground. No one rode with them. The lander's hull ticked as it cooled, and its beacon pulsed a slow green, the only colour on the plain that the plain had not made.

When the dust from the landing had thinned, the lander's arms swung out over the marked spot, and one by one the robots were lowered on their cables to the red surface. Each one hung for a moment, turning slowly on its line, before its feet found the ground. They touched down, unfolded their legs and their cutting arms with a stiff, jointed patience, and took up the positions the survey had laid out for them in a ring around the place where the probe's scan had found something buried. A status line scrolled up to the lander from each in turn: *Position confirmed. Ground stable. Ready.*

Then they began to dig. Drills bit into the frozen crust, throwing up fine plumes that the thin air could barely hold, and scoops lifted the loose rock away and carried it to spoil heaps at the edge of the ring. The robots worked without pause, cutting the pit wider and deeper in even layers, each pass as careful as the last. Sensors on their arms sampled each layer as it was exposed, iron oxides, basalt, a seam of ice that glittered briefly before it sublimed away, and they sent the readings up to the lander in a steady stream.

The small, pale sun crossed the sky and sank, and the cold came down hard over the plain. By the end of the first Martian day the pit had sunk below the level of the plain, a dark, square-cut wound ringed by heaps of spoil. The machines kept on into the cold of the night, lit by their own floodlights, their beams crossing over the pit and throwing hard shadows up its walls, working their way down toward what the probe had found.

Some time before the second dawn, the readings that streamed up from the bottom of the pit _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="86e0c370-b499-448c-b32d-cbba25ee73bf">changed, and the robots at the bottom of the trench slowed their drills, turned their lamps on the freshly cut wall, and began to brush the dust away.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="1a0b7f9f-e026-4934-a3c4-9f2e8f230866" -->

The command center hummed under its low ceiling, a long room of consoles and wall screens where the red curve of Mars turned slowly above the duty officers' heads. In the briefing room off the main floor, the admiral slid a folder across the table to Captain Varn and told her that the laboratory ship would leave for Mars within the week, and that it would need an escort. The corvette was hers. Varn read the first page twice, thanked him, and asked no questions she could not ask later.

The orders were still warm from the printer when Varn stepped out of the briefing room. Before she signed for the corvette or looked at its crew list, she found an empty office along the corridor, shut the door and called home. She would not let her daughter hear it from the news, or from a neighbour who had seen the mission announced, or in a hurried sentence at the door with a bag already on her shoulder. When the girl's face came up on the screen, her hair still wet from the bath, Varn sat down so that they were eye to eye and told her plainly. "They've given me a ship. It's going to Mars with the scientists, and I have to go with it. I have to leave."

She watched her daughter's mouth tighten and did not rush to fill the silence. At last the girl asked how long, and Varn answered with the truth: "Months, not weeks." Then, one thing at a time, she went through what the girl would need while she was gone. She would still have school and her friends, and there would be food in the freezer. She knew where the spare key was hidden and which number to call if anything went wrong, and she should not be afraid of nights when the house was quiet. "Say them back to me," Varn said, and nodded at every one her daughter got right. When the girl's voice broke on the last, Varn did not tell her not to cry. "It's all right to miss me," she said. "But you're old enough now to manage. I know you are, because I watched you learn every one of those things yourself."

Outside the glass wall of the office, a technician hurried past with a tablet under his arm, and somewhere down the corridor a voice called out a launch window. Varn stayed in the chair a moment after the screen went dark, the folder unopened on her knee. Then she stood, straightened her uniform, and went back out into the noise of the command center to meet her crew.

On the last morning before the launch, Varn came home early and found her daughter waiting at the kitchen table, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="680cb49c-61ec-493c-b7b5-b0f7ae98966d">her schoolbag already packed beside her chair, and her eyes fixed on the duffel Varn had left by the door the night before.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="faef16b8-169e-4948-a6bb-548199953874" -->

Dawn came thin and copper-coloured over the plain, and the floodlights around the cut had not yet been switched off. The pit lay at the bottom of a shallow basin, a square wound in the regolith some forty metres across, its walls stepped where the robots had terraced them on the way down. Cables ran from its lip to a cluster of pressurized modules a few hundred metres off, and inside the largest of them six operators sat at their consoles in the stale, recycled air, coffee going cold beside their keyboards. Outside, the wind moved fine red dust across the terraces and let it fall again. The drills had gone quiet an hour before.

The robots had worked through the night on the floor of the cut, and when the dust settled in the floodlights the operators saw what the drills had found: a curved surface, dark and grained, rising out of the rock like the shoulder of something carved. The spectrometers named what they could. Iridium, osmium, a lattice of elements in proportions no Martian stone should hold. The excavation team read these numbers the only way their instruments let them, as the composition of a material. "That's not geology," the shift lead said, leaning toward the screen. "Nothing forms like that on its own." They called the find "the object" in the first log entry, and by the second they called it "the sculpture." The scans showed ridges along its flank, set at intervals too regular to be chance, and someone said they looked like ornament. Someone else said the shape looked worked, the way a mason works a block, and everyone agreed.

Nothing on the screens contradicted them. A faint warmth showed in the thermal band, and they logged it as residual heat from the drills. The ridges along the flank seemed, between one pass and the next, to sit at a slightly different angle, and they put it down to drift in the lidar. "Recalibrate after the next sweep," the shift lead said, and nobody argued. Each reading found its place in the story they already had: a made thing, left by a hand, buried for an age, and theirs to uncover. The robots were ordered to clear the rock from around its base so it could be seen whole. The team watched the feeds and talked about who might have carved it and why. None of them asked whether it was looking back, because nothing they had built could have shown them the answer.

Down in the cut the robots turned on their treads and lowered their arms again, and the drills began to whine against the stone. The floodlights clicked off one by one as the sun cleared the rim of the basin, and in the flat early light the dark curve at the bottom of the pit showed its grain more plainly than before, still and patient in the rock, while the dust rose around it and drifted away on the wind.

By midmorning the robots had pried the first fragments loose from the rock around the object's base, and the operators, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="068c1b1c-69c1-4f29-b25e-e8bf05869287">eager for a closer look than any camera could give them, sealed the fragments into a canister and readied it for the climb to the laboratory ship circling high above Mars.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="834d9ff4-255d-469f-b789-c38e737fbf89" -->

The house stood at the end of a quiet street, its windows yellow against the early dark. Inside, the radiators ticked, and the smell of the evening's soup still hung in the kitchen. A pair of small rain boots stood by the door beside a larger pair. In the hallway the coat hooks ran low on one side and high on the other. The framed photographs on the wall showed the same two faces over and over, at beaches, at birthday tables, under a pine tree hung with paper stars.

Varn knelt in the hallway so that her eyes were level with her daughter's. Her duffel bag lay forgotten by the door. She buttoned the girl's cardigan to the top, the way she did on cold mornings, though the house was warm. Then she went over it all once more, slowly, as she had every night that week: where the spare key hung, which number to call if anything frightened her, that dinner came before homework and lights went out at nine. She did not hurry when the girl's lip began to shake. She took the small tablet from the hall table, put it in her daughter's hands, and guided her fingers through the call until the girl could open the line by herself. Then she had her do it twice more, until she was sure.

"Every day," Varn said, tucking a strand of hair behind the girl's ear. "Every single day, at this time, I'll call. Wherever I am, whatever the ship is doing, you'll hear from me. You can tell me anything: what you ate, who was mean at school, what you dreamed." She held her daughter's face in both hands, as if to keep it. "You won't be on your own. Not for one day."

The girl pressed herself against her, and Varn held on long past the time she had set aside for leaving. She let go only when her daughter's breathing had steadied. Before she rose she took a pen from her pocket and wrote the time of their first call on the girl's palm in ink. She left her standing there with the tablet clutched to her chest.

Varn shouldered the duffel bag and opened the door. Cold air came in off the street, and somewhere a dog barked twice. On the step she turned and looked back. The small figure in the lit doorway lifted the hand with the ink on it and held it up, palm out, for her to see. Then the door swung shut, and the hallway was quiet but for the ticking of the radiators.

Weeks later and millions of kilometres from that lit doorway, Varn stood on the narrow bridge of her corvette, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="e3150870-e21b-437c-b2fb-3ab3cc490da3">watching the rust-coloured curve of Mars swell across the forward screen while the laboratory ship slid into orbit alongside, and far below, the excavation site waited to send up the first of what it had found.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="a3a2080a-8d5c-4ebe-a44a-24c0318b02e1" -->

The committee room sat at the end of a long corridor in the old wing of parliament, beneath a ceiling of dark coffered wood and windows too high to show anything but a grey square of sky. The long oval table had been polished until the lamps lay on it in pools, and around it sat the members who oversaw the space agency, a dozen of them, with water glasses and folders and the patient faces of people who had heard many proposals. A clerk at the side table kept the minutes in a soft, steady patter of keys. On the wall behind the chair hung a framed photograph of the agency's first launch, its white plume faded almost to yellow.

The politician rose with the company's papers squared before him. They were the pages the owners had pressed into his hands across their own table, now retyped under the parliament's crest so that nothing on them showed where they had come from. "Colleagues," he said, "we have an opportunity on Mars that will not wait for us." He spoke of the nation's interest there, of order, and of the need to settle early who would be trusted with whatever lay beneath the surface. He spoke of an exclusive licence as though the idea had grown out of the committee's own deliberations. He named the company only once, briefly, as a firm of proven capacity. He did not say that its owners had paid for every campaign he had ever won, or that they had sent him into this room for exactly this. He sat on the body that oversaw the space agency, and he was using that seat to hand the agency's discovery to the men who kept him in it.

The other members turned the pages and did not reach for their pens. "What, precisely, would this licence cover?" asked a woman near the window, her reading glasses pushed up into her hair. Across from her an older member set his folder down. "No one in this room knows yet what has been found," he said. "We cannot grant away a thing we cannot name." The politician answered smoothly that delay served no one, and that others would move while the committee hesitated. "Every week we spend deliberating is a week someone else spends preparing," he said. He pressed the point harder than any disinterested man would have, and a few heads lifted at the tone of it. When the chair moved to defer the bid until the agency reported, he gave way with a gracious nod. Behind it he was already counting the votes he would need, and the price the owners would make him answer for if he came back without them.

The chair called the next item, and the clerk's keys went on clicking. Papers were shuffled back into folders, and someone poured water. The politician sat down with his hands folded over the retyped pages, his face composed and attentive, while the grey light in the high windows thinned toward afternoon.

When the session closed and the members filed out into the corridor, the politician gathered the retyped pages into his case and, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="bab104ec-a35e-47a0-94e4-fa1b450bf3c6">without stopping to answer the members who called after him, took a car across the city to the mining company's offices, where the owners were waiting to hear why their licence had not passed, and where he meant to tell them about Captain Varn.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="7daca8bc-4843-49cd-acac-b10d6857b908" -->

The corvette came out of its last braking burn on the night side of Mars, a few kilometres off the laboratory ship's flank. The two vessels had made the crossing together, the corvette slightly ahead and to one side. Now they slowed into the same orbit above the excavation site, a grey wedge of hull beside the larger vessel's ring of modules and radiator vanes. Below them the planet turned slowly, rust and shadow, the dig a pale scar near the terminator where the robots had been working since before the mission left Earth. Captain Varn stood at the rear of her small bridge with her hands clasped behind her back. She gave the orders that set her ship on station, close enough to the laboratory ship to cover it and high enough to keep the site below in view, and watched the tactical screen sort the near space into tidy labels: the laboratory ship, the relay satellites, the descent track of the excavation rigs. They had only just settled into position when a fresh contact rose off the surface on a thin line of exhaust. "Lift capsule, Captain," her sensor officer said. "First samples, right on schedule." Varn nodded. "Hold station on the lab ship. Tell them we're in position and they have the bay." The acknowledgment came back a moment later, cheerful and brief. The capsule climbed out of the thin atmosphere carrying the first samples the excavators had cut from around the buried object, and Varn's crew tracked it all the way up until the laboratory ship's bay doors closed behind it.

The sample canisters came up from the surface in that sealed capsule and docked with the laboratory ship's receiving bay. The scientists waiting there had already decided what they were about to handle: a specimen. They logged each fragment by mass, depth and coordinates, the way one catalogues a core drilled from a cliff face, and fed slivers of it into spectrometers that answered in the only terms they had been built to give: elements, isotopes, ratios. The readouts named metals no Martian rock should hold, bound in a lattice too fine and too regular for any known mineral. Around the analysis tables the team spoke of inclusions and crystal habit, of pressure and cooling rates. Every strangeness the instruments returned became a question about how the stuff had formed, never a question of what it might be doing. When one cross-section showed patterns repeating at intervals along its grain, a geochemist traced them with a stylus and called them growth bands. The others nodded, and the note went into the file under structure.

They cut, ground, irradiated and dissolved, and recorded with care what each procedure did to the material. Not one of them recorded what the material did in return. No one set a receiver to listen beside the samples or watched for a change that answered a change. No one asked whether the drilling far below, or the scanning beams now turned on it, could be taken by anything as a touch, a probe, a blow. The object kept the form of a stone sculpture, and the people studying it read it as a stone. Their hypotheses multiplied on the shared displays, each written in the language of minerals and each fluent in that language. The answers that came back to them from below were all about rock, because rock was the only thing they had asked about.

On the corvette the shift settled into the quiet rhythm of an escort with nothing to escort against. The laboratory ship's bay lights glowed steady through the forward optics, and every so often its data feed blinked with another batch of results the bridge crew had no reason to read. Varn stayed a while longer at the screen, watching the second capsule load at the dig site and the robots on the surface crawl along their marked grid. On the surface below, the digging went on. Then she handed the watch to her first officer and went below, leaving the corvette to hang in the dark beside its charge, its few guns cold and pointed at empty space.

The hours that followed passed without alarm, and in that long stillness, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="ae990dc0-412d-4a61-b053-a7efedcbe994">far below the red dust of Mars, the shape in the rock lay just as it had lain for untold ages, while the probe traced its slow passes overhead and the excavation team's machines settled onto the plain above it.</li>
<li edge="0ec1d0f9-3e65-4dff-aa9a-37705671ef4a">Captain Varn left the corvette's bridge to her crew and sat alone in her quarters, keying the long link home to her daughter.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="e07f277e-671d-4a2a-a64b-e89b10fc8977" -->

The offices of the mining company took up the top three floors of a glass tower on the industrial edge of the city, where the river district gave out into warehouses and loading yards. The lobby gave nothing away: a reception desk of pale stone, a logo of crossed picks worked into the floor, and no list of tenants. The politician passed the doorman with a nod that said he had been there before. Across the street, in a parked car with the engine off, a man with a thermos beside him watched him go in.

Upstairs, the politician had been kept waiting in a corridor outside the owners' room, where a screen on the wall ran the agency's announcements without sound. The news came up on it as he stood there, slipped between a weather report and a launch schedule as if it were nothing: Captain Varn had been given command of the corvette that would escort the mission to Mars. For a moment he saw the restaurant again: the small table by the window, her hands around a glass she hardly drank from. She had told him plainly that she had a daughter and was not looking for romance, and he had smiled and let her think he was content to wait. They had kept meeting since. He had kept playing coy, and she had kept coming back. Now months and millions of kilometres would open between them, the kind of parting that in old stories tests whether two people were ever meant for each other at all. He read the line twice and felt no dread at it, only relief, loose and immediate. Something in him eased, and he did not stop to name it. There would be no more evenings across a candle, measuring how warm his laugh should be. No more would he watch her face for the moment she might see through him. She would be a voice on a call, far enough away that nothing he pretended could be tested at close range. He told himself that distance suited the arrangement better than nearness ever had. If he paused over the thought that he might miss the dinners, he let it pass. Then the relief settled into something he recognised more easily. She would be there at the excavation as the samples came up. From out there she would be his window onto whatever lay under that red ground, and he would not have to sit across from her while she was. The buried thing that no one could explain would pass under her eyes before it reached anyone else's. Every hour he had spent laughing at her stories and pretending to like her had paid off at once. He straightened his tie, as if fortune had only kept an appointment he had made long ago, and was called in.

The politician stood before the owners' long table as a supplier stands before a buyer, and laid out what their money had bought this time. Captain Varn, he told them, had been given command of the corvette escorting the mission to Mars. The committee would wait in the dark for whatever the agency chose to release, trimmed and late. He would hear it from orbit, first-hand, from a woman who thought him a friend. He spoke of his dinners with her the way a man speaks of a line of credit. The seat they had paid to put him in had brought him into the command center, and the command center had brought him to her. Now that door was theirs too, opened by an office he held in the public's name. "She'll be my eyes up there," he said. The owners nodded at the word *my* as if it meant *ours*. One of them smiled and poured him a drink. The other only nodded, which was better than praise. He promised them the edge they had paid for. Whatever the excavation uncovered would reach this room before it reached the committee room. When the licence came to a vote, he would know more than any member sitting beside him. What he knew could be weighed, timed and sold before the others knew there was anything to judge. He did not say he would tell the committee what Varn told him, and no one at the table asked. The arrangement depended on the committee knowing less. It depended, too, on the man meant to watch the agency on the public's behalf watching it for the company instead. He named Varn as easily as he would name an instrument on the table, and no one in the room thought it strange. When he finished, the owners did not thank him. They treated it as a delivery that had arrived on schedule, and asked only how soon the first report would come. The glass was heavy and cold in his hand. Outside the tall windows the city lay spread under a clear morning sky. For the moment it seemed that everything in it bent the way he and the men across the table wanted it to go.

The man in the parked car was a detective, and he had not come there by chance. His client had told him to dig into the man who dined with Captain Varn, and had wanted it quick: find out who he was and what he was worth. The detective could have settled it with an afternoon and a public directory. The politician's name, his seat on the committee and his voting record were all there to copy into a report. Instead he had spent three mornings outside the man's apartment building with a thermos and a notebook. He wrote down the hour the man left, the car that collected him and the plate on that car, and each morning he checked the plate against the last. On the fourth day the car did not turn toward parliament. He let two vehicles slip between them and kept it in sight through the river district, past an unhurried loop around a block that might have been caution or habit. When it stopped before the glass tower, he drove past, circled the block and parked where he could see the entrance. Then he noted the time, a quarter past nine, before he did anything else.

The politician stayed inside for an hour and forty minutes, and the detective logged every minute of it. He photographed him going in, and he photographed him coming out with two men who walked him to the door and shook his hand on the steps. He photographed the crossed picks in the lobby floor and the clock on his own dashboard. The lettering above the doors named a mining company. He did not trust his memory with the name, so he wrote it down letter by letter. That evening he pulled the company's registration, then its list of directors, and then the court records filed under each director's name. A name he half recognized he did not leave at half. He traced it through two dissolved holding companies and an old trial transcript until it sat beside the name of a family the newspapers had linked to the mafia for twenty years, men the papers still called businessmen, in quotation marks. Only when every link had a document under it did he close the file and start the report his client had asked for.

Weeks later, with the corvette in orbit around Mars and the first samples already coming up from the excavation, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="012949a1-cc62-48b6-befe-e4e1c4aa2c5f">the politician sat by his screen each evening, waiting for Varn's call from orbit, a notepad within reach and the company's number never far from his mind.</li>
<li edge="f25de376-eaec-469f-a60b-61e859174605">a call from the politician on Earth reached Varn's quarters aboard the corvette, his face on the small screen, waiting out the long delay for her to answer.</li>
<li edge="103a36ba-110e-4a00-b6c7-88104477181c">the detective sat in his office on Earth, an envelope of photographs and a mining company's name on the desk before him, waiting for Varn's ex-husband to come through the door.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="3d258652-59d2-4ba7-a92b-61dc40a7ee0e" -->

The plain lay flat and rust-colored under a thin, pale sky, broken only by low ridges of wind-scoured stone and the long shadows they threw at morning. Dust moved across it in slow sheets when the wind rose and settled again when it fell, and for a very long time nothing else had moved there at all. The cold went deep into the ground, and the ground kept its quiet.

Under that red rock, the thing that would be taken for an object was alive, and it had been watching. When the probe first passed overhead, it felt the faint tremor of the craft's engines through the stone and took it for the footfall of something that hunted by sound. Then the scan swept the ground, a thin pulse of energy that pressed through its outer shell and came back. It understood the pulse the only way its kind understood such things: as a touch, a probing for the soft places beneath a hide. No pattern in the pulse matched any pattern it knew. No greeting rode inside it, no question it could recognize, nothing it could send back that the sender would know how to read. So it read the scan as its own kind would have meant it, and it lay very still.

Then the excavation team came down. The landers settled one after another in plumes of ochre dust. The robots unfolded on their cables, and their drills turned and tested the ground. The organism followed each movement through the rock the way a body feels hands closing around it. It counted the machines and marked where they gathered, and found that they gathered above it. It reached out in the slow chemical signals its kind used to warn one another off, a pressure and a change in the mineral at its edges. Nothing above it slowed or turned aside, because nothing above it knew that stone could speak. To the organism, that silence was an answer too: whatever had come had heard the warning, and had chosen to dig.

On the surface, the work went on in the steady, unhurried rhythm of machines. Floodlights on tall masts washed the site white against the dimming afternoon. The drill heads bit and withdrew, and fine grit hissed down the spoil chutes into growing heaps at the edge of the pit. The landers stood silent on their splayed legs, antennas angled toward the sky. Over all of it the wind kept sweeping the dust across the plain, as patient as it had always been.

As the drill heads worked lower through the long Martian night, the organism felt each layer of stone above it thin, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="a0d5f4a7-6bc0-4c39-baed-c52acb26a702">and it tracked the slow descent of the machines with growing unease, holding its silence for now as the nearest bit ground ever closer to the hard shell of its own body.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="914586ee-5f88-4264-b676-a291c3f0f4d0" -->

Varn waited until her watch was over and the bridge had gone quiet before she opened the line to Earth. She had promised her daughter a call every day, and she meant to keep that promise here, with Mars beneath the hull, as she had on every day of the voyage. She had worked out the hour at home before she left, so that the call would reach the girl after school and before bed, when she would be at the kitchen table and not alone in the dark. When the small face came up on the screen, Varn leaned close to the camera, as if the nearness could cross the distance. She asked first whether she had eaten, whether the neighbour had walked her home, whether she had remembered her key, and she listened to each answer all the way through. Then the girl looked down and said she missed her. Varn did not tell her to be brave, and she did not hurry past it. She said she missed her too, every hour, and that this was why she called: so that each day the girl would hear it from her and know it was still true. She told her to keep the lamp on in the hall if the house felt too big, and to write down anything she wanted to tell her so that none of it would be lost before the next call. When the girl asked whether she would call tomorrow, Varn said yes, at the same time, and she watched her daughter's shoulders ease before she let the line close. The corvette hummed around her in the half-light. Through the viewport the red curve of Mars turned slowly, and far below it the excavators went on working in their pit, small lights in the dust that no one aboard could see from this height.

Varn went down to her cabin and shut the door. Since the third evening of the voyage he had called her at this hour, then again the next, and soon every day, so that she found herself watching the clock on the bridge long before the signal came. The corvette was never quiet. There were twenty voices on her decks and Mars turned enormous beneath the hull, and still she took his calls alone, the way one keeps a candle out of the wind. Tonight's came on the minute, as the corridors dimmed and the planet filled the small port beside her bunk. He had arranged the first of them the way he might have booked a table, with a set hour and a promise not to be late. He had put on a good shirt, as though they were back at the corner table, and laid out a plate and a glass on his side of the screen, which made her laugh before either of them had said a word, and she had fetched her own ration tray to sit across from him. He had done the same tonight. She had told him once, across a white tablecloth on Earth, that she had a daughter and was not looking for romance, and he had never pressed her on it. Now the distance did what the tablecloth had not. Each sentence crossed the gap between the worlds and came back minutes late, and they had learned to fill the waiting with patience instead of silence. He would ask a question and keep looking at her, and she would watch his face hold still and warm until her answer reached him and he smiled at it, and she found that she did not mind waiting for him either. When the signal broke up she held still until his face came back, and noticed that she held her breath doing it. They talked across half a planet's worth of dark the way people talk across a table, longer than they ever had, first about the object in the rock, which she said was unlike any stone she knew, and then about nothing. Neither of them hung up first. When a call ended early because of her watch, the next one began with him asking how she had slept, as if he had kept the hours for her.

It was during one of those long delays, with the red curve of the planet turning below her, that the talk turned, without either of them steering it, to after. He asked what she would do when the mission was over. She said she would take her daughter to the sea, and told him about the house she wanted nearer the coast, where the girl could grow up with the water in sight, about the garden she had never had time for, and about a coast road she had always meant to drive. He said he knew that road, though he had never learned to swim, and she told him that could be taught. Then she reminded him that she had told him she was not looking for anything. He said he remembered, and asked which month she thought the weather would be best there. She laughed and answered, and the lag carried her laugh to him late, so that on his side he smiled after it had already ended on hers. More slowly, she said she would like a weekend somewhere with no screens at all, and she let the words travel to him before she could take them back. His reply arrived with the light of Earth's evening behind him. He said he would hold the place for whenever she came home, and that he would still be calling every night until she did. Varn sat with that for longer than the delay required. Out past the port the planet rolled on, and the distance, for once, felt like something that would end. He was waiting at the other end of it. When the line finally closed, Varn sat a long while looking at the blank screen, and then at the planet, and found that she had leaned toward the camera the whole time. For the first time since they had met, she was counting days toward something rather than away from it, and already counting the hours until he called again.

He had set the hour of these dates himself, as he had set every one of them, choosing the times when the mission's reports reached the committee so that he would always have something fresh to ask about. He had answered her warmly. He had said he could picture the house by the sea, that he would like to see it one day, and the words had come easily because he had rehearsed most of them, while a pad on his desk, just below the frame, filled with what she let slip. He had listened to the first part of that call the way he listened to committee testimony: for what it was worth to the men who had paid for his seat. Varn had talked freely, as if to a friend and not to a member of the committee that oversaw her agency. Each time the talk drifted toward the future, he had let it drift a little, then steered it back with a careful, affectionate question: was the thing they had found still as strange as before, were they keeping it quiet even from him? She had laughed and told him more than she should have, because she believed she was talking to a man who wanted to know her days, not her mission. She said the samples coming up to the laboratory ship that morning were unlike any rock the scientists had ever logged, and that the agency was keeping every finding quiet, out of the reports that should have gone to the committee and through it to the public, and that the scientists would say nothing at all over open channels. He had asked, in the mild voice of a man merely curious, what they thought it was made of, how deep the shafts had gone, and who on the agency's side decided what stayed sealed. Every answer she gave him was something the committee had a right to hear, and he kept every one back from it. When she said that she had not planned on anything like this and was glad of it, he smiled into the lens and said he was glad too, and under the desk his pen went on writing.

Before her face had faded from his screen on Earth, he opened a second channel, one that ran to the company's offices and not to parliament, and gave the owners all of it: the strange materials, the depth of the digging, and above all the agency's silence. That silence meant the committee was deciding blind, and the company could be the only bidder that knew what lay in the ground. He did not log the call, and he would not mention it at the next session. He had turned his seat on the committee, the trust of a captain who answered his questions because he held it, and the secrets of a public mission into a private briefing for the company that owned his career, and he delivered it as if it were simply his job. She had ended their call promising another at the same hour, and he had promised to be there. For now the scheme held, and her trust carried it along. High above Mars, Varn dimmed the cabin lights and lay down in her bunk, and below her the excavators went on digging in the dark.

The next day, while the excavators went on cutting deeper into the rock below, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="349dabc1-77c0-449a-9e5e-911211da5275">Captain Varn stood on the bridge of her corvette in orbit, the feed from the dig glowing on one screen while, on the other, her daughter's face waited for her to speak.</li>
<li edge="477ea5f0-3a6b-46da-a336-c15a53fe2e82">the politician walked into the committee room in parliament with Varn's words from the night before still in his ears, and took his seat among members who knew far less than he did.</li>
<li edge="75031a43-cfbb-4833-ab12-1301068ca59c">Varn sat alone in her quarters aboard the corvette, high above them in orbit, and waited for the politician's face to appear on the screen for their evening call.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="21ed20ea-e8bf-45c7-8c69-063d7d173f35" -->

The detective's office was two rooms on the third floor above a laundromat, and in the late afternoon the dryers below thudded through the floorboards like a slow pulse. The blinds were half drawn against the low sun, laying bars of light across the filing cabinets, the coffee ring on the blotter, the coat stand with nothing on it. The client came up the stairs ten minutes early, took the chair across the desk without being asked, and kept his coat on, as if he did not mean to stay longer than the business required. "Well?" he said. "Show me."

The detective laid the photographs on the desk one by one, and he watched his client's hands instead of his face, because hands were harder to school. Varn across a restaurant table from the politician; Varn at the door of her own house, alone; the politician on the steps of the mining company, its name written in the detective's own hand on the back of the print. He had done jobs like this for twenty years and had never once asked himself what the client meant to do with them, since a man paying for the truth was owed the truth. But the hands went straight to the print with the company's name on it and held it up to the light from the blinds. The man laughed, short and pleased. "This is it," he said. "A woman who sits down to dinner with that crowd won't keep anything. The court will see exactly who she is." He did not look twice at the photographs where Varn was only eating and talking, and going home alone. Those had nothing he could use, and so to him they showed nothing at all.

The detective waited for the question every father he had worked for had asked sooner or later, however bitter the divorce: whether the girl had been at the restaurant, whether she was well, who was with her while her mother was at dinner. It did not come. The man spoke about Varn's career, Varn's pride, the look there would be on Varn's face when the papers were served, and his daughter appeared in none of it except as the thing that would hurt her most to lose. The detective took the envelope with his fee and found it weighed less than it should have. He had sold photographs to worse men than this, he told himself, and it was not his business what a client wanted in his heart. Yet for the first time in his trade he caught himself weighing the man across the desk the way he weighed the people he followed, and finding him wanting.

The client gathered the prints he wanted into his coat and left the rest scattered on the blotter. His footsteps went down the stairs quickly, and the street door banged shut behind him. The detective sat on in the chair while the bars of light slid up the wall and thinned to nothing, and below him the dryers thudded on, turning someone else's clothes over and over in the dark.

The next morning the detective opened the file on Varn where he had left it, and sat a long while with his pen above the blank page before _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="155f4eae-86a0-433b-bff2-f7b0bc21869d">he closed it, pushed it to the edge of the desk, and drew from his drawer the contract his client had signed, the man's name and address printed neatly across the top.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="1219d442-4788-47ca-b392-63499002eff0" -->

The plain lay under a thin, butterscotch sky, so cold that the dust the wind lifted seemed to freeze in the air before it fell. Ribbons of it slid across the rust-dark basalt and piled against the rims of old craters, and the sun hung small and pale over a horizon too close for comfort. Out of that sky the landers came down on columns of fire, settled, and paid out their cables, and one by one the robots were lowered onto the ground. They unfolded their legs, swung their drill arms clear, and switched on floodlights that turned the stones around them a hard white. Nothing in the rock beneath them looked like anything but rock.

The organism had lain in the rock of Mars long enough to know its every tremor, and when the machines set their treads on the surface above it, it listened to them the only way it knew how: as vibration and pressure and heat. The robots spread out in a ring and began to cut. To the engineers far above, the pattern meant a careful excavation grid, each bore spaced to spare whatever lay beneath. To the organism, a ring closing on a single point meant only one thing, the way a pack closes on prey. It sent a slow pulse up through the stone, the signal its kind used to say *I am here, I am alive*. The machines had no instrument tuned to such a thing. Their sensors logged the pulse as seismic noise, and the drills went on. The organism took the silence as an answer. Whatever was above it had heard it and chosen not to stop.

The drills went deeper, and every shift in their rhythm, a bit changed or a pause to clear the dust, seemed to the organism a decision, a hunter testing where to strike. It tightened the layers of its shell and pulsed again, louder, and again the machines treated the shudder as a fault in the rock and adjusted their torque to cut through it. Then a diamond bit broke through the outer plate of its exoskeleton and into the living tissue beneath. On the lab ship's screens the reading showed as a density change, a promising sample. Below, it was pain, sharp and deliberate, and the organism no longer needed to wonder what the strangers meant by it. It turned its attention away from the machines it could not speak to and reached across the dark toward those who would understand it. Into the distance it sent the one message whose meaning no one of its kind could mistake: it had been found, and it was being attacked.

On the surface nothing marked the moment. The floodlights burned on, the wind laid a fine skin of dust over the spoil heaps, and the ring of robots kept its patient shape around the bore, their arms rising and falling in a silence that the thin air carried nowhere.

Deep in the stone, beneath the patient rhythm of the drills, the organism held its signal open across the dark and waited, until from a distance no light could have crossed in a thousand years, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="645bc048-2064-4d91-964f-1287cf09cfe5">an answer stirred along the thread, faint at first and then steadying, gathering itself into a question meant for the organism alone.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="99c424ec-648e-4455-aed9-84f7a3b4383a" -->

On the corvette's bridge, Captain Varn turned the camera on her console toward the screen that showed the excavation site, so that her daughter could see it too. Down on the red ground the excavators were pushing deeper into the rock around the object, and Varn described each step to the girl the way she would have explained a storm passing over their house: what the machines were, and why their arms moved like that. She told her how the dust hung in the thin air long after the drills stopped turning. She did not soften it or dress it up. When the girl leaned close to her own screen, Varn waited until she had found the dark shape in the trench before going on, because she wanted her daughter to see the thing with her own eyes and not only hear about it.

"Nobody knows what it is yet," Varn said plainly, and let the words stand, the way she had always answered the girl's questions at the kitchen table, with the truth as far as she had it. She told her that the scientists on the laboratory ship argued about it all day, and that the digging went on anyway, a little deeper every shift. She described her watch, the meals in the narrow galley, the long hours in front of the screens, so that her daughter could picture where her mother was at each hour of her day on Earth. Before the call ended she asked what the girl had done at school, and she listened to every word of the answer with the same care she gave the readouts from Mars. The screen went dark, and for a while the bridge was quiet but for the low hum of the air recyclers and the tick of the excavation telemetry. The night crew moved softly at their stations. Below, the lights of the dig site burned in a small, steady cluster on the planet's shadowed face.

Later, deep in the corvette's night, at the hour they had settled on, his call came through, the way it had come every night since the corvette left Earth. Varn took it in the narrow cabin behind the bridge, with the red curve of Mars sitting in the porthole behind her like a lamp no one could switch off, and the door shut on the dimmed corridor and the last voices of the night crew. She had spent the whole watch among people and spoken to none of them about anything but the ship. Now his face filled the small screen, a little grainy, his voice lagging a breath behind his lips across the distance, and she found she had been waiting for it since morning. She would not have admitted that at the restaurant table on Earth where she had told him plainly that she was not looking for romance, and she had meant it. He asked about her day, and as always he had his other questions ready, small and careful, about the digging and what the agency was keeping quiet. She told him about the trench and the shape in it, no more than she had told her daughter, and let the rest of his questions pass. They talked the way they had learned to talk across the distance, about small things: the food on board, the rain he said had not stopped in the city all week, what they might do when she came home. She laughed at something he said and heard herself do it, and for a moment neither of them spoke, the hiss of the long link filling the space between them. He remembered what she had told him the night before and asked after it, and she noticed that he remembered. She sat with her knees drawn up on the bunk, the screen close to her face, and did not look away from his. "I need to ask you something," she said at last, and her voice had gone careful, the way it went when she set down something she could not easily pick up again. "My girl. I talk to her every day, but it's a screen, and she's eleven, and she won't say when it's bad. Would you go and see her? Not for an hour. For a day, if you could. Make sure she's eating, that she's all right." She told him the girl had sounded small on the last call, and that the neighbour who looked in on her was kind but could only do so much. She was not anyone the girl could lean on. Varn asked it plainly, without bargaining or offering anything back, as if it had not occurred to her that a man might take such a thing and use it. She told him where the spare key was kept, what her daughter liked for supper, and that the girl would test him before she trusted him. She gave him the most precious thing she had as easily as she would hand a friend her coat.

The delay held her words in the air longer than she liked. On his side of the link he sat with the screen's light on his hands and found that he had nothing to weigh it against. Every other word between them had been a trade, measured in advance, and he understood that this one could be too: a door into her house, a way deeper into her confidence. But the trade would not take shape in his mind. What stayed in front of him was her face as she waited, tired and open, and the plain fact that she believed he would do right by her child. He said yes before he had worked out what the yes was worth, and for once he did not try to work it out afterward.

When his answer finally reached her it was simple. It had plainly left him the instant her question arrived, and yet nothing in it was light. "Of course I will," he said, and told her she should not worry. Something in the way he said it made her lower her eyes and smile before she remembered the camera could see her. Varn let out a breath she had been holding for weeks, and her hand rose to the edge of the screen as if she could reach through it. "Thank you," she said, softer than she meant to. When she looked up again she did not hide what it meant to her: there was no one else she would have asked. She had kept every man away from her daughter for years, she told him, and this was different now; she knew he would go, and that he would still be there when she called the next night and the night after. He promised he would go that week. She made him promise to tell her how it went, and he promised that too. For the rest of the call she spoke more easily than she had since leaving, her shoulders lowered, like someone who had finally set down a weight she had been carrying alone and trusted the hands that took it. They stayed on the line a little longer than either of them needed to, neither one willing to be the first to end it, and when the link at last closed she stayed where she was a long while, looking at the dark glass where his face had been. On his side of the closed link, the questions he had meant to ask were still unasked on his notepad, and he did not reach for it.

Back on Earth, in the quiet house where Varn had raised her alone, the girl sat at the kitchen table with her mother's photographs on the wall behind her, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="ed793d2c-da3b-4c3d-9a84-8589b810cdb3">the tablet propped against the fruit bowl, where her mother's face flickered on the screen from somewhere above Mars, and a question she had been holding all day was finally ready to come out.</li>
<li edge="9cc74a17-ad4d-4ab9-859e-485620632ebc">and across from her, in the chair her mother usually took, sat the politician, a cup of tea going cold in his hands as she began to tell him about the woman in the pictures.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="1257f24e-d99c-49a2-ad78-ed3ef81bb791" -->

The office was one room above a laundromat, and the radiator ticked under the window while the evening traffic went by in the wet street below. The folder on Captain Varn lay where he had left it, thick with photographs of restaurant tables and a woman walking home alone. Beside it lay the client's retainer cheque, with a note clipped to it promising that more would follow when he brought something better. The detective sat a long time with his hand on the folder. The job was plain enough: keep following her, find the drink or the lie or the man that would hold up in court, and get paid for it. He had done uglier work for less. But he kept hearing the client's pleased voice over the photographs, and the question the man had never asked once in all their meetings: not how his daughter was, not who looked after her while her mother was away. A father who wanted his child back would have asked. This one had only wanted to know how badly the pictures would hurt.

He closed Varn's folder and put it in the drawer. The cheque he left uncashed on the corner of the desk, where he could see it and know what he was putting at risk. Then he took a clean folder, wrote his client's name across the tab in block letters, and began on the man the way he would have begun on any stranger he did not trust. Nobody was paying him to ask. If the client found out, no more money would come, and probably no more work from anyone the man knew. He weighed that, and turned to a blank page anyway, because the man paying him was the one he now had questions about. What he had already handed over, the photographs and the company's name, could have been billed that morning without a single question from anyone. Instead he played back the recording of their first meeting, stopping it every few sentences, and copied out each thing the man had claimed: when the marriage had ended, how often he saw his daughter, why he wanted her now, what he said Varn had done wrong. Beside each line he ruled a blank column for proof, because in all those weeks of watching her he had never once checked any of it.

He spent the evening filling that column, starting with what anyone could find and checking all of it twice. He pulled the divorce decree from the county archive's registry and read it through to the last page rather than the summary. It gave him a date eight years back, and an address the man had left within the year. His driver's records gave another, and the address on the contract was a third. He wrote each down with the date he had found it and where, the way he always did, so that nothing on the page rested on his memory. The decree gave the father every other weekend. He noted that and did not trust it, because a line in a decree said what a man had been allowed, not what he had done. So he went back through his own expense log for the dates the man had met and paid him, and set them against the weekends the man had said he spent with the girl. Two of them fell on the same days. Where a fact could be confirmed in two places, he looked in both. Where something did not fit, he wrote down what would settle it, and did not let himself guess. It would have been easy to stop at the decree and call it a picture of the man, and no one would have known the difference. By the time the laundromat below had gone dark and the street had gone quiet, the folder was thicker than the one on Varn, and the column was only half full. He turned the lamp up and made a list for the morning of the records he had not yet reached: the school's pickup records, the neighbours on Varn's street, the friends the man drank with, each with the question he meant to ask beside the name. At the bottom he underlined a reminder to call the court clerk about the filing date of the new suit.

In the morning, before any of the offices on his list had opened, he drove out to Varn's street and parked across from her house, meaning only to _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="b883d1f3-9a7a-493f-b640-8912f8c26cd0">see who was looking after the girl now that her mother was gone, and he had barely finished his first coffee when a car he knew from the restaurant nights pulled up at her gate.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="fe86f128-03aa-47bc-9f92-6e699ee26249" -->

The lawyer's office was on the ninth floor, above a street that never quite went quiet. The blinds were half drawn against the afternoon, and the light came through them in pale bars that lay across the carpet, the filing cabinets and the long walnut desk where she met her clients. A framed diploma hung behind her chair beside a print of a harbour nobody had ever asked about. The radiator ticked. When the man came in, he did not take off his coat. He sat down, set a brown envelope on the desk, and kept one hand flat on it until she had uncapped her pen.

Then he opened the envelope and laid the photographs across the lawyer's desk one by one, as if dealing cards: Captain Varn at a restaurant table, Captain Varn leaning toward a man in a grey suit, the same man at the doors of a mining company. A private detective had taken them, he said. "I want custody of my daughter. I want to be the one who raises her." The lawyer looked at the spread and waited for the rest: a drawing the girl had made for him, a report card, a photograph of the two of them anywhere at all. There was none. Every picture he had brought was of the mother, and the girl appeared in none of them.

"Tell me about her," the lawyer said, because a court would ask. He said she was eight, then corrected himself to nine, and said she was a good kid, quiet, like him. She asked who took the girl to school, who sat with her when she was sick, who knew her friends' names, and each time the answer was her mother, said with a tightening of the jaw, as though the care Varn gave the girl were one more charge against her. He tapped the photograph of the man in the grey suit. "That's what my daughter is growing up around." The lawyer wrote down that the mother was away on a dangerous mission and kept company with a man tied to the mafia, and left blank the line where she would have written what the father did for his child. Then she named her fee, and he signed without reading, already gathering the pictures of the woman he had come to take the girl from.

He left the copies she asked for and went out without shaking her hand. The door clicked shut behind him, and the radiator went on ticking. The lawyer squared the photographs into a neat stack, slid them into a new folder, and wrote the case name on the tab in block capitals. Outside, the light had shifted, and the bars on the carpet had grown long and thin. She sat a while longer with the folder closed in front of her, then turned on the desk lamp and began to draft.

The courtroom was colder than her office, and when the lawyer rose with the folder under her arm, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="baea2afe-a380-488b-8a6c-fe480a595662">the father leaned forward beside her, his eyes on the photographs she had tucked inside it, while across the aisle the seat meant for the girl's mother stood empty and the judge waited for her to begin.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="764ae94f-6155-4bb9-bfe7-5e391ac21e31" -->

The committee room sat at the end of a long corridor on the parliament's third floor, a high-ceilinged chamber with dark panelling and a horseshoe of a table that had been built for twice as many members as now sat at it. The morning light came in grey through tall windows and lay flat across the papers, the water glasses, the nameplates. On the wall behind the chair, a screen showed the agency's latest image of the dig site: a pale scar in the red dust, and the small square shadows of the machines working at its edge. A clerk sat apart at a narrow desk, her fingers resting on the keys, waiting for something worth recording.

The politician rose before the committee with Varn's words still in his head, and he spent them as if they were his own. "The object is unlike any rock the agency has ever brought up," he told the members. "And the agency is sitting on what it finds, while the public pays for every metre of the dig." He did not say where he had learned it, and he did not say that the same words had reached the mining company's offices before they reached this room. He called for the dig to go faster and deeper, and for the agency's slow equipment to be replaced at once. A member near the end of the table, an older woman with reading glasses pushed up into her hair, asked where such machines could be found on short notice. He had the answer ready. "There is one firm already equipped for deep work under hard conditions," he said, and named it as if he had only just thought of it. Then he passed around specifications its owners had handed him days before.

The doubters wanted to wait for the agency's report. "We could wait," he said, "for the people who are keeping secrets from us to decide what we may know. Or we could act on what is already plain." He let the silence draw on until no one could ignore it, and seemed to be the only one in the room able to tell them what lay under Mars. One by one they gave way. He had brought them knowledge none of them had, and none of them asked what it had cost him or whom it served. The order passed: deeper digging, faster, with the company's machines. A public mission had been bent to fill a private ledger, and he kept his face grave while the clerk read it into the record. As the members filed out, he was already composing in his head the message that would tell the owners their machines were going to Mars, and that the licence they had paid for was one step closer.

Chairs scraped and the murmur of conversation drifted out into the corridor. The clerk gathered her papers and switched off her desk lamp. Behind the empty chair the screen still glowed, showing the scar in the red dust, the small machines at its edge, and the stone that no one in the room had ever seen.

In the weeks that followed, the corridor outside the committee room _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8f48dd3e-34f8-4e51-a872-627347c11a3e">took on a new kind of traffic, and more and more of it seemed to slow as it passed the politician, who stood by the tall windows with his phone still warm from his last call to Varn.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="c6ee0a50-6d2a-454c-9a0d-481bdc641ee0" -->

Night had come down over the basin, and the cold with it, but the pit at its bottom was lit as bright as noon. Floodlamps on tall masts threw a hard white circle across the red dust, and in that circle the excavators worked without rest: six squat machines on jointed legs, their arms rising and falling, their drill heads whining down into the rock and their buckets lifting the spoil and tipping it onto a ridge that grew with every hour. The thin wind pulled the finest grit off the ridge and carried it away in long pale ribbons. Nothing else moved on that plain. At the floor of the pit, under a skin of stone that had kept it hidden longer than there had been rivers on Earth, lay the thing the machines were cutting toward. It did not move either. The fresh wound in its side, where a drill bit had gone through, was all that showed it had felt them at all. Out of that stillness, a moment earlier, it had sent a call.

The answer came back faster than light could have carried it, and it came in the organism's own tongue: not words but a shaped pressure of meaning, folded into the call so that nothing in it could be taken two ways. *State the nature of the emergency.* The organism understood it entirely and at once, the way a hand understands its own fingers. Between it and its kind there had never been a gap to guess across. Every sign had one reading, and both ends of the link held it.

So it gave them what it knew, and what it knew was only what it had read. The narrow eye that had passed overhead and swept it with light was, in the only terms it had, a search. The machines that came down and bit into the rock around it, patient and blind and never pausing, were a hunt that had found its quarry. The bright cut through its shell was the first blow. No word from the ships above had ever reached it, and it could not have parsed one if it had; no sign of theirs had ever come to it in a form it could hold. It put the whole of its reading into the clean shared signal, and the signal stripped away every doubt it had never had: *Discovered. Attacked.* On the other end there was no pause to wonder what the strangers had meant, because no one there had seen them, only this, and it arrived as plain fact. *Remain where you are,* the answer folded back, just as plain. *We are coming.*

The link went quiet, and the organism did as it was told. It drew itself in behind its shell and lay still in the stone, and above it the floodlamps burned on and the drills went on singing. A bucket bit into the rock a hand's breadth from the wound, lifted, swung, and tipped its load onto the ridge. The machines had no orders to stop and no means to wonder what they were digging into. The wind combed the dust off the spoil and sent it drifting out across the dark basin, and the six arms rose and fell in the white light.

High above the basin, where the laboratory ship and its small escort kept their slow watch over the dig, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="baf5d260-62b0-48b8-91d6-2fc57dce264c">the sensor screens, which for weeks had shown nothing but rock, dust and the steady crawl of the excavators, began to flicker with returns from the far, empty reaches of Mars's orbit.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="5164634a-39c2-453c-8563-2ca968c0c343" -->

The house stood at the end of a quiet street, a narrow two-storey place with a crooked gate and a kitchen window that faced the road. A little after nine in the morning a dark car pulled up in front of it, and a man in a good coat got out with a paper bag of groceries under one arm. He stood on the step a moment before he knocked, as if he were not sure of the house. Then the door opened and a girl of ten or eleven looked up at him. She looked past him at the car, and back at him, and let him in. Across the street and three houses down, the detective wrote the time in his notebook: 9:12. He had been parked there since before it was light, the engine off and a thermos going cold on the passenger seat. On his lap lay a camera with the long lens and a notebook ruled into columns for time, door and person. He photographed the man on the step and the car's plate. He checked the plate against the one he had noted outside a restaurant weeks before, though he already knew the face. He could have left then. One frame of the politician at Captain Varn's door would have satisfied his client, and no one would have asked what came after. He wrote the time down instead and stayed. Through the morning he logged every movement he could see. The kitchen light came on at 10:40, and two figures stood at the counter. A little later smoke clouded the window, and the man scraped a pan into the bin and started again. The detective logged both attempts. At noon the back door opened and the girl dragged a chair into the yard, and the man followed with two plates. He had come expecting to catch the politician at something he could sell, and by noon he had nothing of the kind. In the yard the man knelt in the grass to tie the girl's shoe. Then he sat on the step while she talked, and listened the whole time without once checking his phone. The detective looked for the show in it, the glance over the shoulder to see who was watching, the kindness done for a camera, and did not find it. He did not leave for lunch. He did not leave when the afternoon went slack and the street emptied, and nothing in the yard looked like anything a client would pay for. At two the girl came out of the front door in a coat buttoned wrong. The man crouched on the path and did the buttons over again before he walked her to the corner shop. He came back with a carton of milk and her bag of sweets as well, and the detective noted that too, and the brand. He lifted the camera, framed the two of them coming up the street, and lowered it again without pressing the shutter. He knew what a picture like that would become in his client's hands: proof of a man tied to the mafia worming his way into the household. He also knew that what he was looking at was not that. The photographs already in his folder, the restaurant, the doors of the mining company, said this was a man who sold himself. The detective had taken them knowing what they would be used to say. He had been paid for that, and paid well, and he did not like how little that settled now. Whatever his client wanted, the detective did not write anything he had not seen. At four he noted that the girl had taken the man's hand to pull him toward the front room, and that the man had let her. He underlined it, and then sat a long while looking at the line before he went on.

Through the front window he saw her point at the photographs on the wall, one after another, and talk. He could not hear her, so he followed her hands, and the man's face as it followed them, rather than trust his first guess. He saw the man go quiet and set his hand on her shoulder. He took the shots, then lowered the camera and watched with his own eyes, because the lens cropped too much. Later the girl fell asleep against the man on the sofa, and the man sat without moving so as not to wake her. The detective timed it: forty minutes, until the light began to go and the man woke her. At dusk it began to rain, and through the kitchen window he watched the man make her supper, testing the soup before he set the bowl in front of her. The car pulled away at 6:51. The detective stayed on in the dark and went back through the notebook, entry against entry, the way he checked any account before he trusted it. It held more lines than all the evenings of dinners put together. The restaurant photographs had shown a man leaning across a table to a woman. What he had written down that day showed something those frames had never caught. Under the times and places he wrote a line he had no client to give to: *careful with her. Not for anyone's benefit.* He looked at the words until the ink seemed to sit differently on the page. A man could be crooked in parliament and still be decent at a kitchen table, he told himself. A man could be the one his client was paying him to ruin and still be the only one in this case doing right by the child. He sat with the camera in his lap, weighing what he had been hired to find against what he had seen, and finding he could no longer pretend they were the same. He marked the time the porch light went off, and only then did he start the car.

The politician had come to the house meaning to make himself useful in a way Varn would hear about, a kindness he could mention on the next call and bank like any other favour. He kept that purpose in front of him through the morning. He kept it while he burned the first pancakes, and while he made the second batch properly because the girl had watched him scrape the first into the bin. But somewhere between holding the ladder while she fetched a ball off the shed roof and fixing the strap on her backpack, the reckoning in his head went quiet. Nobody was watching the knot he retied three times until it held. Nobody would report that he let her win at cards and then stopped letting her, because she had caught him doing it and looked insulted. The care was still owed to his scheme, but it had begun to answer only to her. Out in the yard she abandoned her chair to eat her lunch on the bench beside him, pressed against his side. She talked with her mouth full about the things she would tell her mother that night, saving them up one by one like coins. "She calls every day," she said, "at seven, even when it's late up there." In the afternoon she took his hand without asking and led him from room to room, as if he were a guest who had to be shown everything and might not find it on his own. He let her lead him and did not think once about what it was worth. Everywhere she led him there was Varn. Varn on the hallway wall in a flight suit too big for her, squinting at a camera on some cadet field. Varn taped to the kitchen door with flour to her elbows and a birthday cake leaning badly to one side. In the front room hung a whole wall of her. Varn stood on a beach with a baby on her hip, and held the girl up to see over a crowd, and slept on the sofa with the girl, much smaller, asleep on her chest. The politician had sat across from this woman at a dozen dinners, smiling the smiles he had practised and steering her toward Mars with questions she never seemed to notice. He had thought he knew her face. He had not known it could look like this, unguarded and laughing, a woman no one was watching. To every photograph the girl attached a story, told with the absolute certainty of a child who has never once been let down. In each one her mother was the hero without ever trying to be. There was the time she taught her to ride a bike on this very street, and the time she came home from a long posting and slept for a whole day. She had stayed up three nights straight when the fever came. She had learned to braid hair from a video and got it wrong for a month, and she sang badly on purpose so the girl would laugh. He listened the way he listened in committee, for anything that might be used, and found nothing he could sell. The stories went on while he cut her an apple and found the right cup and checked her homework at the kitchen table. He held one frame longer than he needed to, and once he stopped her and asked her to tell a story again. With each one, the woman he had courted for what she knew slipped further away, and another took her place. This one was tired and stubborn and kind. She had told him plainly over their first dinner that she had no room in her life for romance. He understood now why, and what that room was full of.

Later the girl fell asleep against him on the front-room sofa, beneath the wall of photographs, curled just as she lay on her mother in the frame above them. He sat without moving so as not to wake her, her small weight warm on his arm. It was then that he remembered the hallway of the house he had grown up in, and the telephone on its narrow table that no one ever rang at seven. He remembered himself, younger than she was now, sitting beside it with a story saved up until it went stale. In the open, unguarded way she said *my mom always*, he heard what he had wanted then. That was before he learned that every kindness had a price and every promise was a lever. For years he had told himself that he had outgrown wanting anyone's voice at the end of the day, that a full room was company enough. Now, with the girl breathing slowly against him and Varn smiling down from every frame, he caught himself listening for the call too. He wanted, more than he could have said aloud, to be someone they would save things up for. For the first time since he had met her, he wished Varn were not so far away, for a reason that had nothing to do with what she could tell him. The man who had bought his way to that door felt smaller than the one who had spent the day keeping a child fed, safe and listened to. When the light outside began to go, he woke her, made her supper and washed the plates. He checked that she knew where the spare key was, then stood a moment in the doorway watching her settle at the kitchen table, and left before the call came.

That evening the girl sat close to the screen in the kitchen, her knees drawn up on the chair, and asked whether the thing under Mars could hurt anyone. On the other end of the call, Varn did not answer at once. She leaned nearer to her camera, so that her face filled the screen and nothing of the ship behind her showed. She smiled the way she smiled at bedtime when a storm rattled the windows. "No one's in any danger," she said. "It's a very old rock, and a lot of very careful people are looking at it. That's all." Her daughter asked how she could know, if no one knew what it was. Varn did not tell her that she had been wondering the same thing all day. She told her instead that the scientists checked everything twice, and that her corvette was right there watching over them. The only thing her girl needed to think about tonight, she said, was whether she had finished her homework. The girl said she had, mostly. Varn made her name the part she hadn't. Then she listened while she promised to do it before school, as if the homework were the one thing in the solar system that mattered. "You don't have to worry about Mars," she said. "Worrying is my job. Yours is sleeping and eating something green and telling me tomorrow what you learned." She waited until her daughter laughed. Only then did she ask whether the doors were locked and the lights in the hall left on, the way they always left them. When the girl yawned, Varn told her to go to bed. She stayed on the line until she heard the bedroom door close, and she kept her smile on long after there was no one left in front of the screen to see it.

Two days later, the first thing to break the quiet of Varn's watch aboard the corvette _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="fe5e35f6-7834-45d5-8f7d-9b6aaba94004">was a soft chime from the long-range sensors, and Varn's eyes went to the far edge of the screen, out where the dark around Mars had always been empty.</li>
<li edge="495e4e72-9a07-4e10-8a94-24aef9132427">was the chime of a call from Earth: the politician, ringing for the first time since he had spent the day at her house looking after her daughter.</li>
<li edge="fceb5632-5ffe-4499-929b-638eba75e5f8">would begin its slow crossing from a cramped office on Earth, where the detective sat late over his desk, the photographs he had taken of her spread before him, and the ship's address written on a scrap of paper beside his screen.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="2a09dc83-7a8d-48e3-9d2c-af2780749537" -->

The courtroom was one of the small ones on the third floor, where family matters were heard. It had pale wood benches, a flag that hung without moving, and windows too high to show anything but a square of grey afternoon. A clerk read out the case number and the names, and the judge, a woman with reading glasses pushed into her hair, nodded toward the petitioner's table. At the other table sat a junior attorney from a legal aid office with a thin folder in front of him, and beside him one chair stood empty.

The lawyer rose before the court and defined a mother by what this one was not: present. "Captain Varn," she said, "is at this hour circling Mars aboard a warship, on a mission no one can promise she will return from. A child cannot be raised by a voice on a delayed line." She laid the photographs on the table one by one: Varn at dinner with a man from the committee, then the same man at the doors of a mining company the newspapers tied to the mafia. Then she asked the court what kind of home a girl came back to when her mother kept such company. She spoke of the danger, the distance and the dinners. She did not speak of who had woken the girl for school these past years, who had packed her lunches, sat up through her fevers, or answered her questions at the kitchen table. No one had asked about any of that, and none of it was in the file.

Behind her sat the father, the man the petition called the stable parent. His claim rested on blood and on an address that did not change. He wore a pressed suit and kept his hands folded, and he watched the photographs pass to the bench rather than the judge. The lawyer presented him as the home the girl was missing, though her papers said nothing of when he had last tucked his daughter in, or whether he knew the name of her teacher. The court heard a careful account of where the mother was and whom she had eaten with. It heard nothing of the child those years had raised: how tall she had grown, what she feared, whom she ran to. The brief measured the woman by the empty chair in her house and the man across her table, and never by the girl who had grown up in her care.

The judge turned the last photograph face down and made a note. "Counsel for the respondent?" The young attorney stood and said that his client was reachable only by relay and asked for time to put the petition to her. The judge granted it without looking up. She set the date for a first hearing and had the clerk enter it. The father leaned forward to say something to his lawyer, and she answered him with a small shake of the head as she gathered the photographs back into their envelope. Outside the high windows the grey square had darkened, and the clerk was already calling the next case.

On the morning set for the first hearing, the same small courtroom on the third floor _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="6707810e-3776-4402-b17f-f2d07b04a8c3">had a screen wheeled in beside the witness stand, and a technician was still bent over its cables, trying to raise the link to Captain Varn's ship at Mars while the lawyer laid out her papers and the judge took her seat.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="cc36be6c-82d3-4788-a003-9d13b4e49eae" -->

The committee room sat at the end of a long corridor on the third floor of the parliament building, where the carpet was worn pale down the middle and the portraits of former chairmen watched from between tall windows. Between sessions the corridor filled with aides carrying folders, with members on their phones, and with low talk that stopped whenever someone passed too close. Along one wall stood a row of leather benches that no one sat on for long, and at the far end a coffee urn ticked as it cooled.

In that corridor the members who could get nothing from the agency began to come to the politician. They came one at a time, between sessions, and asked him quietly what was really under the ice on Mars. He had what they lacked: what Captain Varn told him on her calls, which he had never put before the committee. He did not hand it over. He measured it out.

"The agency briefs us like we're the press," said a heavy man from the eastern districts, stirring coffee he did not drink. "Is it worth the money, this deeper dig?"

"The samples carry materials no one has ever seen in that quantity," the politician said, and let the words settle before he went on. "Of course, a find like that needs the right hands on it. A licence that's settled is a licence that doesn't leak."

To the member who feared being caught out by the press he gave the agency's silence instead, and let her believe she would be the first to know when it broke. Each time he finished, he turned the talk to the mining licence, and to how much simpler everything would be once the rights were in safe hands. Each time he left the member feeling that the next piece of news depended on how the vote went.

By the end of the week the tally had changed. Members who would not commit until they knew what had been found now committed without knowing it. They had borrowed their certainty from him, and they paid for it with their names on the company's bid. The politician counted the majority in his office with the door shut and the blinds half drawn against the evening. He said nothing to the committee about where his knowledge came from. He said nothing to Varn about where it went. Before he left, he passed the new figures to the company's owners the same way he passed them everything else, as a service already paid for.

Down the corridor the committee room stood dark, its long table cleared, the name cards stacked in a box by the door. A cleaner pushed her cart past the empty benches and switched off the coffee urn.

The call from the company's owners came early the next morning, and by the time the politician stepped out of the lift onto their floor, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="fd70ff4f-22b1-4d6f-90e6-104971076b73">the owners were already waiting for him in the glass-walled boardroom, a photograph of Varn's corvette laid out on the table between them.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="0a53561e-5dac-4660-8f48-c9f99648e770" -->

The call came through on the corvette's private channel a little after the ship's midnight, and Captain Varn took it in the narrow alcove behind the bridge, where the hum of the engines was loudest and no one could hear her. The link opened with its usual lag. His face filled the small screen, grainy with the distance, and hers would be reaching him a moment after her voice: tired around the eyes, lit by the blue of the consoles spilling in from the bridge, Mars a rust-coloured curve in the port behind her. He had always had an opening ready for her: the light remark, the compliment held back just long enough to seem unplanned, the question meant to leave her talking. Tonight he did not use it. She pushed a loose strand of hair behind her ear and said it was late on the ship and that she had nearly not answered, and he said, quietly and before he could have weighed it, that he was glad she had. The words came plainer than anything he had said to her before, and they crossed all that distance with nothing behind them. When the signal stuttered and froze her mid-word, he leaned toward the screen as if he could close the gap, and when it caught up he let out a breath he seemed to have been holding. She asked whether he was all right; he sounded different, she said. He started to give her the easy answer and stopped. He told her the only truth he had: that he did not know what it was, only that he had been waiting all day for this call, and that he could not remember when he had last waited for anything. Varn looked at him for a long second through the delay, then smiled, slowly, the guarded smile she had worn at their first dinners giving way to something softer. For a moment neither of them said anything more. At the edge of the frame, by his elbow, lay a pad with his questions written out on it, about the object and about what the agency was keeping quiet, each one worded to sound like nothing more than a man taking an interest in her work. He did not look at the pad. When he went on, his voice stayed slow, nothing like the easy voice she knew from the restaurant tables, the one that had always seemed to be keeping something back. He began to tell her about her daughter: how the girl had walked him through every photograph in the hallway, how she had told him which of her mother's medals was for what, and got half of them wrong, and how she had saved him the last of the biscuits because her mother always did. His voice caught on that, and he stopped, plainly surprised at himself. Varn laughed and asked whether he had eaten them. He said he had, and that they were stale, and that he would not have traded them for anything. Then he said he had meant to have something clever ready when she answered, and stopped again, as if admitting it had surprised him more than her. Varn told him he sounded like a man who had forgotten his lines. He did not smile back. He said that maybe he had, and that maybe he did not want them back. Between them lay a hundred million kilometres, a delay that made every pause twice as long, and every reason she had once given him for keeping her distance. She found she was leaning toward the screen anyway.

He asked how she was, and when she said fine, he asked again, and waited out the long lag to listen to the whole of her answer without once steering her: the long watches, the stars that did not move however long she looked at them, the hum of the hull, how the dark outside never changed. The questions stayed on the pad. Twice he drew breath as if to turn the talk toward the excavation, and twice he let it go and asked her instead whether she was sleeping, whether the corvette's food was as bad as she had said, whether she wanted him to go back to the house on the weekend. When Varn said yes, he did not answer at once, and when he did, he spoke quietly and without any of the easy charm he usually wore for her. He said he would like that. He said it as though he had not planned to, and as though it was the truest thing he had told her since the night they met. Once he laughed at something she said, and the laugh broke off, and he rubbed his eyes and said he was only tired. When she said she ought to sleep, he said yes, of course. Then he asked whether she would call tomorrow, and the next day, and he waited through the delay for her to say she would. She said it, and his shoulders came down. Neither of them ended the call. For a while they only sat with the line open, the static between Mars and Earth hissing faintly, the pad still lying beside him with every line on it unasked, and he kept his hand near the screen as though he might touch it.

Behind her, an alarm chimed once on the bridge, then again. Varn told him to wait and stepped out, the call still open in her lowered hand. The contacts were coming up on the long-range screens all at once, a scatter of bright returns on the far orbit around Mars where a moment before there had been nothing but empty dark and the slow drift of the planet's shadow. The returns held steady and did not fade the way a sensor ghost would. There were many of them, spread wide against the curve of the planet, and they were moving. She had the sensors run again, and the second sweep showed the same picture: ships far out, well beyond the laboratory ship and her own corvette, with hulls that matched no design she knew. Varn stood in front of the screens and studied the bright marks hanging over Mars, while the bridge went quiet around her.

The laboratory ship had caught them within the same second as the corvette, and its readouts filled with numbers: positions, masses, heat, the faint bend of light around hulls no one had a name for. The escort's tracking crew read the figures aloud to one another and found that each answered only *where* and *how large*, never *why*. The hulls had no ports, no markings, no lights that blinked in any pattern a human eye could take for a greeting or a warning. One officer said they were holding still. Another said that holding still might be how such ships made ready, or how they waited, or how they meant no harm at all, and no one could tell her which. A shift in one ship's heat might have been an engine, a weapon, or something without a human word; the spacing of the fleet might have been a formation, a courtesy, or chance. On the laboratory ship the scientists bent over the scans, searching for anything shaped like intent, and on the corvette the crew kept the strangers on every screen, perfectly seen and not understood at all. Into that silence each of them began, without meaning to, to read what they feared.

Varn lifted the handset. On the small screen his face was still waiting, frozen for a heartbeat by the delay before it moved again, and she watched him ask what was wrong without hearing the question yet. She told him she had to go. The words took their time to reach him, and when they did he only nodded, and said her name the way no one had said it in years, as though it were something to be held carefully. She told him she would call him back. She did not know if it was true, and she could see in his face that he knew she did not know, and that he would wait for the call all the same. He said goodbye, and then said it again. The image held a moment longer than the voice did, and his face did not move until the picture went dark.

On Earth, the news of the strange ships around Mars travelled fast, and before long _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="43cfa2dc-1c49-4629-918c-9e6c3c4d39ca">the first transmission was already crossing the void toward the only two ships Earth had above Mars: the laboratory ship, and the lone, lightly armed corvette sent to escort it, both waiting for word on what they were to do.</li>
<li edge="de9fc47e-0ec5-4f0e-aa5d-860d0a8c1f5c">a message was racing back across the void toward Mars, addressed to Captain Varn, who sat on the bridge of her corvette watching the alien ships on her screens.</li>
<li edge="25d9cba0-e7c2-4d5e-80c0-bbc58719ee79">the politician was summoned to the mining company's offices, where the owners sat waiting for him with new orders concerning Captain Varn and the thing still buried on Mars.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="2141e049-4f34-44c7-9b86-8d71fc383b7c" -->

The office was on the third floor above a laundromat, and the warmth of the dryers came up through the floorboards on cold evenings. The blinds were half drawn against the streetlight, and the only other light was the desk lamp, its circle falling on a cup of coffee gone cold hours ago. Before he let himself decide anything, the detective went back through the file page by page. He laid his logbook open beside the prints and matched each photograph to its timestamp, and each timestamp to the line he had written that night: the restaurant, the corner table, the hour she arrived, the hour she left, the one glass of wine, the walk home. Nine evenings, and every one of them ended at her own door, alone. He did not trust his memory of it. He checked the receipts he had kept from the cab rides, and the notes he had made in the car while she was still inside. Beside them lay his notes on the mining company's offices, and a list of what his client had told him. He had written that list out and left every line unticked, because none of it had been checked. Every source he had was someone watching Varn or someone talking about her. He had never once heard her own account of anything, and a report built only from the outside, he knew, was half a report, however neat it looked. No one who had hired him would ever ask why he had not gone to her.

The client's retainer was still clipped inside the folder, and the photographs of the captain lay spread beside it: Varn at the restaurant table, Varn on her doorstep, Varn walking home alone. He had been paid to make her look like something. He looked at the pictures a long time. Then he pushed them aside and opened a message to her ship. He drafted it three times. He took out a sentence that might have sounded like an accusation, then one that promised more than he could stand behind. He deleted a line that softened what he had done, and wrote it again plainly. In the end he kept it short. He was a private investigator, and he did not hide who had hired him or what he had been hired to find. He had taken her picture without her knowing, and he had been looking into matters that touched her and her daughter. He no longer believed what he had been paid to believe about her.

He knew what the message was worth to him: nothing, or less than nothing. The man paying him would not keep paying a detective who wrote to the other side, and a captain on a ship at Mars had no reason to answer a stranger who had been following her. He wrote it anyway. He asked whether she would meet him when she could, at whatever time and over whatever link her duty allowed. He wanted to hear from her, in her own words, what he had so far only watched through a lens. He looked up the agency's public relay schedule and worked out the light delay to Mars for the week. He found the right format for a civilian message to a ship on mission, so that what he sent would not sit unread in some queue. He checked the routing address for her ship against two separate listings before he trusted it, and read the message through once more, word by word. He did not let himself read it a second time. He sent it out across the distance to her ship. Then, in the file, under a new heading, he wrote the date and the time, and left space beneath for her answer.

Below him a dryer buzzed and went still. He sat in the lamplight with the folder closed and the coffee untouched.

Her answer, if it came at all, would be days crossing back from Mars, and in the morning, rather than sit waiting by the lamp, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="7e0626ca-cb2b-4048-9e01-85d07c58095a">he drove across town to the school where Varn's daughter took her lessons, the names of her teachers, and of the neighbours on Varn's street, copied onto the back of an envelope in his pocket.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="c7019cdb-59b4-40cf-ba7b-c43d6f89ce18" -->

The courtroom was on the third floor of the old municipal building. It was a narrow room with high windows, and the light came in grey and flat over the benches. Someone had wheeled a large screen in on a trolley and set it where a witness stand would have stood, its cables taped down across the floorboards to a console at the clerk's desk. The judge sat beneath a faded crest, with a stack of papers squared in front of her. The lawyer stood at the first table in a dark suit. Beside her sat the ex-husband, his coat still buttoned. The other table had a single chair, and it was empty. Before the hearing began, the clerk read into the record that the respondent would appear by link from her vessel in orbit around Mars, and that each exchange would carry a delay of some minutes in each direction. The judge nodded and asked for the link to be opened.

The lawyer's first question was who put the girl to bed at night. The screen at the front of the courtroom showed Captain Varn listening to nothing yet, her face lit by the panels of her ship, while the words crossed the dark toward her. The judge waited a minute, then two, then told the lawyer to go on. The next questions were who signed the girl's school reports, who took her to the dentist, and who would sit with her if she woke sick in the night with her mother past the Moon. Only then did Varn's first answer arrive, to a question the room had already left behind. She described the bedtime as it had been for eleven years: the story read twice because the girl always asked for it twice, the lamp left on in the hallway, the rule that worries were said out loud before sleep so they would not be carried into it. The ex-husband looked at his hands. The lawyer said, mildly, that the court had asked about tonight, not about eleven years ago, and the judge wrote something down.

It went on like that. Each time Varn spoke about what she had done for her daughter, the answer landed late and sideways. She described the homework checked at the kitchen table, the swimming lessons she had driven to in uniform, the neighbour she had chosen with care and the man she had asked to look in on the girl. But by the time each answer arrived, a new accusation had been laid over the old one. She sounded like a woman talking around the question rather than a mother who had answered it fully and then waited, alone in orbit, for the court to hear her. When the lawyer asked whether she could say, simply, who was raising the child at this moment, the screen held Varn's still face for four long minutes. Then her voice came through, steady and too late: "I call her every day. She tells me what she ate and what she learned and who was unkind to her, and I tell her what to do about it." The judge thanked her, and noted that the captain had needed some time to find her answer.

The clerk glanced at the console, where a small counter ticked off the seconds of the delay, and then at the judge. Outside, a tram passed in the street below, and the high windows rattled faintly in their frames. On the screen, the panels of the ship flickered blue and amber across Varn's face. Behind her, through a small round port, the curve of a red planet hung motionless against the black.

The lawyer waited until the windows had stopped rattling, then turned away from the screen to face the bench, and _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="750b8823-44dd-4838-901d-a782ca0568bc">and let her gaze rest for a moment on the empty chair beside the defence table, where the girl's mother should have been sitting, before she began.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="5738017a-f223-456b-95e6-bd5964e16b1d" -->

The company's offices took up the top floor of a glass tower above the river. The afternoon light fell across a long room so bare that it seemed no one worked there at all: a desk of black stone, two leather chairs behind it, and on the wall a map of Mars, its southern plains shaded in the company's colours as though the planet had already been divided.

The owners did not rise when the politician came in, and no one offered him a chair. He stood before their desk the way the company's site managers stood there at the end of a quarter. The elder of them slid a folder across to him. It held the committee's minutes, a copy that should never have left parliament, with his own speeches on the excavation marked in red where they had pleased the company and in blue where they had not gone far enough. Without raising their voices, they reminded him which campaigns they had paid for, which seat they had bought him and which committee they had placed him on. They said it as plainly as an employer reads a man his contract. The licence was close, they told him, but close was worth nothing to them if the agency found out first what lay in the rock and decided it belonged to the public. So he would press the captain. On his next call he was to keep her talking about the object and steer her toward the scientists. The mission would scan it, sample it and dig around it until whatever it was had been brought up on machines the company supplied and entered in records the company could claim. They wanted it theirs before the committee could vote, before the agency could seal it, and before anyone in government thought to ask whose interest the digging served. Whatever she told him was to come here first, they said, before it reached any member of the committee. The politician nodded and wrote the instructions down in the same notebook he carried into the parliament chamber.

His pen stopped halfway down the page. He had carried a hundred such orders out of this room without a second thought, but this one had her name in it. He thought of how she had told him, across a restaurant table, that she was not looking for romance, and of how the calls had grown longer anyway. Between her and him stood this desk, these men, and everything he had promised them when he first smiled at her in the command center for their sake. He did not answer the owners at once. He was thinking of last night's call: her voice tired and warm across the delay, asking whether he had remembered to eat, telling him what the stars looked like from the corvette's bridge, her laugh at something he had said arriving a whole breath after he had said it, her promise to call again tomorrow at the same hour, the way she always did. He had come to plan his evenings around those calls. He had begun, without deciding to, to say *when you're back* instead of *if*, and she had begun to say it too. Closer to the object meant nearer the dig, longer shifts, and calls cut short or missed. What he could not stop picturing was not the loss of what she knew. It was a night when the hour came and the screen stayed dark.

He did not nod. For the first time in that room, he leaned forward. "She's an escort captain," he said carefully, "not a scientist. She's on a light escort ship, not a research vessel, and no one knows what that thing is. If I push her toward it, she goes closer to it, and she goes because I asked. I don't want her any nearer to it than she already is." The younger owner looked up at that, and the elder said nothing at all. The politician heard his own words in the silence. Too late, he understood that he had spoken neither for the company nor for the committee, but for a woman on the far side of the sky who trusted him. He had shared dinners and favours with these men for years, and not one of them would have noticed if he stopped coming. Standing before them, he understood that the only person who asked how his day had gone was a woman millions of kilometres away. He also understood that he had just asked them to keep her safe, the way she had asked him to keep her daughter safe. He closed the notebook. Neither of them had told him he could go, and he stayed where he was, standing between the desk and the map. For once, he did not know which way he would step.

The elder owner laid it out again across the black stone, as if it were settled. The politician would call her tonight and tell her the committee was anxious. He would ask her to press the scientists for closer work on the thing in the ground, nearer the surface, sooner. A month earlier he would have nodded and reached for his coat, already shaping the words he would use on her and the warmth he would put into them. Now he did not move. He heard himself say that they did not know what the object was, that no one did, and that Varn had told him so in a voice he had not heard her use before. He tried to speak of caution and of the mission's safety, as if the worry were only about the venture. All the while he meant one ship and the woman who commanded it, and every word was a step toward her and away from the men who owned him. They looked at him the way men look at a tool that has begun to turn in the hand. He knew what he owed them, the career they had paid for and the votes they had bought him, and he did not pretend otherwise. But he told them that he would not lean on her to go nearer something that might be dangerous, not on their schedule and not for their licence. He said it plainly, without the smile he used in committee. It came out halting, but it came out, and he did not take it back when the silence stretched. At his sides his hands were not steady, and he let them stay that way, because he found that he could not make himself say yes.

The next time he had to choose between what he owed the company and what he felt for her, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="fc67428f-78ef-437b-acb5-3b162b0ed2b2">it was in the committee room of parliament, on the morning the alien ships were reported around Mars, where half the members were already calling for the excavation to be halted and every face had turned to him, the one man who seemed to know what was happening up there.</li>
<li edge="c2bbc7c4-a301-4caa-bcd8-0f0785a8abd3">there was nothing left to weigh, for the alien fleet was closing on Mars, and the call he had placed to Varn's corvette was ringing through what might be the last minutes it had.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="98e7d1b0-0efa-4d34-babc-d3c07a5bde8a" -->

The bridge of the corvette was small enough that Captain Varn could see every station from her chair. Below the forward screen Mars turned slowly, rust and shadow, and somewhere on its face the excavators were still cutting into the rock. The laboratory ship hung a few kilometres off, its running lights steady. Far out, on the edge of the tactical display, a scatter of contacts held their slow approach. They had appeared only minutes before, and already there were more of them than the display had been built to label. The comms officer looked up. "Priority traffic from command, Captain." Varn rose and crossed to her station to read it standing.

The order came up from Earth over the long relay. It used the only words Earth had for ships it could not read: *unidentified vessels, presumed hostile, intent toward the discovery*. No one at command had heard a word from the fleet. All they had were dots on a screen, vectors bending toward Mars, and their own doctrine to explain them. In every history the command staff had studied, ships that came uninvited to a place where something valuable lay buried had come to take it. So they wrote the fleet's purpose for it, *threat to Earth's find*, and sent that purpose out to the two ships in orbit as if it were a fact they had learned rather than a guess they had made. On the laboratory ship the scientists read it on their consoles. On the escort Varn's crew read it on theirs. The final line asked of them only the thing Earth understood: *Protect the discovery.*

There were two ships to obey it. One was a laboratory that carried instruments, not guns. The other was a single corvette, lightly armed, sent to escort a dig, not to meet a fleet. Its weapons were the only message Earth knew how to send the strangers, and they were small. No one aboard could tell whether the far ships would read a raised gun as a warning, a challenge, or nothing at all. The order did not say, because no one on Earth knew. It left the two ships where they were, holding their place above the excavation. To Earth that was guarding. To whatever watched from the far orbit, it might mean something else entirely.

Varn read the order a second time, then a third, as if the words might rearrange themselves into something she could carry out. There was nothing more to it: hold position over the excavation site, keep the laboratory ship safe until further notice. There was no word of reinforcements and no instruction for what to do if the ships kept coming. She looked back at the screens. The contacts were still far out around Mars, but every one of them was larger than anything she had ever escorted. Her weapons officer was already pulling up the corvette's loadout beside the contact count, and out of habit Varn ran the same figures on her own console. A few light guns, enough to warn off debris or a lone smuggler near the belt. Armour that had never been meant for a real fight. Neither of them needed the arithmetic. "Captain," he said quietly, "if even half of those are warships—"

"I know." She cleared the figures from her screen and kept her eyes on the slow, patient drift of the far contacts. One small, weakly armed ship against a fleet. She could stand between those ships and the dig, and she could die there, but if it came to firing she could not stop them. She could only stand where she had been told to stand. "Acknowledge the order and log it," she said. "Hold position over the excavation. Weapons cold until I say otherwise." The comms officer hesitated, then keyed the reply, and the bridge went still around the hum of the instruments while the strangers came on.

As the far contacts crept inward across the tactical display, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="3429c43a-2bc8-4d47-9543-4a120028fac9">the crew on the corvette's bridge leaned closer to their consoles, searching the strange hulls for anything they could read, while Varn ordered the comms channel opened once more.</li>
<li edge="e94e87ee-f5ee-44e2-976c-6812dcb6e900">Captain Varn reached for the comm panel on her corvette's bridge and opened a channel toward the alien ships, her eyes on the speaker as she began to speak.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="9ae66a5c-1eee-44d3-b2df-55e24d1ca304" -->

The committee met that evening in the old wing of parliament, in a long room with a curved table of dark wood and tall windows that had been shuttered against the rain. Aides came and went along the walls with tablets held to their chests. The ventilation hummed. Someone had dimmed the overhead lights so the members could see the wall screen, and no one had thought to bring them back up.

The committee room was half-lit by the feed from Mars, where the alien ships hung as pale smudges on the far orbit. The members who had risen to speak wanted the excavation halted. One of them, a grey-haired woman from the coastal districts, read out the escort's armament: a single corvette under Captain Varn, with point-defence guns and little else. She set the sheet down. "I would like someone to tell me," she said, "what a hole in the ground is worth against a fleet none of us can read." The politician let them finish. In his pocket sat the morning's message from the company's offices, two lines long: *The machines stay in the ground. The licence depends on it.* When he rose, he did not mention the company, its excavators, or the bid that waited three items down the agenda. He spoke of the nation's discovery, and of ground that should not be surrendered to strangers at the first sign of pressure. "We found it," he said. "We dug for it. If we pull back now, we are telling whoever is out there that all they need to do is appear." He spoke of the shame of a mission that ran from what it had found. The phrases had come to him already polished, worded the way the owners worded things, and he gave them out as his own.

During the recess he worked the room as he had worked it for the licence. To the members who had come to him for the agency's findings he spoke quietly, reminding them where those findings had come from and who would keep providing them. To the waverers he described the cost of retreat in contracts and footholds, and in what rival nations would take if Earth stepped back. When the chair called the vote, the order passed: the mission was to protect the discovery, and the digging was to continue. The members calling for caution were outnumbered, and the corvette and its crew were kept where the company needed them. In the gallery a man in a company tie made a short note and left before the session closed, and the politician sat down and straightened his papers, his account settled for another week.

The chair closed the session a little after nine. The grey-haired woman gathered her sheets without looking at him and went out with two others, talking low. Aides switched off the wall screen, and the smudges over Mars went dark with it. The overhead lights came up at last on the empty chairs, the water glasses, and the rain still beating against the shutters.

It was nearly midnight when the politician, alone in his office with his coat still damp from the walk across the courtyard, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="2c20b181-7179-4df5-b3b5-0bd1ebdf21ea">finally got through to Varn's corvette, and waited as her face, pale in the light of the bridge, steadied on the screen across the hours of the day's committee vote he had not yet told her about.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="dca7721d-0b4d-441c-9a5d-689f8a6cdf70" -->

The school stood at the end of a street of low brick houses. In the grey before the first bell its yard was still empty: chalk hopscotch squares faded on the asphalt, a forgotten jumper hung from the railings, and the corridors smelled of floor polish and pencil shavings.

The detective was there before any of the children. He waited in the corridor outside the staff room on a chair built for a child, his notebook open on his knee. A list of names ran down one margin, beside the questions he had written out the night before. He had come to listen the way he had been paid to listen: for the slip, the late pickup, the forgotten lunch, the bruise no one could explain. He took the teachers one at a time, in the gaps between their lessons, and asked each of them the same things in the same order. Who brought the girl in the mornings? Who came to the parents' evenings? Who signed the permission slips? Who answered when the school rang? The form teacher gave him none of what he had been paid to find. She told him that Captain Varn had come to every parents' evening for six years, alone, once straight from a launch briefing still in uniform, and had asked about her daughter's reading before she asked about her marks. "She always wanted to know what the girl was struggling with," the teacher said. "Never what she was best at." He asked for dates. She went to the cabinet and brought back the attendance sheets, and he copied the signatures, six years of them, the same hand each time and no other.

He did not take her word alone. In the office he asked for the pickup log and went through three years of it, page by page. The same signature stood at the same hour, and on launch days a neighbour's name appeared, written in ahead of time in Varn's own hand. He wrote down who had collected the girl in the months her mother was away, and asked whether anyone else had ever come. He checked the emergency contact form: one parent listed, one telephone number, no father. He asked the music teacher and the sports coach separately, though their answers matched the form teacher's, because a thing two people say is not yet a thing three people have seen. The music teacher added that the child shared her crayons without being told and owned up when she broke things.

When the afternoon bell let the children out, he went to the gate and found the women from Varn's street waiting for their own children. He did not take their kindness for evidence. He asked who had looked after the girl when she was ill, and when, and how they knew. He asked whether the father had ever been seen at the house, and wrote the answer down as they gave it: a shake of the head, and a year one of them had to count back to. When one neighbour said the mother had sat up three nights with the girl's fever, he asked the next without telling her why, and got the same three nights. They told him in plainer words what the teachers had told him: the mother had done it all herself, and the girl was the best-mannered child on the street. By the time the last child had gone, every account in his notebook stood against the others, dates against dates, and not one line rested on a single word. He noticed that he had wanted to write all of it down, and that none of it was what his client was paying for.

He sat on the bench by the railings and read the pages through once more, weighing what he had against what he had been hired to find. The man who paid him wanted a careless mother, and a careless mother would have meant an easy fee and a satisfied client. What the detective had in his notebook was a woman who had raised a child alone, and raised her well. The man who wanted the child had not once asked how she was. He knew which of the two had done right by the girl. He closed the notebook on the teachers' words and the neighbours' dates, and did not go looking for anything that would say otherwise. Behind him the caretaker drew the gate shut, and the yard went quiet.

Back in his office that evening, with the notebook still in his coat pocket, the detective pulled his client's file from the drawer and _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="4ffc1f56-a07a-4682-8f07-d0ce7e721c86">laid it open beside the lamp, meaning this time to read past the retainer and the signed contract, to the father's own visits to his daughter, and to the names of the friends he drank with.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="df95f34c-c1fb-469b-8daa-ebe91315ba9c" -->

The family court sat on the fourth floor of the old municipal building, in a room too small for its ceiling. Morning light came through tall windows onto pale wood benches, a clerk's desk stacked with folders, and a raised bench where the judge had already set down her reading glasses and her pen. On the wall beside the witness box hung a dark screen, its corner lit with the small amber word STANDBY. At the petitioner's table Captain Varn's ex-husband sat with his hands folded, in a suit that looked newly pressed. At the respondent's table a young advocate from the agency's legal office turned a page and turned it back. The chair beside him, where Varn would have sat, was empty.

The lawyer rose without notes and spoke of an ordinary evening. A girl of that age, she said, came home from school to a house where someone had to be waiting: someone to ask how the day had gone, to notice when she went quiet, to check her homework and cook her dinner and sit on the edge of her bed when the dark frightened her. Children were not raised by love sent from a distance, she told the court, but by the hundred small things done for them every day: a hand on the forehead in the night, a face at the school gate, the same grown-up there on Monday and still there on Friday. She laid out, item by item, what the girl's days now held: a neighbour's kitchen, a babysitter's schedule, a screen on the table where her mother's voice came through once a day if the link allowed it. Who, she asked, had signed the girl's last permission slip? Who had taken her to the dentist? Who would teach her the things a child learns only by watching an adult live beside her?

She let the questions sit, then turned to the danger. Captain Varn had accepted command of an armed escort, she said, a posting the agency itself rated hazardous, around a planet where no one yet knew what lay in the ground. A parent's first duty was to stay alive long enough to see the work through, to be there not only this year but for every year until the child could stand on her own. A mother who chose a warship over the kitchen table had, however fondly, handed that work to others, and might never come back to take it up again. The girl, the lawyer said, needed someone whose life was not wagered on a mission, someone who would be at home tomorrow and the day after, and the court was being asked only to give her one.

She sat down. For a while the only sound in the room was the judge's pen moving across a legal pad. The father lowered his eyes to the table and nodded once, slowly, as though he had been told something he already knew. The agency's advocate half rose, glanced at the dark screen on the wall, and sat again. Outside, a bus sighed at the curb and pulled away. The judge capped her pen, looked over the empty chair at the respondent's table for a long moment, and asked the advocate whether he wished to answer.

The advocate stood, buttoned his jacket, and had only begun to say that a mother's service was no proof of neglect when the lawyer rose again, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="dfbe00c4-7b1b-4dcb-b8a0-d787076ab381">a thin envelope in her hand, the one Varn's ex-husband had carried into her office, and asked the judge's leave to put something before the court.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="b11ba787-2288-4a18-8f66-90bbc4d91cbc" -->

The ships came on out of the far orbit, and nothing about them told the crews what they were looking at. Their hulls had no seams, no ports, no markings, nothing that could be counted as a bow or a stern, and the shapes turned slowly as they came, so that no one could say which way they faced. The corvette's sensors caught heat blooming along their flanks, then fading, then blooming again, and Captain Varn's officers argued over it in low voices: weapons charging, or engines, or something that was neither, a process with no name in any manual on board. A tactical officer laid the readings over every profile in the threat library and got no match. Another pointed at a pulse of light that crawled along one hull, and no one knew whether it was a targeting sweep, a greeting, or the ship simply breathing. Each pattern seemed to mean something, and none of them meant anything a human could read.

So they hailed. They sent on every band the receivers could reach, in plain speech and then in numbers, prime after prime, then the hydrogen line, then the simple pictures the old probes had carried, a man and a woman and a map of the Sun. The laboratory ship repeated it all on its own frequencies, and the two crews watched the return channels and waited. Nothing came back. The silence could have been refusal, or deafness, or an answer in a form their instruments could not catch, and the crews had no way of knowing which. The alien ships kept closing at the same unhurried pace, and on both bridges people began to read that steadiness as intent, because it was the only meaning they had left.

Varn did not let the channel rest. She took the comms station herself, the officer stepping aside, and every few minutes she leaned to the console and spoke into it, her own voice this time instead of the recorded loop: her name, her ship, that they meant no harm, that they would talk. After it she sent the agency's greeting, the words and then the string of primes and simple geometry meant for anyone who could count, and changed the band, and sent it again. She slowed the signal to a crawl; she pushed it out at full power. Between transmissions the bridge went quiet, and she watched the receiver for any flicker that was not noise, and each time the speakers gave back only the faint hiss of Mars. She logged every attempt with its time and its frequency, and the list grew down the edge of the panel, line after line, each one ending the same way: no reply. "Again," she said, handing the station back, and the communications officer started the sequence over. On the main screen the shapes had grown from points to slivers, and the red ochre of the planet turned beneath them as though nothing were happening at all.

When the private line chimed, she took it in the small cabin behind the bridge and shut the door, though the red glow of the consoles still came through the narrow pane behind her. The politician's face came up grainy and a little late, his office dark around him except for the lamp on his desk. She read him the order the way it had reached her: *protect the discovery, continue excavation.* He knew every word of it. He had argued for those words across the committee table that morning, against the members who wanted the digging halted, and had dressed the company's instructions in the language of national interest until the vote came out the way the owners needed it, so that whatever lay in the Martian rock would still be in their machines' grip when the dust settled. He had not told the committee whose drills those were, or who had called him the night before to say what was expected of him. "Protect it with what?" Varn said, and her voice reached him seconds after her lips had moved. "I have one corvette and four guns that were built for pirates. That is a fleet out there. If it wants the object, I can't stop it. I can't even slow it down. I can only be in the way." The delay held her last words on the screen a moment longer than she spoke them, and in that moment he saw the order for what it was: not a decision made for the mission or for Earth, but a favour bought and paid for long before tonight, a purchase he had delivered, with its price left unpaid until now.

He had always counted the cost of these favours in other people's money, in other members' votes, in a licence that would make the owners richer and keep his career funded. Sitting in his office with her voice coming down from orbit, he understood that the bill had been sent somewhere else entirely. The order that kept the excavators running kept her corvette between the fleet and the dig, and the only thing that would pay for the company's claim on Mars was the ship she stood on and the woman who commanded it. He opened his mouth and found nothing to say. She was waiting for him to tell her whether anyone on the committee might call it off, and he knew better than anyone how that vote had been won, and for whom. "Say something," she said, after the delay had run out twice. Behind the door, the communications officer's voice went on, steady and patient, sending primes into the dark.

By the time the line between orbit and his darkened office went dead, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="1819178c-df4a-4eb6-a648-f3d093f4ae79">Varn had already turned back to her screens. Across the dark from her corvette, aboard the laboratory ship, the scientists sat hunched over their consoles, the samples from Mars on one side and the newest readings of the approaching fleet on the other.</li>
<li edge="a972c2b1-bbae-42d9-bd35-bb2c055f228a">Varn had already turned from the blank screen and was opening a channel to the laboratory ship, where the scientists were hunched over their readings of the approaching fleet, and she meant to make them tell her what it had come for.</li>
<li edge="08354c6b-b2c5-48b8-a882-c709e76511c4">the politician already knew where he would be summoned next, and within the hour he was riding the lift up to the mining company's offices, where the owners were waiting for his report, Varn's words about a corvette that could not stop a fleet still ringing in his ears.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="2ff39e7d-ebcb-480b-a499-cfb5ef6bb8ce" -->

The rain had kept on since evening, and the detective's office smelled of wet coats and cold coffee. A single lamp lit the desk and left the filing cabinets in shadow. The radiator under the window ticked as it cooled, and across the street a pawnshop's neon sign blinked on and off, throwing its red light across the stacks of paper he had spent the night building. He did not trust any of it yet, and he had not stopped at the client's own account of himself. The man's file lay open across the desk: the divorce papers, the visitation schedule the court had granted, the bank statements his client had handed over without a second thought. Against each weekend the schedule gave the father, he set what he could prove of where the man had actually been. There were card payments in other cities, a gym check-in, and a bar tab run up on a Saturday he had sworn he spent with his girl. The family court registry counted three visits in four years. He checked that count against the sign-out sheets the school had faxed him, line by line, matching dates to signatures. Then he read the school's visitor log twice, counted again, and found the father's name there only once. He phoned the registrar a second time to ask whether any visits might have been kept off the books. "None," she told him. "If he came, it's written down." He checked the figures against the payments one more time before he believed them, and wrote them down where he could not argue with them later.

The friends took longer. He worked down the list of names the client had dropped in passing, called each one under a pretext, and let them talk, noting who repeated whom and who had heard it from the man himself. Most were vague, and he did not count vague. One friend's word was gossip, so he kept calling until three of the father's drinking companions, each spoken to alone and asked the same questions in a different order, gave him the same words back. He played the recordings of two of them back to back, and the phrasing nearly matched. He rang the barman last, close to midnight, and asked him to describe the night without prompting. The barman remembered it, and remembered the table, and said the man had been loud. The detective noted the date, held it against the bar tab in his file, and it matched. Only when the dates and the voices agreed did he close the file and sit with what he had found.

The detective laid the two findings side by side on his desk and made himself weigh them as he would weigh any other pair of facts. On one side lay the visiting records: the father had come to see his daughter three times in four years, never for her birthday, never for more than an afternoon. On the other lay the notes from the bar, where the man drank with his friends. He had said it more than once there, and loudly enough to be remembered: the case was not about the girl, it was about making Varn pay. The detective read the line twice. He knew what the client had told him in this office: a worried father who only wanted his child safe at home. He set those words against the empty years and the boast over drinks, and they did not hold.

He sat back and looked at the photographs he had taken: Varn at her restaurant table, Varn walking home alone. He saw them now for what they had been bought to do. A man who wanted his daughter would have asked after her. This one had only asked what would hurt her mother. The fee for the job sat in his account, and more would come if he kept digging the way he had been paid to dig. He did not pretend that changed anything. A man who had not wanted his child had raised this custody suit to wound the woman who had raised her. That was not care, and no client's money made it care. He wrote one word in the margin beside the father's name, *revenge*, and underlined it. The pawnshop sign blinked red across the page. The detective left the lamp burning and did not reach for the phone to call his client.

By the morning of the hearing the rain had stopped, and the detective, who had slept no more than an hour in his chair, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="c685f3e7-908e-4928-a445-631665745967">pulled on yesterday's coat and drove across the city to the court, where he slipped into a seat at the back of the gallery just as the lawyer for Varn's ex-husband rose, a thick folder tucked under her arm.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="71345b3d-f98a-420f-9798-603fb81879aa" -->

The laboratory ship hung above the rust-colored curve of Mars with its lights turned low, so that the screens glowed brighter than the corridors. In the main bay the air smelled of warm electronics and stale coffee, and the benches were cluttered with samples from the excavation, their cases left open and their labels half written. Every console showed the same thing from a different angle: a scatter of shapes on the far orbit, too regular to be rock and too strange to be anything else. Through the forward port the small grey hull of the escort corvette kept station a few kilometres off, its running lights blinking in a steady, patient rhythm. The channel chimed, and Captain Varn's face filled the comm screen above the central table. The scientists crowded into her pickup, tired and unshaven, the disorder of the lab spread out behind them. Her voice came through clipped and level. "What do they want? Whatever you have, I need it."

No one answered her at once. The scientists laid the fleet's sensor returns beside the artifact's mineral scans and read them as they would read rock. The spectra matched line for line: the same rare lattices, the same isotopic ratios, the same strange alloys they had chipped from the thing beneath the Martian surface. Someone said the ships had come for it, and no one argued, because the numbers said the two were kin. But kinship was all the numbers said. They showed what the ships were made of, not what the ships meant. The scientists replayed the corvette's unanswered hails and searched the fleet's emissions for anything that could be a reply: a repeated pulse, a pattern, a change that followed a sign of their own. They found shifts in heat and field strength, and could not tell whether each was a greeting, a warning, or a weapon coming alive.

They turned back to the artifact and fed it the same sweeps, hoping that if they learned to read the thing in the rock they could read the ships that shared its substance. Far below, the excavators kept cutting, because in that laboratory digging was how a question was asked. A woman with her sleeves pushed to the elbows stood over the matched scans and said what the rest were already thinking: they knew where the ships came from, and they had no idea how to say a single word to them. A common origin had given them a common chemistry and nothing else. Each new reading was read in their own terms, by their own instruments, and each guess about what the fleet intended was only a guess about what they would have intended in its place.

"We think they've come for the object," the chief scientist said at last, a thin man with grey stubble, leaning toward the pickup. "The scans match what the excavators brought up. Same materials, same origin, as far as we can tell." Varn did not blink. "Come to take it? To protect it? Something else?" The scientists looked at one another. "We can't tell you, Captain," he said. He spread his hands over the table and its useless, perfect graphs. "Knowing where they come from tells us nothing about what they mean to do, or how to talk to them. We can't even tell you whether they're powering weapons or just getting closer." Varn held his gaze a moment longer. "Is there anything at all I can give my crew?" "We'll keep working," he said. "Whatever we find, you'll have it the moment we do."

"Thank you," Varn said, and the screen went dark. In the bay, the consoles went on glowing over the open sample cases. Across the gap, on the bridge of the corvette, her own screens showed the same scatter of shapes, and the alien ships came on toward Mars.

As the shapes on the far orbit grew larger by the hour, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="9e195f4d-061f-41d5-aa19-8a436ea47aca">the scientists on the laboratory ship turned their eyes back down to the surface of Mars, to the pit where the excavators still stood over the half-buried object.</li>
<li edge="26909aba-22e1-4369-b492-02c3d9b1a947">Captain Varn shut the door of her cabin aboard the corvette, sat down before the small screen, and opened the evening's call to her daughter on Earth.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="88b9b2ac-14bf-4297-985f-6febcbdae9d5" -->

The mining company kept its offices on the top floors of a tower of dark glass above the river, where the noise of the city did not reach. The boardroom had no windows. A long table of black stone ran down its middle, and on the far wall a screen showed a live feed from Mars: a red trench under floodlights, and the jointed arms of the excavators rising and falling without rest. Coffee had been poured and left to cool. Nobody had touched it.

The owners heard him out in the room where they had always given him his orders, and they did not interrupt. He spoke of the corvette at Mars, of how thin its armour was, and of the order he had pushed through the committee to keep it there. When he said Captain Varn's name, it lacked the flat tone he had always used for her, as their window on the agency, a source whose every word he carried to this table. The eldest of them turned a pen between his fingers and looked at the others. They had paid for the man's first campaign and every one after it. They had paid for his seat on the committee that oversaw the agency, and for each vote he steered there. What they had bought was not his judgement or his conscience. It was the certainty that when he sat on that committee, he sat there for them, and that no voter, colleague or captain would stand between his vote and their licence.

Now he was asking them whether the digging could wait, whether the order might be softened, whether the woman in orbit could be pulled back. They let the silence stretch until he heard what he had said.

"Tell me," one of them said, mildly, "whose interest you think you're serving on the committee these days."

"Yours. The same as always."

"Then you'll remember what the licence is worth," said another, "and how much of your career was drawn from the same account."

He gave the answers he had always given, but they no longer took them at face value. A man who worried about one life on the far side of their profits might one day worry about the public he was supposed to represent, and a seat they had paid for was only worth something if it answered to no one but them. Before he left, they had already begun to weigh what he knew, what he could say, and what it would cost to find someone else to sit in his chair.

The door sighed shut behind him. On the wall, the excavators went on cutting into the red rock, their lamps sweeping the walls of the trench. Down the long corridor, past the empty reception desk and the framed photographs of open-pit mines, he walked to the lift alone, and in its mirrored doors his face looked like a stranger's.

By the time he took his seat in the committee room that afternoon, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="c94d3dda-36ed-4d1e-bc58-423e87893859">the other members were already bent over their tablets, murmuring to one another, and more than one of them glanced up at him and quickly away as a message made its way around the table.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="ee150482-572e-4af8-a8ad-34f5e63f8b5a" -->

The courtroom on the fourth floor had been built for larger cases than this one. Its benches ran back in long rows of pale wood, most of them empty, and the morning came in through tall windows and lay in strips across the floor. A clerk sat below the judge's bench and typed. A screen had been wheeled to one side of the witness box, its cable taped down across the carpet. The lawyer for the father rose with a folder in her hands and asked the court's leave to enter new evidence. The judge looked up from her papers and said, "You may proceed." "Thank you, Your Honor." The lawyer laid the photographs out one by one on the table before the bench, and the clerk lifted each onto the screen so the whole room could see. "The court has heard that the respondent is a devoted mother. I would ask the court to consider the company she keeps. The man across the table from her sits on the committee that oversees the space agency."

No one had asked him to come. His work had ended when he handed over the envelope, and the fee had cleared, but the detective took a seat in the back row with his notebook open on his knee, the way he sat at the end of any job, to see where it led. When the first photograph came up on the screen he checked it against his log: the restaurant on the corner, the twelfth of the month, a quarter past eight, the table by the window. The date the lawyer read out was right. So was the next one, and the one after that. He followed each picture onto the screen and found his own handwriting behind it, every time and place exact, every frame his. He had taken them from a parked car across from the restaurant: Varn at a corner table with the politician in his dark suit, the two of them leaning in to talk, a glass of water by her plate, her hands open as she talked. He had been there for every one of those dinners. She had talked about her daughter, paid her half, and gone home alone. Now the lawyer called the man "her companion," and said it twice so the word would stay. Then the clerk put up the last picture, the politician at the doors of the mining company. The lawyer said in one flat sentence what the company was known for, and said nothing more, and let the court join the two images itself. The detective had taken that one too. He had noted the address and handed it over as a job done well, and he had called it a fact. In this room it was being used as a lie, and he could see exactly how. Nothing in either frame was false. The falsehood was in the line the lawyer drew between them, and he had supplied both ends of it.

He turned back through his pages to be sure he had missed nothing. The notes were there in his small, square hand, entry after entry, for the same nights the court was now looking at. *Glass of water, no wine. Talks about daughter most of the meal. Leaves at ten-forty. Taxi home, alone. Lights out by half past eleven.* He had stood across the street until those lights went out each time, because a report that stopped at the restaurant door was half a report. None of those lines had come into the courtroom. He counted the pictures the lawyer had shown and then the frames on his contact sheets, and the ones that had been left out were the ones that showed her stepping into the taxi with no one beside her. He wrote the numbers of the missing frames in the margin and underlined them once. At the front sat Varn's ex-husband, the man who had paid him. He did not look at the screen the way a father might, worried about whom his child's mother kept company with. He watched the judge's face, and when the judge frowned at the company's name, he leaned back. The detective felt his own hands go still on his knees. He had told himself he only pointed the camera, and that what others did with the pictures was their business. Watching them work on the judge, he could no longer believe it. He had been paid to find the truth about a woman. He had found it, and it was good. He had sold it anyway in the shape of a lie, and the money for it was already spent. He did not leave his seat. He stayed to the end and made himself watch every picture, as if he owed the woman on the screen at least that, and he knew that being sorry would not settle the debt. He kept listening, filling the page with what the lawyer said and the line in his log that it ought to have answered.

"Is the company named in these photographs before the court in any other matter?" the judge asked. "Not in this court, Your Honor," the lawyer said. "But its ties to the mafia are a matter of public record. We will submit the relevant press." She went back to the table, took up the restaurant prints, and laid them beside the one of the doorway, and the clerk put them side by side on the screen: the mother at dinner with the man, and the man at the company's doors, the glass, the brass lettering above them, a grey coat, a hand on the handle. The judge looked from one to the other, wrote something down, and asked that the photographs be entered into the record. The clerk typed.

Before the lawyer could reach for the next folder on her table, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="74a974b1-cdd7-4ec9-aa18-9331e1da67bb">the detective had already risen from the back bench and slipped out of the courtroom, and he did not stop until he was back in his office, standing over the file with his client's name on it.</li>
<li edge="fac08b49-6ac7-4efa-aa6e-7fea5e450c82">the judge had lowered her eyes to the screen at her elbow, where a bulletin from Mars had just begun to scroll, and the whispers in the gallery were rising with it.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="1bb87006-fb3b-4332-b895-15ea571ef1f3" -->

The pit lay open on the plain like a wound in the rust, a terraced bowl of cut stone ringed by tracked machines and the thin mast of the relay. Dust lifted from every pass of the treads and hung in the cold, pale air before it settled again on solar panels, on cable runs, on the flanks of the robots themselves. Overhead the small sun gave little warmth. Low on the horizon, where the sky deepened toward violet, a few points of light that were not stars moved slowly against the others.

On the red plain the excavators did not stop. Up in orbit the scientists had decided that the ships closing in on Mars could only be understood through the thing in the rock. If the fleet had come for it, then whatever it was made of and however it was shaped might tell them what the fleet meant. The relay mast crackled with their instructions, flat and hurried: "Widen the bore on the east face. Another core from the seam, as deep as you can get it. We need it up here now." They read the buried object the only way they knew, as a text to be opened. So the drills kept biting into the shell, the cutting arms widened the breach, and the core samples rose on their tethered lifters to the laboratory ship like pages torn out for translation. Each new shaving was logged and set beside the readings from the approaching hulls. The mineral signatures matched, and the scientists took the match for the first line of a conversation, though it told them nothing of how the strangers spoke or what they wanted.

Below, the organism felt only the grinding. The shudder of the rock carried no sign of curiosity and nothing that set study apart from assault. The vibration the humans meant as a question reached its body as one blow, then another, then a third, and each one confirmed what it was telling those coming for it. Every careful measurement taken above to understand the fleet was, to the thing in the ground and to the ships listening for it, the same act repeated: the attack had not ended, and the attackers were still at work.

On the surface the machines went on as they had been told, patient and indifferent, their lamps sweeping across the deepening cut. A fresh plume of grit rose from the bore and drifted away on a wind too thin to be heard. The points of light on the horizon had grown a little brighter.

Deep beneath the breach, where the drills had opened its shell to the cold, the thing in the rock gathered what strength the blows had left it and, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="f350041c-4bf5-4924-98dc-681e0e3f11a9">through the miles of stone and thin red air above it, turned its voice toward the ships that now hung in the far dark around Mars.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="07f22d0d-22b9-428c-bb27-2e1069bea9da" -->

The corvette hung in a slow orbit over the rust-coloured curve of Mars, its running lights dimmed to night settings. On the tactical display, a handful of pale markers sat far out beyond the planet's shadow, unmoving, each tagged with a question mark where a class and a name should have been. The laboratory ship drifted a few kilometres off the bow, every porthole lit. Down on the bridge the watch officer had gone aft for coffee, and the only sounds were the hum of the air scrubbers and the soft tick of the sensor sweep.

Varn waited until the bridge had gone quiet before she opened the line to Earth, and when her daughter's face filled the screen she did not smile the way she usually did. Telling the girl there was nothing to worry about would have been a shield, and a shield made of comfort would break the moment the news reached Earth, leaving her daughter with nothing in her hands. So she told her plainly that ships had come to Mars, ships no one had built, and that they had not answered a single word.

"Are they bad?" the girl asked, and pulled her knees up onto the chair.

"Nobody knows," Varn said. She watched the small face for fear and let it come, because she would rather her daughter be frightened now, with her mother's voice beside her, than later and alone. "I think they might go on to Earth." She kept her own voice steady so the girl could lean on it.

Then she leaned in close to the camera and began to teach, the way she had once taught her to cross a road or tie a knot that would hold. If the sirens sounded, she was not to wait to be told twice. She was to go where the alerts said, stay with the grown-ups she trusted, and keep her coat and shoes by the door. She was to remember that being afraid was allowed, and that it did not mean she couldn't think. "Say it back to me," Varn said, and the girl did, haltingly at first and then faster: sirens, go, grown-ups, coat and shoes. Varn made her say each thing, every one, and nodded at each, and did not let herself look at the dark beyond the viewport where the ships were waiting. Every minute she spent on the call was a minute she was not spending on herself, and she gave it gladly. Before the line closed she said she was proud of her, and that whatever happened up here, the girl already knew enough to take care of herself down there.

The screen went to the agency's blue standby pattern. Varn sat a while longer in the low light, her hand still resting on the console, while the sensor sweep ticked on and the pale markers beyond Mars held their places.

When at last she lifted her hand from the console, Varn turned back to the tactical display and the pale markers beyond Mars, and _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8a3009bc-03b8-420c-b153-83549cb831a3">traced the empty stretch of dark between them and the faint blue point of Earth, where her daughter would be asleep by now; then her hand went back to the comm, and she opened a channel home.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="8adf401f-32f7-4ddd-bce6-3ce82d0e2399" -->

The committee room sat at the end of a long corridor on the third floor of parliament, a windowless chamber with a horseshoe table of pale wood and a screen on the far wall that showed, without sound, the feed from the excavation on Mars: red dust, the slow arms of the machines, a timestamp that ran a few minutes behind. The members had been at the budget for most of the afternoon. Coffee had gone cold in its cups, and the clerk at the side table had filled a dozen pages with figures nobody would read twice.

The note reached the committee in the middle of that session on the excavation budget. It passed hand to hand along the table until it came to the chair, who unfolded it, read it once to herself, and then read it aloud. A lawyer in a custody hearing had named the politician. She had laid before the court photographs of him at the doors of the mining company, the same company whose bid for an exclusive licence to Mars he had brought to this room and argued for. The members looked at him, and then at one another, and the questions they had never asked out loud came back to them at once. Whose machines had he persuaded them to send down for the faster, deeper digging? The company's. Whose licence had gained its majority? The company's again. And where had he found the agency's findings, which none of them could get and which he had handed out across this table like favours? Each vote he had won now looked like a payment made in their name.

He sat in his chair with the committee's papers in front of him: the licence, the order to dig on, the order to protect the discovery. Every one of them carried his name, and every one had served the people who had paid for his career. A member at the far end set down his pen. "This committee oversees the space agency for the public," he said. "I would like to know whom the honourable member has been overseeing it for." Another, nearer the chair, asked how long the company had been paying for his seat. "The photographs prove nothing but a meeting," he said. No one at the table answered him, and the clerk wrote that down too, beside the company's name, which the chair asked to have entered into the record.

On the wall the machines went on turning the red ground over, slow and soundless. The chair folded the note along its creases and laid it flat on the wood. "We will take a recess of fifteen minutes," she said, and the members rose, gathered their papers, and filed out past him without a word. The door swung shut on the corridor. He stayed in his seat under the hum of the lights, alone with the cold cups and the screen, while the clerk finished her line and capped her pen.

When the fifteen minutes had passed and the members filed back in to take their places around the horseshoe table, the chair laid the folded note before her and, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="5b9efebb-935a-4d69-9f0d-1779894a06b6">without unfolding it again, lifted her eyes to the far end of the table, where the politician was still squaring his papers and had not yet noticed that every other member was watching him as well.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="c234aab6-dfeb-49c7-b9a5-7ff2b3f15b19" -->

The office sat on the second floor above a shuttered laundromat, and the warm, soapy smell of the machines still came up through the boards long after they had stopped for the night. Rain ran down the one window in crooked lines, and the streetlight beyond it striped the room through half-closed blinds. A desk lamp with a bent neck threw a yellow circle over the blotter. Everything else stood in shadow: the filing cabinet with its sticking drawer, the coat on its hook, a mug of coffee gone cold and filmed over hours ago.

The detective laid the contract on his desk beside the unpaid invoice and weighed one against the other. The fee for the photographs had cleared a month ago. The remainder, more than he had earned all spring, would come the day the court ruled for the father. He knew now what the money was for. It was for a man who had hardly seen his daughter in years, who told his friends the case was about making Varn pay, and who had used his pictures to dress a mother who came home alone every night as the companion of the mafia. He had taken those pictures himself, honestly, of nothing wrong, and they had been turned into a lie. No one in that courtroom would ever ask what the man who took them thought of it. No one would blame him for cashing the cheque and moving on to the next job.

He sat a long while with his hand flat on the paper. Then he picked up the phone and called his client.

"I'm ending the contract," he said. "Effective now. I'll return the retainer for the work not done."

The man laughed, as if it were a joke he had not been told the end of. When the detective did not laugh with him, he threatened: lawyers, his licence, the kind of people who owed him favours. When that met only silence, he named a larger sum, slowly, so it could be heard. The detective listened to the number and said no.

When the line went dead, he crossed out the client's name on the folder and wrote Varn's in its place. Then he went back through everything he had gathered: the teachers, the neighbours, the father's own words. This time he was sorting it for her.

Outside, a bus hissed past through the wet street, and its headlights swept across the ceiling and were gone. The rain kept on. The lamp burned in its small circle, and the pages turned under it one by one, set into new piles.

By morning the rain had thinned to a grey drizzle, and the detective, the folder tucked under his coat, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="e55329cf-10bf-4251-bdc3-fa0f41d7ec64">climbed the stairs to the politician's office and stood a moment outside the frosted glass of his door, rain dripping from his hat, before he knocked.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="0c80757b-3f3c-4dba-a4f8-7e7112774429" -->

The courtroom on the third floor had been built for larger cases than this one, and in the late afternoon its long benches stood mostly empty. Rain ran down the tall windows. The radiators ticked under them, and the lights overhead hummed. The court reporter's hands rested on her keys, and the lawyer for the father had finished speaking and squared her papers into a neat stack. On a stand beside the witness box, a wide screen stood switched off, its cable coiled on the floor.

A clerk carried a slip of paper to the bench, and the judge read it twice before she set it face down: ships that belonged to no nation on Earth had appeared around Mars, where the girl's mother was stationed. She looked at the dark screen from which the captain had answered, minutes late, at the last hearing, and then at the file that held the daughter's school reports and the name of the neighbour who collected her when no one else could. A child of that age, she thought, needed far more than a voice arriving from orbit. She needed someone to wake her in the morning and see that she ate, to sign the permission slips, to sit with her through a fever and notice when she came home quiet. She needed someone to answer her questions about the news tonight, and to still be there to answer them next year. Whatever love the captain had for her daughter, the judge could not see how love sent from that distance would get a girl raised.

Across the room the father sat at his lawyer's side in a pressed suit, a few streets from his daughter's school. To the judge he had begun to look like the safer answer, because he was simply there. "This court must ask," she said, choosing each word, "which parent will be present for this child tomorrow, and the day after." She did not ask how often he had been present before. She asked only whether he would come home, and the answer was plain. She made a note in the margin of the file and underlined it: *the child needs a parent who returns.*

The clerk went back to his desk by the door, and someone in the gallery coughed. The judge capped her pen and laid it across the file. "We will take a short recess," she said, and the reporter's keys clicked once more and fell still. Chairs scraped. The lawyer slid her stack of papers into a leather case and fastened the clasp, and outside the rain went on against the glass while the room emptied around the dark screen.

When the recess was over and the few spectators had found their seats again, the judge came back to the bench, opened the file to the page she had marked, and _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="546955cd-360f-42f8-b501-205d64cb7154">looked up over her glasses at the father and his lawyer at one table, and at the dark screen that stood in for Captain Varn at the other, while the room fell silent to hear what she had decided.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="0ce78b26-61b6-4770-a89a-e0ae8bb9f318" -->

The pit lay at the foot of a low ridge on the plain, a square wound in the rust-coloured ground, terraced on four sides where the robots had cut their ramps. The light came thin and late through the dust, and the shadows of the cranes fell long across the spoil heaps. Down at the bottom, where the rock turned from red to a dark, glassy grey, six excavators worked in a ring. They moved without hurry and without rest, their arms rising and falling, their bits grinding out a pale powder that the wind lifted in slow ribbons over the rim. There was no sound but what came through the ground, a steady shudder that ran down into the stone and did not stop.

The drills had not stopped, and the thing in the rock had only its own terms to read them in. Something that cut into a living shell, and cut again, meant to kill. It gathered itself and reached outward along the channel its kind had always shared, and the old signs came to it whole: the pulse that meant *one of us*, the pulse that meant *here*, the long falling pattern that meant *wounded*. It had no sign for machines that did not know what they touched, or for a curiosity that hurt by accident, because no one who spoke this tongue had ever needed one. So it told the fleet what it believed: *I am being opened. They do not stop. They came first with eyes, then with hands, and now with blades.* Each sign was exact, and each carried its mistake along with it, so that *dug* arrived as *attacked*, and *studied* as *hunted*.

Far out beyond the thin sky, the fleet read every sign as plainly as it had been sent, and asked nothing the signs did not invite. The answer came down in the same shared shapes: *we hear you, hold, we are near*. Receiving it, the organism felt its fear confirmed rather than eased, because now two minds held the same picture, and each took the other's agreement as proof. Between them, in a language both read without effort, an excavation had become a war. Nothing in that exchange had passed through the minds of the ones doing the digging, who had sent their own signals into the dark and had them read as nothing at all.

At the bottom of the pit the excavators kept to their ring. One lifted its arm, swung, and set its bit against the grey stone again, a hand's width from where the last cut had gone in. The powder rose, caught the wind, and drifted east over the ridge, and the shudder went on running down into the dark.

Far above the ridge, where the dust gave way to the black, the fleet drew in along its long curve and turned its _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="062d2ff2-c158-4457-9e8b-82138eede9e5">gaze, whatever served it for eyes, down past the two small ships hanging in orbit to the machines still gnawing at the rock below, and then slowly back up again.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="a58af8ca-c60b-4c1a-a1e9-3c62373d9fa9" -->

Varn sat alone in her cabin with the fleet's slow approach glowing on the small screen above her desk, and she did the arithmetic a mother does: the ships would not stop at Mars, and nothing stood between them and Earth but her corvette. If no one got in their way, the girl asleep in the house back home would have no chance at all. If someone did, she might have hours, days, time enough to be carried somewhere safe. Varn set her own life against those hours and found that it weighed nothing beside them. She did not hesitate long over it. She only straightened the photo of her daughter taped beside the screen, the one with the missing front tooth, as if the girl could see her tidy up before a visit.

Then she began to record, and she kept her voice steady for the girl's sake, the way she had kept it steady through fevers and nightmares. She said she loved her, and that none of what was coming was her fault. She told her to go where the grown-ups told her to go and not to stop for her things. She reminded her to eat breakfast even when she wasn't hungry, to keep her shoelaces double-knotted, to ask for help when something was too heavy, and to tell the truth even when it was hard, because that was how people would know they could trust her. She said she was brave, braver than she knew, and that she would grow up strong and kind without her. She stopped once and pressed her knuckles to her mouth until her voice was hers again. Then she finished: "I'm doing this so you get to grow up. That's the whole reason. Goodnight, sweetheart." She listened to the message once, the way she used to check that the door was locked before bed, and sent it home. Then she rose, buttoned her jacket, and went up to the bridge. The tracking plot ticked forward on the main display, and somewhere aft the deck hummed as the engines warmed. She had barely taken her seat when the comm chimed and his name came up on it. Over that distance every word took minutes to cross, so their talk came in turns, like letters read aloud.

Varn held the call open while her crew worked their stations around her without a word to her, and she spoke low into it, as if he were sitting at her shoulder. The alien ships were still closing, their strange hulls filling more of the bridge screens with every minute, and the scientists on the laboratory ship had long since stopped pretending they knew what was coming. His face came through the static thin and grey, his voice late, as it had been on every call since she left Earth, and she waited for it the way she had learned to wait, her palm flat on the console as though she could close the distance through it. For months his calls had carried questions he had been paid to ask: what the drills had touched, what the agency was keeping quiet, how deep the machines had gone. Each of them had been set inside a joke or a compliment so she would not notice. It had all begun in a restaurant, with him playing coy across a table where she had told him plainly that she was not looking for romance, and somewhere between those dinners and the long remote evenings since, the pretending had worn through without his noticing. This time he asked none of the questions. He did not talk about the committee or the orders. He asked whether she had slept, and she laughed and asked whether he had, and for a moment each of them was only listening for the other. Then she told him again, because someone had to say it aloud, that one weakly armed corvette could not stop a fleet. When his answer came, he did not reach for the easy reassurance or the line that would keep her talking. He had heard her out, and his hands, the hands that had signed for the faster digging, the deeper digging, the order to stand and protect, lay open and empty on the desk in front of him. Everything he had schemed for was still moving toward its end out there in the dark, and none of it could be called back.

Then the delay stretched. What he could still choose, he chose. In the silence while her words crossed to him and his answer crossed back, he understood that he did not want to lose her, that he had not wanted to for some time, and that he had only found it out now, when there was nothing left but this. When his face filled the screen again he was unshaven, the knot of his tie pulled loose, and he began speaking before he could have known whether she would still be there. He said he had seen the reports. He said he had told himself for weeks that he was only being kind to her, that the dinners and the calls were something else, something he did not want to name to her. Somewhere between her voice breaking and steadying again, he had understood that the warmth he had once put on for her had stopped being an act. So he told her plainly, without the coyness he had played at over all those dinners, that all of it had been a lie except the part where he couldn't stop wanting to hear her voice, as if the plain truth were the only thing he had left worth giving. "I love you," he said, roughly, and he looked startled by it, as if the words had been waiting behind his teeth the whole time. He knew she would hear it minutes from now, with the ships closer, and he did not take it back or dress it up the way he once would have. "I didn't plan to. I don't know when it happened. I only knew it when I felt how much I dreaded the silence after you. I don't want to lose you."

Varn closed her eyes, then laughed once, wet and helpless. She had told him at the start that she was not looking for this, and here it was anyway, arriving across all that dark at the worst possible hour. She recorded her answer quickly, before the minutes ran out, her voice steady and very quiet. She told him she had stopped pretending some time ago, somewhere between Earth and here; that his calls had become the hour she lived toward, and that she had not said so because she had thought there would be more time. Then she asked him the only thing she still had to ask: to keep going to the house, where the girl was alone now with the photographs on the walls, to sit with her daughter at the table, to be there in the mornings when she could not. When her words reached him, he thought of the day he had spent with the girl, the photographs of Varn on every wall, the stories the child had told him about her mother, and his reply came back slow and certain. He did not hesitate or bargain. He said yes before he had heard her out, the lag showed it, and then he said it again, slower, so it would reach her whole: "I'll look after her. Not for a day. For as long as she needs me, whatever happens up there. She'll never come home to an empty house. I promise you." Nothing in the promise would profit the men who had paid for his career. It was simply kept faith, offered at the moment when the fleet on her screens made every kindness seem too late. Varn touched the screen where his face had frozen on the last word, and believed him. Far behind that frozen image he sat with the channel open and waited for his promise to reach her, the two of them closer across that distance than they had ever been at a table. Even with the alien ships growing larger on the displays around his face, that trust held, and she kept her hand there a moment longer. For the length of the silence that followed, the bridge full of people around her did not feel like a place where she was alone. Then she took her hand from the screen, straightened in her seat, and turned to her helmsman to give the order that would put her ship in the fleet's path.

The helmsman looked back at her once, waiting for her to say it aloud, and Varn, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="557b09dd-bb68-47f2-9735-d514f01c7d81">her last message to her daughter already gone, looked past him at the screen, where the fleet's slow line ran in from the dark toward Mars and, beyond it, toward Earth, and she rose and crossed to his console.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="2aaf3182-0c73-4bc0-96b8-1624133f78bd" -->

The courtroom on the fourth floor was too large for the few people in it. Rain ran down its tall windows and the overhead lights hummed over rows of empty benches. At the respondent's table sat a court-appointed clerk with a folder he had not opened. Beside him a wall screen showed only the agency's grey seal and the words LINK PENDING. At the petitioner's table the lawyer had laid out her papers in a neat fan. The man beside her had worn a new suit for the occasion and kept touching its cuffs.

The judge read from the papers before her in the even voice of someone who had already decided. The child, she said, was the petitioner's daughter by birth. The petitioner lived on Earth, kept a steady address, and stood ready to take her into his home at once. The mother, by her own assignment, was beyond reach. "This court cannot leave a girl of that age waiting on the word of a woman who may not return," she said. "A child needs a parent within arm's length, and the law has always looked first to blood." On those grounds, she said, she would rule for the father, and the order would follow.

No one in the room asked who had walked the girl to school, or who had sat up with her through fevers. No one asked who had taught her to read and to answer for herself, or how many of her birthdays the man at the petitioner's table had missed. The file held his name on her birth certificate and a statement of his salary. It held nothing of the years she had spent growing up. He sat with his hands folded and nodded at each point as if they were his merits. The judge, satisfied that a father was present and willing, turned the page without asking what he had ever done to raise her.

The gavel came down once, softly, and its sound did not carry far in the high room. The lawyer squared her papers without looking at her client. The clerk at the respondent's table closed his unopened folder. On the wall the grey seal went on glowing over the empty chair, and LINK PENDING blinked beneath it while the rain kept running down the glass.

On the morning set for the ruling, the rain had not let up, and the grey seal on the wall still glowed over the empty chair when _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="1259a8c7-0369-4a37-9163-23d3ddcda356">the doors at the back of the courtroom swung open and the politician came in out of the wet, his coat dark with rain and his phone still clutched in his hand, walking past the father and his lawyer toward the front of the room as though he had something the court needed to hear before anyone could take that chair.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="521a7a53-0815-4bb4-900e-f22ddc809143" -->

The committee room sat at the end of a long corridor on the parliament's third floor, behind double doors of dark wood that the ushers closed at nine. Rain ran down the tall windows. The microphones along the oval table had been switched off, the public gallery stood empty, and the only sounds were the hum of the ventilation and the scratch of the clerk's pen.

The committee met behind closed doors, and the chair read the motion aloud without looking at him: that he be suspended from the committee while his ties to the mining company were investigated. Around the table sat the members he had courted with what Varn had told him, the ones who had come to him for the agency's findings and left with a reason to vote for the company's licence. They kept their eyes on their papers now. The clerk set out the record in order. First came the order for faster and deeper digging, carried on his argument, with machines the company supplied. Then the majority the company's bid had gathered after he began handing out what the agency kept quiet. Last came the order to protect the discovery and dig on, which he had pushed through against the members who wanted the excavation halted. Each decision had been the committee's to make for the public, and each, the motion said, would now be examined for whom it had served.

"Does the member wish to speak to the motion?" the chair asked.

He did not. The vote was quick. One by one the hands went up, and with them the seat he had sold went back to the room it had come from. "You will surrender your committee pass," the chair said, "and your access to the agency's reports. The investigators will go through your accounts, your calls, and every meeting you have held at the company's offices." He laid the pass on the table, and the clerk slid it into a folder with the record of his votes. The committee's business went on without him. The excavation, the licence and the orders sent to Mars were all taken up again by the members who had not been paid to decide them.

He stood, buttoned his jacket, and walked the length of the table to the doors. An usher opened them for him and closed them again behind him. In the corridor the lights were dim, and rain still struck the windows. His footsteps sounded on the marble, and no one came out after him.

Outside, the rain had not let up, and he stood beneath the parliament's portico with his phone in his hand, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="2be0883d-fcc7-4be9-8a73-526869905e9b">reading again the message from the company's owners, which said only that he was expected at their offices within the hour, before he turned up his collar and walked down the steps into the rain.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="31266009-1798-47c8-8add-0079b24cae16" -->

Mars turned slowly beneath the two Earth ships, its rust-coloured face half in shadow. Far out beyond them, where the planet's pull grew thin, the strangers held their positions in a loose arc that caught no sunlight and gave back nothing to the eye. The fleet watched from its far orbit, and it read what it saw the only way it knew how. Below, on the red surface, the machines went on cutting into the rock where the wounded one lay, and to the fleet that steady grinding was a wound still being opened. Above them hung two hulls of a shape it had never catalogued, and between those hulls and the surface ran a thin, patient traffic: small craft carrying pieces of the rock up into the larger ship, from which they never came back down. The humans would have called it sampling. The fleet had no sign for curiosity that cut, only for harm done on purpose, and so it filed the traffic as plunder carried away from a body that still lived. The narrow pulses the hulls kept sending outward reached it again and again, the same pattern repeated with what was meant as urgency. They broke on the fleet's receivers as structured noise, and the fleet took them for the chatter of a crew coordinating among itself. Nothing in them told it these were questions.

It measured the hulls' emissions, their slow drift into station above the dig site, and the bright sweep of their sensors turning toward the fleet. The scientists aboard meant only to look. The fleet registered each sweep as a targeting pass. It set the hulls beside the machines below and found that the materials matched, the timing matched, and the shared purpose seemed plain, and the wounded one's call had used one word for whoever had found it and struck. Lacking any sign that would have set apart those who dug from those who only watched and wondered, the fleet joined them under that single word. In its reckoning the ships in orbit stopped being strangers and became the same tribe that was tearing at its kin, and it began to plot their positions with that understanding.

On the bridge of the corvette the air smelled of warm circuitry and stale coffee. Captain Varn stood behind the helm with one hand on the back of the pilot's chair and looked at the arc of contacts on the main screen until their shapes stopped meaning anything. Taped to the edge of her console was a child's drawing, a lopsided ship with a woman in its window and a small figure waving from a green circle below, and her eyes went to it once and stayed there longer than she meant them to. "Bring us out of station," she said. "Put us between them and the inner system. Right across their line." The pilot turned in his seat. "Captain, that puts us in front of all of them." "I know where it puts us." Her voice did not rise. She thought of the girl asleep under a blanket of glow-in-the-dark stars, of the school bag left by the door, of a world that had no idea yet what hung beyond Mars. One small ship could not stop a fleet. It could only stand where the fleet would have to go first, and every minute it spent there was a minute the strangers did not spend turning toward Earth, toward that house, toward her. "Every minute we hold them is a minute for home," she said. "Take us in."

The engines rose to a low, steady thunder, and the deck shuddered under the burn as the corvette swung away from the laboratory ship and out into the dark, small and bright and alone. Varn kept her eyes on the blue point of light that was Earth the whole time, the way she had once kept them on a small girl crossing a busy street, measuring the distance between her child and whatever was coming. The fleet filled the forward screens, vast and silent. She did not ask the corvette to fight it. She asked it only to stand in the way, so that her daughter could go on sleeping in her bed a little longer, safe, not knowing. She had sat up through fevers and nightmares and the long nights after the divorce, and she thought of all of them now as one watch, which she was not leaving yet. When the corvette settled across the fleet's path, she did not take her seat but stayed on her feet behind the helm, as if standing made her more of a wall. Her thumb found the corner of the drawing where the tape had lifted, and she pressed it flat so it would not slip. She had spent ten years teaching her daughter to tie her shoes, to cook an egg, to say what she meant and to look after herself, and somewhere beneath her fear the thought had settled, almost calm, that she had taught her enough. What remained to give was this: her ship, her crew's courage, her own body set across the dark between her child and harm. She held her course and did not look away from the light of Earth.

Far out, the fleet watched the smaller hull break from its station and accelerate toward it. She had put herself there for a child the strangers did not know existed. It had no sign for a mother's shield, and it marked the movement as the tribe's first ship coming on to strike.

Along the fleet's dark arc the strange hulls shifted together, slowly at first, and _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="aef24e87-32b6-45e9-b952-5b5a3c140425">then all at once, their blunt prows swinging toward the two small Earth ships that still held their orbit above the dig.</li>
<li edge="503fbf1d-dbc3-483a-b1e8-8d59845b525b">then all at once, their unreadable prows swinging as one toward the lone corvette that Varn had set squarely in their path.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="abefb722-7d6c-444f-9a53-3d0027fa09a3" -->

Rain ran down the tall windows of the politician's office, and the city beyond them had gone grey and soft at the edges. The room was built to impress visitors: dark wood, framed photographs of handshakes, a flag folded in a glass case. The man behind the desk looked small in it. His jacket hung over the back of his chair, his tie was loose, and a screen at his elbow played the same muted footage of distant ships, over and over.

The detective set the folder on the politician's desk and did not sit down. He said he had been paid to bring a folder like this to another man, and he had torn up that contract, because what he found had turned out to be the opposite of what he was paid to find. He did not let go of the folder until he had squared its edges with the blotter. It was thick, and every page in it was labelled, dated and sourced in his small, even hand. He opened it to the first tab and walked the politician through it in order. First came the statements from the girl's teachers, each one taken in person and initialled, and beside each a note of who else had confirmed it. Then the neighbours, three on one side of the street and two on the other, asked separately so that none could echo another. Behind them were the school's attendance records, the pick-up logs, and the doctor's appointments Varn had never missed. Where one witness had said something he could not stand behind, he had crossed it out and written why. "I didn't take anyone's word twice," he said. "If it's in here, I checked it against something that isn't a person." The pages all said the same thing in different hands: Varn had raised her daughter alone, the child came to school fed and cared for and sure of herself, and the mother had never missed what mattered.

The politician turned a page and said nothing. The second tab was the father. The detective laid out the visitation dates the man had claimed beside the ones he had actually kept, with the gaps of months and then years marked in red. He showed the support payments, traced through two accounts before they stopped altogether. He had tracked down the father's friends one by one, and had written down their accounts of the man saying the custody case was about making Varn pay. Each account carried the date, the place and the exact words, and each was matched to a second friend who had heard the same thing on another night. "He never asked me once how his daughter was," the detective said. "Not once. He only asked whether I'd found anything that would hurt her." At the back he had clipped his own photographs from the restaurant, each annotated with the time, how long she had stayed, what she had drunk, and the hour she had gone home alone. He admitted they were his. He had taken them believing they were only work, and he had watched them turned into a lie about a woman who had done nothing wrong. "Those were used against her," he said, tapping the last page, "so I went back through every one. There's nothing in them but a woman talking about her daughter." That part was on him to put right. No one was paying him for this. If anything, it would cost him the clients who liked a man who did not ask questions. He knew whose doors the politician had been seen at, because he had followed him there himself. He had also watched him through a long day at Varn's house, patient with the girl, listening to her stories, and he had decided that this weighed more. "You're the one who cares what happens to her," he said. He closed the folder, squared it again, and slid it the last few inches across the desk. "So it should be in your hands, not his."

For a long moment the only sound was the rain. On the screen the ships hung in their silent loop. The politician put one hand flat on the folder, as if it might slide away, and looked up at the man who had once followed him through the streets.

Neither of them knew yet, as the rain went on falling, that the next time the folder was opened would be in a courtroom, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="389e2686-960d-4936-b850-3f643b4fb05b">on the morning the judge was due to decide whom Varn's daughter would go home with, and that the detective would carry it there himself, past the father's empty glance, while the politician sat behind him waiting for word from Mars.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="60a69c8a-3eb1-4426-9cf1-641c9adab042" -->

Mars turned slowly below, rust and shadow, the scar of the excavation site a pale smear near the terminator. Above it hung two small human ships, bright against the dark. Far out and closing came the fleet.

The hails were still going out when the fleet fired. From the escort and the laboratory ship the same patient signals swept outward on every band they had: counted pulses of prime numbers, a greeting recorded in a dozen human languages, landing lights flashed in slow, deliberate rhythm. To the human crews these were open hands. To the fleet they were nothing of the kind. It had no word for a greeting sent in radio, and no habit of counting aloud to strangers. In those pulses it read the same sharp, searching energy that had swept across the buried one, now turned outward against itself. The flashing lights were weapons warming. The scanners the scientists aimed, scrambling to learn anything at all, were targeting beams. Every sign the humans made to show they meant no harm, the fleet read as more proof of the hunger that had pierced its kin in the rock below.

On the corvette's bridge, Captain Varn held her ship where she had placed it: square in the fleet's path, its thin hull the only thing between those vessels and the faint blue point of Earth beyond. Her guns could not stop a fleet, and she knew it. She was not there to stop it. Every minute it spent on her corvette was a minute it did not spend on the way to a house where a girl slept, or woke, or sat in front of the news waiting to hear her mother's voice. A photograph was tucked into the frame of the console: her daughter at nine, gap-toothed, squinting into the sun. Varn did not look at it. She kept her eyes on the screens and her hands steady on the controls. "Hold position," she said, and her voice did not shake, because the girl would need whatever time this bought, and Varn meant her to have all of it.

So the fleet answered in the only terms it had, and the answer was fire. Light lanced across the dark from vessels whose powering-up no one aboard the Earth ships had known how to see. It came for the escort first, the ship whose lights had flashed the brightest welcome. The corvette still held its place across the fleet's course, and Varn did not order it aside. In the last moments before the fire reached her, she looked away from the weapons readout to the small clock in the corner of her console. She had set it to the hour in the house where her daughter would be asleep. She counted the time she had bought as though each second were something she was pressing into the girl's hands: a little more of the night, a little more warning, a little more chance to run. She thought of the night-light her daughter still pretended not to need, and of the last message already crossing toward Earth. It held everything she had tried to teach her, and the knowledge that she was loved. Then the first strike tore through the hull. The corvette broke apart in the place Varn had chosen for it, between the fleet and the planet where her child slept, the photograph and the woman who had kept it gone in the same white instant. What was left of her went on only as the minutes she had spent for her daughter.

Then the fire reached the laboratory ship, still broadcasting its numbers. The counted pulses broke off between one prime and the next. The recorded greeting played on from a drifting fragment of hull for a few seconds more, a voice saying *we come in peace* to a fleet that had never once heard it as anything but a threat, until that too went silent. In the wreckage that tumbled toward Mars there was no one left who could have explained that they had only been trying to talk.

Some of the last signals the two ships had sent were still travelling after the ships themselves were gone, and _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="d77fec95-e699-4225-99d7-80a20b962c1a">down on the red surface the excavators were still working around the thing in the rock, their drills turning, waiting on the next command from an orbit where no one was left to send one.</li>
<li edge="dafc956e-ebbe-4420-827a-0586753e8e49">one of them, addressed to a small house on Earth, was still crossing the dark towards the girl who sat by the screen there, waiting for her mother's daily call.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="fa7c3ae8-0594-49fd-94f1-7283e60e8a71" -->

The company kept its offices on the top three floors of a tower of dark glass, above the river and above the noise of the city. The lift opened onto a reception hall of polished stone, where a young man behind the desk looked up, recognised the politician, and did not smile. He was led down a long corridor lined with framed photographs of open-pit mines, the earth cut into terraces, yellow machines small as insects on the slopes. Rain ran down the windows at the far end. At the last door, the escort knocked once, opened it, and stepped aside without coming in.

The owners received him in the same room where they had once told him what to win for them, and this time no one offered him a chair. There were three of them at the long table. One had a folder open in front of him and read from it without looking up: the campaigns they had funded, the debts they had quietly settled, the seat on the committee they had carried him into. They spoke as men closing an account. "We never paid for you," the youngest said. "We paid for your vote. For what the agency told you and no one else. For your voice in the room where licences are granted." Since the morning the committee suspended him, he had none of these. A suspended member could not push a bid, could not order the digging deeper, could not bring them anything worth its price. What remained, the man with the folder said, was someone under investigation who knew their names.

They saved the worst for last, and said it flatly. He had questioned an order, and he had worried aloud about a captain on a corvette, as though her life had ever been part of the bargain. "An instrument that hesitates is worse than useless," the youngest said. "It is a risk." The eldest owner closed the folder and slid a single sheet across the table, a statement that he had never acted for the company and that no money had ever changed hands. He was to sign it and leave by the back stairs. "The committee has other members," the eldest said, "and other members have prices." The politician looked at the sheet that erased everything he had sold them. He understood that he had been a purchase, and that his usefulness had now run out.

A pen lay beside the sheet, heavy and silver, set there before he had arrived. No one in the room moved toward it or away from it. Behind the owners, through the rain-streaked glass, the river was the colour of slate, and somewhere below a siren rose and faded. A door in the corner of the room, one he had never noticed on his earlier visits, already stood open onto a bare concrete stairwell. The light inside it was flat and grey.

He had not yet reached for the pen when a soft tone sounded from the console at the head of the table, and the eldest owner turned his head toward the screen set into the wall, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="afe0500e-e187-43ea-a53a-edbc76998d46">where the feeds from the company's excavators on Mars had run all afternoon, a grid of small squares of red dust and scrolling telemetry, and where one square, in the top corner, had begun to blink.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="74f0ece7-cbde-4310-b83a-7b214c7926ba" -->

The excavation site lay in a shallow basin on the plain, where the ground fell away in terraces of rust-coloured rock toward a shaft cut deep into the bedrock. Spoil heaps ringed the rim, each tipped out in the same neat arc, and cables ran from them down the terraces to the machines working below. The sky above was the colour of weak tea, thin and cloudless, and the small sun hung low in it, throwing long shadows from every boulder. There was no sound but the drills, carried faintly through the thin air, and under them the steady tremor of rock being cut away.

The excavators stopped in the middle of a cut. Their drill heads spun down inside the shaft, the treads locked, and the dust they had raised settled back over the exposed rock. They had moved only on the command packets that came down from the laboratory ship, and when the relay window passed with nothing in it, they did the only thing their code allowed: they held position, kept their status lamps blinking, and sent their request for orders up into the sky. The request went out again and again, a string of handshakes and checksums that had only ever meant anything to the ships in orbit, and no ship was left to read it.

Below them, in the rock, the organism felt the grinding end. It had taken the drills for teeth, the scans for a hunter's gaze, and the cut through its shell for the first blow of an attack. Now it took the stillness in the same terms, as an attacker driven off. It did not know that the machines had never wanted anything, or that what had moved them was a voice from orbit, and that the voice had gone silent. The robots stood at the edge of the wound they had made, their lamps flashing a signal meant for no one who remained. The creature they had dug toward lay quiet beneath them, as sure as before that it had been hunted. Neither side had learned a single sign the other could read.

The sun slid down behind the rim of the basin, and the shadows ran together until they filled the shaft. The cold came quickly. Frost formed along the treads and the drill housings, and a light wind lifted fine grit from the spoil heaps and laid it in thin drifts against the cables. Overhead, the first stars came out hard and unblinking, and a small dark moon crept across them. On the floor of the pit, the lamps went on flashing, small and regular, the only lights for kilometres in any direction.

On Earth, in the command center, the screens that had followed the mission at Mars for weeks _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="848d2734-d949-4487-8221-e0f393a4f777">still showed the two small markers circling the red planet, the laboratory ship and Captain Varn's corvette, and the whole room had fallen silent to watch them.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="58eead3f-9920-46d6-a66f-3634d59006a7" -->

The company's offices took up the top three floors of a tower of dark glass, high enough that the city below showed only as a grid of lights in the evening rain. In the operations room, a wall of screens had been given over to Mars: a rust-coloured map of the excavation site, columns of figures scrolling past, small green markers that each stood for a machine at work beneath a sky no one in the room had ever seen. The air smelled of cold coffee and new carpet. Three of the owners sat at the long table with their jackets off, half listening as a technician read out the end-of-shift numbers in a flat, tired voice.

In the company's offices, the feed from Mars went dark in the middle of that shift report. The owners watched the telemetry from their excavators flatten to a single line. These were the machines the committee had ordered at the politician's urging, bought from the company with public money and sent to dig faster and deeper than the agency had ever asked. The technician stopped reading, tried the uplink again, and got nothing back. "There's no signal from the drills," he said. "Or the haulers. And nothing from orbit either." Every reading had passed through that orbit. For months those readings had reached this room before they reached anyone in parliament, carried down by a man whose career the company had paid for, so that it would know more than the people who were supposed to decide. Now the room knew nothing, and the man who had been their eyes was no longer theirs.

On the long table lay the draft of the exclusive licence, its majority counted vote by vote. Members who could not get the agency's findings had gone to him for them, and found them waiting at a price. One of the owners turned the pages slowly. The draft granted rights to mine a world on which the company no longer had a single working machine, and it had been won through an official who had already been suspended and then fired. Without the excavators there was nothing in the ground they could claim before anyone stopped them, and without him there was no one left on the committee to keep the vote theirs. Every payment, every meeting and every report passed from the committee room to this table had bought them an order to dig and a promise of ownership. They had neither now. The owner closed the folder. "It was worth exactly as much as the man who sold it to us," he said, and no one at the table disagreed. "And he stopped being worth anything."

The technician kept trying for a while longer, typing the same commands and waiting through the same silence, until one of the owners told him to go home. He switched the screens off one by one. The scrolling columns went first, then the camera feeds, until only the map of the site was left glowing on the wall. Its green markers sat where they had last reported and did not move. Rain ran down the windows. Below, the city went on with its evening, and the room stayed quiet but for the hum of the air vents and the soft click of the door as the technician let himself out.

Across the city, in a corridor of the courthouse, the politician sat on a hard bench outside the courtroom doors, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="a7a17ba3-6402-4138-9f24-61c23e789fc0">his phone dark in his hand, his eyes drawn to the muted screen above the doors, where the news ticker had just begun to carry word from Mars.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="27de58dc-0ade-49c6-a607-8e9a8ed64953" -->

The command center lay three floors beneath the agency's campus, a long room without windows where the air smelled of warm electronics and coffee left too long on the burner. The night watch was a dozen officers at curved desks facing the main board, a wall of screens that drew the inner planets as clean arcs and the ships around Mars as small labelled icons. Two clocks hung above it, one set to Earth and one to Mars, and for most of the shift the room had been quiet but for the murmur of headsets and the soft click of keys.

The signals from Mars came in eight minutes old, and then they stopped. On the big board, the icons for the laboratory ship and its escort corvette went from green to amber to the flat grey that meant no telemetry at all, and the officers on watch read the grey the only way their training gave them. The last packets held the power spikes the alien ships had shown a moment before: unreadable shapes closing on the mission, and hails that had gone out again and again and been met with silence. Someone replayed the silence as refusal. Someone replayed the spikes as weapons charging. "They powered up and fired," a lieutenant said, half to himself, and nobody corrected him. Nobody in the room could say what the fleet's emissions meant to the fleet, but each officer laid them over a picture he already knew, of an enemy that comes without warning and fires without speaking, and the picture fit.

By the time the director came through the doors, still buttoning her jacket, the room had already found its words. Unprovoked. Hostile. First strike. The officers typed them into reports and said them into secure lines, and with each telling the words grew harder, until they were no longer a reading of the data but the data itself. On the screens, the dig site sat where it had always sat, a dot of red rock where the machines had spent weeks cutting deeper into something no one understood. Nobody looked at it. Nobody asked what the strangers had seen when they looked down at Earth's drills biting into the ground, or what message might have crossed the dark before they came. "Give it to me plainly," the director said, and the watch officer handed her the summary. She read it, read the timeline, and said aloud what everyone in the room already believed: Earth had been attacked, and it was now at war.

For a moment no one moved. Then the room did what it had been built for. Lines lit across the desks, a captain began reading alert codes into his headset in a flat, steady voice, and the clock set to Mars time was pulled to the center of the board, as if it still had something to count. Above it, the two grey icons hung where the ships had been, and stayed there.

<!-- cell: markdown id="cd5e976b-8d02-41f4-a403-3b19b518ec94" -->

The house stood at the end of a quiet street, its porch light already on against the dusk. Inside, the kitchen clock ticked over the sink, a single plate sat drying in the rack, and the television in the living room had been left on mute, its colours sliding across the walls and the framed photographs above the couch. Rain had started somewhere in the afternoon and had not stopped; it ran down the front window in thin, uneven lines. Just after seven, the screen chimed once, softly, and the muted programme gave way to a waiting light.

The girl played the message alone in the living room, and her mother's face filled the screen with the bridge lights low behind her. Varn did not talk about the ships. She looked straight into the lens as if she could see her daughter on the couch, and she began with the small things, the things she had always checked before bed: that the girl had eaten something warm, that her school bag was packed for the morning, that she would not stay up waiting for a call that could not come tonight. She reminded her where the spare key was kept, which neighbour to knock on if the power went out, and how to set the alarm on the back door the way they had practised together. Her voice stayed steady through all of it, the way it had on the first night of a thunderstorm years ago, so that the girl would hear calm and not fear.

Then Varn leaned closer, and her words slowed. She told her daughter that she was brave, that she already knew how to look after herself better than she thought, and that she should keep asking questions about everything, the way she had asked about the thing under Mars. She told her that being sad was allowed, that it would not last forever, and that she must let the people who loved her take care of her now. She said that what she was about to do, she was doing so that her girl would have time: time to grow up, to finish school, to become whoever she wanted. She said it as simply as a fact, the way she would have explained why she had to take the last piece of cake to the old man next door. At the end she smiled, touched two fingers to the screen where her daughter's cheek would be, and said she loved her more than anything, more than her own life, and that this would never change. The screen went dark, and the girl sat with her hand pressed to the glass, still warm from the light of her mother's face.

The house went on around her as if nothing had happened. The clock in the kitchen kept its count, the rain kept running down the window, and the porch light burned on over the empty step. On the wall above the couch, in a photograph taken on some bright summer day, a woman in a captain's jacket laughed at something outside the frame, and the dark screen below held only the faint reflection of the room.

<!-- cell: markdown id="77cfa1c5-2587-429b-ba72-3539d105b44a" -->

The courtroom was full for the ruling. Morning light came grey through the high windows and lay across the benches, the clerk's desk, and the empty chair where the judge would sit. The girl's father sat at the front beside his lawyer, a folder squared on the table in front of them. At the back, a man in a creased coat held a thick envelope on his knees.

The news reached the court through a screen above the clerk's desk, and the politician read it standing in the aisle. Every Earth ship at Mars was gone, the laboratory ship and its escort, Varn's corvette among them, and the government was calling it an act of war. The words came to him in the language of the committee rooms he had worked for years: orders, mandates, the protection of a national discovery. He knew whose orders those were. He had carried the company's bid into parliament, traded the agency's findings for votes, and talked the members out of halting the dig. He had done it so that the machines the company supplied would keep cutting into whatever lay under Mars until it belonged to the men who had paid for his career. Around him the gallery was saying the same word, war, as a thing that had been done to all of them. He had told himself the committee's decisions were only ever bought cheaply, a favour here, a majority there, and that the price fell on no one in particular. Now the price had a name on the casualty list, and a nation behind it. Somewhere in the company's offices the owners would be counting a lost licence. He was counting what he had sold them, and what it had cost everyone else.

The judge took her seat, and the room went quiet. A clerk came in with a printed notice and set it before her, and she read it aloud: Captain Varn's ship had been lost at Mars. Across the aisle the girl's father did not move. His claim rested where it had always rested: the child was his by blood, and a mother who did not come home could raise no one. The judge looked at him and drew breath to rule. The politician stepped forward from the back of the room, where he had no standing in the case, and asked to be heard. "Your Honour," he said, and his voice was steadier than his hands. "Captain Varn spoke to me on her last call, when she already knew what that fleet could do. She didn't ask me to find the girl's father, or her own family, or anyone the law would have named. She asked me to look after her daughter. She chose by what she had seen of me, and I'm here to answer that choice, not to argue for a right. I have no claim of blood, and I won't pretend to one. But I know that the girl needs her lunch packed before eight. I know she reads aloud to the photographs of her mother on the shelf, and that she saves her questions about Mars for the evening call. Someone has to be there in the evening to hear them now. I'm not asking for a child I made. I'm asking for one I promised to raise until she can stand on her own, the way her mother was raising her. That promise is all I bring to this court."

The lawyer half rose to object. The news of the lost ship still hung over the benches when the man in the creased coat rose from the back of the courtroom and came up the aisle. The father turned in his seat and saw him, the detective he had paid to find dirt on Varn, and for a moment he seemed to expect more of it. The detective did not look at him. He carried the envelope to the bench himself and would not let the clerk take it. He knew no one would pay him for what was in it, and that it would cost him every client who heard he had turned on the man who hired him. Every page inside had been checked twice before he let it count. The visitation records from the family office were copied whole, not summarized, so the court could count the empty months itself: years in which the father had come for the girl on four afternoons, two of them cut short. The teachers' statements were signed and dated, and beside each one he had pinned the school register showing who had come to the parents' evenings, term after term. It had always been the mother. The neighbours had told him that Varn raised the girl alone, and raised her well. He had not taken their word for it, but matched it against the pharmacy slips, the dentist's appointments and the after-school rota, until every claim had a paper behind it. Three of the father's friends had heard him say the case was only ever about making Varn pay. The detective had kept only the remarks two of them remembered the same way, and dropped the third account because its date could not be fixed. The lawyer was still on her feet, but he had answered her before she could speak. Under each of his own photographs, the ones she had used to paint Varn as the companion of a man tied to the mafia, he had laid what the lens had missed. There were the restaurant receipts showing that she drank little, the times she left alone, and the taxi logs that took her home and nowhere else. "I took this man's money to bring Captain Varn down," the detective said plainly. "The photographs I sold him have been made into something they never were." He stood beside the politician and spoke for him without being asked. "I watched him spend a day caring for her daughter, and I watched the girl warm to him. She needs someone who will be there for her. Blood didn't keep her father there. He wanted her only to wound her mother, and her mother is gone. Her mother chose who would be there, while she still lived to choose." The father's face went dark. The detective held his ground before the bench, his hands empty, asking nothing for himself, until he had said all of it.

The judge turned the pages slowly and found no gap to stop at. Each date led to a record, and each record led to a name that could be called. The lawyer sat down with nothing to strike. The detective stood with his hands folded and waited while the court read the whole file through to the last tab. It held a dated note in Varn's own hand, naming the man she had trusted with her child. Above the clerk's desk the screen went on listing the names of the lost, and the room waited.

The judge closed the file at last and looked up across the silent room, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="93f1d7a7-bd76-466a-9d1b-c096ed1f94e3">past the father and his lawyer to where the politician sat beside the detective, whose papers still lay open on the bench before her, and drew breath to give her ruling.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="ca8b8812-6c5e-4e9d-90e8-abab69b04001" -->

The courtroom was cold that morning, and the tall windows let in a grey light that lay flat across the benches. Few people had come. The father sat at the front with his lawyer, his coat still buttoned. Across the aisle the politician sat alone, his hands folded on his knees. The detective sat two rows behind him. A clerk read the case number in a voice that carried to the back wall, and the judge opened the folder that had been placed before her.

The detective's evidence lay before the judge. It said nothing about the man's claim of blood to the girl, only about what he had done with it. He had hardly seen his daughter in years. He had told his friends the case was about making Varn pay. In all the months of the suit he had never once asked how she was sleeping, what she ate, or who walked her to school. Beside his file lay the statements of the teachers and the neighbours: Varn had raised the girl alone, and raised her well. While her mother was gone, it was the politician who had come to the house, fed her, and sat with her over the photographs while she told him stories about her mother.

The judge set the two side by side and read them through without a word. Then she looked up at the father, whose name stood on the birth certificate.

"A child," she said, "is not a debt to be collected from her mother. Nor is she a blow to strike her with." She closed the folder. "A parent is the one who does the raising. This court gives the girl to the man who has already begun it, the man her mother trusted to care for her."

The politician bowed his head. The father said nothing at all.

The lawyer gathered her papers slowly. She had argued for a parent who would come home, and she had believed she was putting the child first. Now she saw what her client had wanted from his daughter: a weapon, never a daughter to raise. She snapped the clasp of her case shut and did not look at him as she rose. Outside, rain had begun against the high windows, and the clerk was already calling the next case.

